      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 28, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Memorial Service at Frogmore.—The King and Queen have been spending the end of the month it Windsor, where their Majesties were joined by many members of the Royal Family for the purpose of attend,. ing the special memorial service to the late Queen Victoria. This is held at the Royal Mausoleum, Frog- more, and the musical portion of tha service on these occasions is especially beautiful. Four years have passed since the death of our late Queen, but there remains undiminished feeling and emotion in this annual tribute of respect and affection for the departed Sovereign. To Princess Henry of Battenberg it must be particularly touching, revelling as it does her devotion to her mother, and following two days after the anniversary of the death of her husband, the late Prince Henry. The Risk of being Buried Alive.—The incident at Accrington, where a woman whose death had been certi- fied was discovered by the undertaVer to be living, has been followed by an authenticated case of a man being actually buried alive In France. The marl was subject to fits, and after a seizure, wag sueposed to be dead. The funeral took place, but the gravedigger thought afterwards that he hearo groans, and on the coffin being opened it presented unmistakable proof that its occu- pant had died in horrible agony after the burial. Such occurrences will almost alter the attitude of the public towards the question of premature burial. It is evident that the dread of such a fate has been constantly present to the minds of many individuals, such for eXaruple as Mrs. Herbert Spencer, Lady Burton, Miss Harriet 'Martineau, and Mr. Edmund Yates. Not very long ago a gentleman left his fortune to his relatives with the stipulation that unless his body was severed in places, the money was to go to a chanty. So far, however, the dread of premature burial has not been a general one, and people who agitated on the subject were regarded as aruiable enthusiasts with one idea. But it is evident from the cese at Accrington that people in a cataleptic condition may be certified to be dead, and in France there is perhaps the greater risk of being buried alive, ; because in that ceuntry it is seldom that more than two days elep3e between death and burial. The 1 rince's Visit to lreamd.—Lord and Lady Dudley have been much occupied with preparations for enter- taining the Prince of Wales at Dublin Castle where a ball and other functions are to take place after the Prince's return from his his visit to Lord Ardelauu at Ashford in Connemara. The Prince leis been several times in Ireland—on one or two occasions to see his brother, the late Duke of Clarence, who sets stationed there with his regiment— but this is the first shooting ' expedition for which the Prince has gone to Ireland. The Ashford estate affords some of the best sport to be had.in the country, and the house is both modern in structure and also splendidly decorated and furnished. It is beautifully situated ; in the midst of wild and . picturesque scenery, and the grounds extend for two ; miles along the shores of Lough Corrib. Lord Ardilaun, , who is the head of the Guinness family, hasltwo other he would move a resolution dealing with the applies- ! seats in Ireland: St. Ann's, on the shores of Dublin Bay tion. . and Muckross House, situated amidst the unsurpassed Mr. BILLTNGE asked whether it was the intention of scenery of the Lakes of Killarney. The 'Londe.] Sea on.—ln London the prospects for the the committee to appoint additional relieving officers, ' as suggested by the master in his recent paper on the forthcoming season are disturbed by the expectation of unemployed question. an early dissolution of Parliament. Should such an The CHAIRMAN : The paper has not been fully dis- event take piece, it will keep many people away, and This Board met on Wednesday, Mr. A. Brooks pre- cussed yet. have a detrimental effect in diminishing the number and siding. There were .also present :—Mrs. Clay Mrs. The