      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>MDS..ASTOR'S DANCR.</p>
        <p>_ The balls given by Mrs. John Jacob Astor at her hluse in Fifth-avenue are always among the most notable events of the New York season. The great feature of Mrs. Astor's second dance' which took place recently, was the cotillon led by Mr. Albert Stupford. It was marked. writes a correspondent, by that charming novelty and .graceful originality which constitute the dist:nction of cotillons arranged by Mr. Stopford. The most beautiful figure consisted of four huge trees with red, white, pink, and yellow roses. These were brought into the ballroom, and entwined in the foliage of each tree were hundreds .of electric lights. As the figure was danced the other lights were turned down, making a charming effect. During the dance the roses were distributed as favours.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S ADMITTED RIGHTS. No sane man in these enlightened days denies a woman her right to "gang her am n gait"; strong- mindedness in a woman is no longer the unpardon- able sin; and if she prefers to make speeches in public to making pancakes in private, no one thinks the less of her on that account. Whether a woman's forte ia perorations or puddings, she is quite right, declares Chic, to cultivate it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>EVENTNO COAT.</head>
        <p>EVENTNO COAT.</p>
        <p>Black broche, cream Paris lace, and cerise velvet combined form this smart evening coat. The full coat is gathered on to a small yoke on the shoulder, and is drawn in beneath a wide belt, finishing in a short, full basque. The stole ends are em- broidered in steel beads, the fronts of the basque being ornamented in the same way. The /wing sleeves open over full puff ones of cream Paris lace, gathered in at the elbow, and finished with a full frill of lace. The lace insertion, of which the</p>
        <p>I yoks is formed, reaches well on to the sleeve, 16 pretty effect being given by the little balls of cerise velvet hanging from the end. The neck is finished by aV of lace insertion. The full blouse or front is of lace like the sleeves, the fronts of the coat being softened by cascade frills of cream chiffon edged with narrow cerise velvet. This coat could also be made separately, without the lace sleeves and front, and worn over any evening gown or blouse. They are much worn composed of velvet, in black or colours. TIME'S REVENGES.</p>
        <p>A curious little incident, showing the turns of Fortune's wheel, occurred the other day in the Rue de Ricoli, in Paris. A tall lady, quietly dressed in black, was about to cross the thoroughfare when she was imperiously waked back by a sergeant-de- who stopped the traffic, then at its height, to let a carriage containing a single occupant pass down towards the Champs Elysees. The lady in the carriage was Madame Pelletan, wife of the hirsute Minister of Marine: the woman of no importance standing on the kerb was the ex-Queen of Naples.</p>
