      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>BEST OF ALL COSMETICS.</head>
        <p>THE BEST OF ALL COSMETICS.</p>
        <p>It seems strange, remarks a contemporary, that women should have to go to the beauty doctors and pay expensive fees in order to learn the lesson that their grandmothers knew by heart, namely, that Nature has provided them with the best unguent there is for the face in the form of rain water. This is a tonic, a softener, and a beautifier for the skin of the very highest reputation. Golden sove- reigns may be lavished upon unguents from the East while at our very doors, or, rather, in our water butts, free of all cost, Nature's own restorer may be procured.</p>
        <p>INDOOR DRESS.</p>
        <p>Our sketch shews an indoor dress realised in a soft material, a mixture of silk and wool, in a pale shade of blue. The bodice has a deep yoke of cream lace, the lower part being laid in graduated tucks, and draped like a crossover in front, the back being rounded off at the edge of the yoke. The crossover is bordered with a ruching of the material applied fairly flatly, so that no ungainly</p>
        <p>iulk is added to the figure. The short, full, puff sleeves end iu a deep frill of the material, which is tucked like the bodice. The full skirt is quite plain save for the band of tuckings that runs round the hem. This group of tucks might easily be repeated higher up the skirt, if the wearer is sufficiently tall to carry oil the extra trimming advantageously.</p>
        <p>LLDY STRADEROKII.</p>
        <p>The Countess of Stradbroke, who is in such ill health that she has been ordered to take a voyage to South Africa, is one of the loveliest of our younger peeresses. She is the only sister of Sir Keith Alexander Fraser, a daughter of the late General Keith Fraser, and a granddaughter of the famous Madame de Falbe, who was so old a friend of Queen Alexandra. She was married to the Earl in 1898, and the Queen and Princess Alexandra were among the wedding guests. The Queen, too, was godmother to their little son, who was born in the spring of 1903. Ifeuham Hall, their charming place in Suffolk, was bought by Lord Stradbroke's atcestor, Sir Anthony nous, in 1544. TO-DAY'S ReclpE.</p>
        <p>POTAGE A LA CREME DE MARRONE. —Blanch abont fifty chestnuts, remove the outer husk and the inner akin, boil them ten minutes, and then put them into a stewpan with a sliced onion, a sliced stick of celery, a little salt, and sufficient stock to cover all; put a buttered paper over them, cover them closely, and stew them slowly for an hour; then rub them through the tammy. Return the purée to the stewpan with half a pint of milk, and sufficient stock to make it a nice consistency, and whtm it is hot stir in the yolk of an egg mixed with a teacupful of cream, and serve it at once.</p>
