      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>MICROBE MANIAC, i!</head>
        <p>MICROBE MANIAC, i!</p>
        <p>FRED WHISHAW (Author of " A Forbidden Name," 4.c.)</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Proposed Extension.</p>
        <p>' 71,!.',4'.:.',,....".•4.,.;.:',.:;;',...;.,..,..4y .. .... ..... •. ...</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>friend. I took it fur granted that Le hair sear- lowed the diptheria tat,ilitis and treated him strictly in necordance with the im.trm:tions I had received. Whoa I felt doubtful I sent for you— I don't see what I could have done more than I have."</p>
        <p>Both Royce and the student did all that was, possible to save the old Mall, but their efforts - were in vain, and he died within an hour, a vic- tim .to his own microbes.</p>
        <p>As soon as the breath was out of the old man's body Royce appropriated the casket in which were collected the fateful phials. " I think I'll take these medicines with rne, and destroy them," said Royce, addressing the faithful valet ; " you needn't say anything about it if enquiries are made ; they are dangerous. drugs and should not be left about !"</p>
        <p>I Royce carried Hie casket it;le-Sr. and trembling:. I What if he should slip and drop it ? Thpre were eleven phials, each filled with sutliciedt deadly organisms to infect and destroy the entire popu- lation of London ; the thought made his head swim, and he stood a moment to pull himself to- gether. Then he hailed a cab and drove out tr Barnes Common, where he despatched it. Select- ing a quiet spot, far from the frequented patli. that led across the Common, he collected a pile of dry grass and sticks, sufficient to roast a sheep withal. Over this he emptied first one phial, then several, and watched the flames spit and crackle as they ruthlessly destroyed the deadly fruits of msny years of patient research and investigation. He allowed the fire to burn itself out : then he col- lected the ashes and mixed them with a new sup- ply of dry fuel and rekindled this ; he dug up and burned the earth around the fire, and when all was scorched and charred he kindled a third fire : then he went away. A fortnight later a tramp passed the spot ; his pipe would not draw and he took a small charred twig to clean it out: Not long afterwards an imitate of the Casual Ward at Brixton was taken ill ; it was found to be a case of rapid tubercu- losis. The tramp died, but fortunately—so far as can be known—without spreading the mischief. A child, playing at hide-and-seek on Barnes Common with his companions, several months, after this, dropped a sweet as he ran : it fell upon the remains of Royce's bonfire. Unwilling to lose the dainty, he picked it up and popped it into his month. 'Within a fortnight the poor lad lay tossing in a river-side fever hospital, while the vestry, authorities in his district were deeply concerned to discover the origin of the attack. The milk and the drinking water of the district were suspected and scrutinised, but they were in- nocent and the mystery remained unsolved. As for Royce. he is rapidly making his for- tune. The son of the Rajah of his district had the ill-luck to succumb to the .cholera epidemic rag- ing that season in the province. Royce, whose name was already upon the lips of the people as a doctor of marvellous skill—was st,t,' for, and within a couple of days the yowl-, prince was well. The Rajah, who was devoted to the lad, (lid not consider half a lac of rupees too large a fee for this service. He has further appointed Royce regular medical attendant to his court at a' salary of the equivalent of £1,500 a year us a retaining fee, illnesses to be extra. Sir Bruce's last pupil proved utterly unworthy of the trust confided in him in the shape of a phial-fat of the diptheria baccillus. He was fool enough to experiment upon himself, believing. that he would be able to apply the remedies as easily in hie own person as in any other. But in the languor . and feebleness of the horrible illness that followed he had neither the strength nor the energy to attend to such matters. When he died the phial, containing more potential death than the grim armaments of a battle ship, was put away with "other medicine bottles." The popu- lation of the town and district in which he lived, walk with naked lights over a powder magazine, suspecting nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Speech by Professor Dale.</p>
        <p>On Monday, Vice-Chancellor Dale, of Liverpool University, distributed the priaes and certificates to the pupils and students of the secondary and evening schools of Runcorn Technical Institute.</p>
        <p>The Vice-Chancellor in the course of an address upon the question of education, said we all looked forward to the day when we should have done with the conflicta and controversies that now entered into the subject, and were able to give our whole strength and our whole conviction to the improvement of the education of the people. (Applause). He contrasted the American and English systems and said a member of the Mosely Cum- mission summed up the whole case in a single sentence: If American education to-day is alert, vigorous, pro- gressive, and popular, it is because the people wish to have it so." We could not say the same of the English people. We needed a new spirit and a new faith about education, and all that education included, and until we had a real belief in education and a real love for education, the schools of the country would never be what they might be, and never do what they might for the country. He urged parents to imitate Scotch parents in the sacrifices the latter made for the educa- tion of their children, and not to think their duty was done when the education rate was paid. (Applause).</p>
        <p>"FAILURES IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—Accwding to Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial failures in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, January 20th, was 151, viz.:—New bankruptcy proceedings published in the London Gazette, 88 ; deeds of arrangement registered, 76: The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: bankruptcies, 91 ; deeds of arrangement, 83—total 174 being a decrease of 20. The total num- ber of commercial failures recorded during the 3 weeks of the present year is 487 ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 3 Weeks of last year was 464 showing an incrense of 23.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>RAILWAY WORKERS' SECRETARY Testifies to Bile Beans. CURED OF RACKING HEADACHES AND INDIGESTION.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY WORKERS' SECRETARY Testifies to Bile Beans. CURED OF RACKING HEADACHES AND INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>Everybody knows that the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants is a most important trade organisation. It has a membership of over 60,000, and a balance of funds of over £280,000. Mr. G. Burgneay, of 137, Sandhurst-road.Catford, the secretary ol the Rotherhithe Branch of this important society, and a member of the Executive Council, has recently proved the great value of Bile Beans in cases of acute headache, nausea, etc., and recently gave to a "Weekly Budget" representative his views on the same. Said he:.—</p>
        <p>"For eight years, up till about two years ago, I scarcely ever knew what it was to bo free from head- ache. The pain was mostly in the back of my head, and when at its worst I seemed absolutely unfit for any- thing. A feeling of drowsiness and depression over- came me, and I turned quite sick. "Indigestion also appeared. Time after time I have heenunable to eat because of the nausea and of the pain which food caused. Many a day I have brought back untouched the food I took with me to work, and for- three or four days at a stretch have been unable to take any nourishment. I not only fel,t bad but looked it. The inability to eat and digest food made me so ill and to weak that I was obliged to be continually off work.</p>
        <p>"Many a time, though I dragged myself to day, scarcely know how I got through my day's work. I also began to suffer from piles, and the pain and weak- ness cans( 1 by this ailment added to the debility from the other gsources mude my existence most wretched. The doctor I consulted could give me no satisfaction, and I was really at the point of despair. One day I was in the lobby at the railway siding when one of the men began to talk about Bile Beans, and to tell his work-mates now much good they had done him. I was feeling very ill at the time, so rtook his advice and got a box.</p>
        <p>" I never spent money; to better purpose. By the time I had taken a few boxes of Bile Beaus I was feel- ing a great deal better. The headaches stopped, my appetite returned, the piles were cured,aml new life and' vigour seemed to have been infused. I continued with Bile Beans until I was again restored to complete, heal h." "That is over eighteen months ago,and since that time I have never had a single days illness. It is clear., therefore, that the cures which Bile Beans work are permanent. To-day I feel as well and strong as any men could wish, and no amount of work in any sort of weather upsets me. "My wife, who has sufferel from indigestion, hns also found Bile Beans very beneficial, while more than one of my fellow-Workers ,to whom T bave recommended the Beans, have also proved their merits." Every worker or worker's wife who reads this case will doubtless be linpressed with Mr. Bargneay's state- ment. Bile Beaus win golden opinions wherever they are used, because they cure, not merely relieve. They cure all liver ailments, biliousness, indigestion, co n- stipation, piles, female ailments, wind, rheumatism, and tone up the system to withstand the effects of cold a chill. Of all chemists at Is. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box.</p>
      </div>
