      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Warrington v. Bradford.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Bradford.</p>
        <p>The return match between these teams was played on Saturday at Bradford before about 8,000 people. Much interest was taken in the encounter as Warrington had already beaten the Bradford men once, and a repetition of such would endanger the home sides chance for the championship. Bradford had their full strength, whilst Warrington made one alteration from the side which had defeated Hull the previous week, Naylor taking the place of S'augars, who had Ix 3R called home owing to illness in his family. The teams and officials were as follows : Warrington: Hallam, back; Filth, Isherwood, Brooks, and Kenyon, three-quarter backs; Davies and Hocken- hall, half-backs; Boardman, Thomas, Jolley, Preston, Cook, Belton, Naylor, and Swift, forwards. Bradford: Gunn, back; Dechan' Molby, Eagers and Connell, three-greeter backs; Marsden. and Brear, half- backs; Leidlaw, Francis, Barker, Creenwood, Turner, Smales, Westmoreland and Thresh, forward. Referee, Mr. J. H. Smith (Widnes) •' touch judges, Messrs. G. Holdsworth (Halifax), and T. G. Kilner (Huddersfield). Preston started the game and Bradford were the most to defend, but from a pack Brear essayed to clear only to '3O redulsed k Fish, who, in charging the kick, was slightly darnaeel'and the gains was delayed a few minutes. He was able to resume and soon came under notice in connection with Isherwood and alockenhall in staying a desperate rush of the Tykes headed by Laidlaw and Barber. A series of quick transfers between Davies Hockenhall, Brooks, and Kenyon placed Warrington on the a egressive again, and twice Gunn was called upon. Speedy clearances were effected and then came a fine round of passing by the Bradford backs, Eagers making a desperate effort to break through. The tackling of Warrington, Hallam, Hockenhall and Isherwood in particular being very fine, saved the situation, and eventually Brooks accepted a pass from Hockeehall,and • Bradford were compelled to fall beck. An exchange of kicks ended in Isherwood finding touch well, and then some faulty work by the Warrington backs, Eagers nipped in and affairs looked bad for the " Wirepullers." Deohan was in attendance and the speedy Bradford wing man being given possession at half-way, he had a clear coarse. Fish, however, utilized his great tarn of speed and overtaking the Sootchman, polled him up a foot from the line after a brilliant race. From a long kick by Hockenhall, Connell was floored by Kenyon and another effort by the Warrin.gton becks gave Gunn an anxious time of it. Alersden cleared, but Bradford were again forced belle by Eloekenhell, and had the oval ceen more accurately handled, a try must ha.e resulted. Following this came an attempt at cross kicking by ‘Brear, who tried to make era opening for Connell. 'Davies and Hockenhall were alert and the former, securing the ball, handed ove,- his charge to hie partner. Meanwhile Keayon had joined issue in the movement, and receiving the bill when the Bradford custodian had been reached, the light haired player soon showed his opponents a clear pair of heels, and scored near the posts. Davies brought the ball oust much to t'ae astonish- ment of the Warrington supporters, and took the shot at goal. His effort struck the posts and rebounded into the field of clay. Vigorous play marked the restart and the Bradford backs ran strongly, only to be repulsed by Kenyon and Davies. The interval arrived with War- rington leading bye try (three points) to nil. Turning round the pace- did not slacken in the least and Brad- ford by strong forward work were soon aggressive. Twice Niarsden tried to break through without success but at the third attempt the Bradford skipp -accepted a pass from Brear, and scored near the posts, a goal following from the scorar's foot. Boardman started a movement, which was deserving of substantial reward. Gunn was lucky in savine. A epell of give-and-take play ensued, and though Marsden twice get under weigh he was not allowed any liberties to travel far. The Bradford backs persistently kept the ball away from the left wing, knowing full well what a dangerous quantity Fish is. but Kenyon and Brooks met the invasions seleadidly and on one occ usioe Kenyon got well away with a dribble. Nearing Gene he essays I to cross to the opposite wing. only to see the ball fail in its object, for it did not travel many yards and Mosby stepped in and saved. Final: Bradford 5 points, Warrington 3 points. / _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>LEEDS V. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>LEEDS V. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>There would be about 8,000 people present at this match at Leeds. The game for some time ran prin- cipally in favour of the home men, and it was not long ere they had placed a try to their credit,Nsylor getting over after some smart passing by Bravahaw,Thomas,and Naylor. This reverse undoubtedly did the Reds a power ,of good, for they pulled themselves together and paid a visit to the Leeds half. Here Lorr^.9 got hold and put in a fine ran. Harding off with terrible force, he crowned a splendid effort b landing a goal. Thus at half-time Salford were leafing, by five points to three. Leeds could claim to have had the best of nutters though, and in the second half they further increased their lead, Stead getting a try and l3rayshaw and Ward dropning goals. In the last few minutes Lewis scored "for Raiford, and Lomas kicked a goal which made the game a draw.but Leeds, thoroughly roused, rushed right atvay in the last minutes, and again scoring placed the issue beyond doubt. Final : Leeds 15 points; Salford 10 points. BROUGHTON RANGERS v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>- - -- This match was played at St. Helens before 4,000 spectators. In the first hnlf St. Helens had the best of the game, their forwards being better than the Rangers but they were unable ta score, and half-time arrived with the score sheet blank. In the second half Harry scored for Bronghton, and Hurls kicked the goal. Harris also added another try to the Rangers' score. Traynor then dropped a goal for St. Helens. Creeyey was tacklsd when racing for the Rangers' line. ani shortly after Traynor kicked two goals .for St. Helens. Resnit :—Broughton Rangers 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points), St. Helens 3 goals (6 points.) HALIFAX v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>The attendance at this match at Halifax was much below the average. The home players were soon dangerous after the kick off, and Wedgwood £3ll when he was going well for the line. Halifax kept up the attack, and Joe Riley completed a fine passing move- ment and scored, Little failing at goal. Leigh recovered themselves, and some smart all-round play hg Danbavin Whitaker. Orrell, and Johnson followed. There was no further score unto half-time. Halifax played throughout the second half without Nettleton, who was injured. The visitors got dangerous several times, and once Johnson kicked over the head of Little, but the ball rolled into touch. The Halifax hacks kept up a strong attack, and eventually a strong run by Joe Riley ended in a score, this player, after making great headway, passing to Balmer. who got over. Little again failr at goal. Result—Halifax 2 tries (6 points), Leigh, nil</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>HULL v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</head>
        <p>HULL v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</p>
        <p>This match WAS played on the Hull Boulevard Ground before about G,003 sr oetators. The more lively points of-the latter portion were the lucky escapes of the Hull -side from defeat, thanks to the brilliant work of Taylor, -and one or two penalties. Wakefield attacked almost coainually after threequarter time, a fine goal was dropped by Newbonld from a serammtw, and it yr/ after this that Hull made a vigorous effort. They got into their opponents' quarters, and D'Arcy kie.kinz over 'the line, there was a rush by the forwards, and the ball</p>
        <p>was grounded by Wade, who however failed at the goal kick. Result :—Hull I try (3 points), Wakefield I goal (2 points). BATLEY v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Central Park, Wigar, before 6,000 spectators. Within the first minute Wigan were penalised, and Wattie Davies had a fine chance, but he sent the leather wide. .The interval was reached with- out a score. With the resumption Wiga'n attacked in vigorous fashion, and eventually Hopkins received from the pack, and piloted the leather over the bar. Subse- quently play was of a poor description, but Patley had several chances from marks, On three occasions Wattie Davies endeavoured to lower the home goal, and each time he failed. Rusult: —Wigan 1 goal (2 points), Batley nil. OLDHAM v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>- At Oldham. There would not he more than 6,000 !people round the field when Ferguson set the ball in ! motion against the wind. The play had only been four minutes in progress when McLean scored under the posts. Ferguson added another goal. The play had not proceeded many seconds from the rc -start when Albert Goldthorpe got possession behind the s3rimmage well in Oldham territory, and put in one of those grand i drops for which he s renowned, and the leather sailed nicely over the bar. This considerably discounted Oldham's lead. In the second half, Oldham began to pile on the pcOats. A fine swift round of passing begs from Arthur Len to Lawton, vw'so sent the l• ..ther out to McLean. The centre got smartly away, and then swung the ball across to Civil, who passed it on to Carpenter, and the little wing had only a few yards to fly over then and score. Fergusoa fittingly finished the effort with a fine, long kick. Civil and Jardine next scored, and Ferguson placed two more goals. Final : Oldham 20 plints, Hunslet 2 point'.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY ALATCEIES. LATCHFORD 'SINGERS V. PA.DGA.T6 Jtmlioßs. Played on the grouad of the former. Final result : Latchford, 11 points; Padgate, nil.</p>
        <p>_ WARRINGTON "A " v. Roc Pi D A.L141 HORNETS. These teams tried conclusions at Wilderspool on Saturday before a fair attendance. Werrington st ,^Led, and immediately pressed. From the first scrummage R. Bate got possession and gave to Short for the latter to score behind the posts. No goal resalied. Shorily afterwards Berry scared in flue style aid added a goal. Half-time: Warrington "A," 8 points; Rochdale Athletic, nothing. Turning round Warrington tud by far the best of matters and whilst the " vVirepullers" added 18 points, the Rochdale men scorad a goal and a try. Final : Warrington "A," 23 points ; Rochdale Athletic, live points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ALBIONS Y. CRIIIYR BOILER WORKS. Played on Albioas' ground on Saturday. The visitors kicked off, and the home team at once got on the attack Woods scored three goals in about as many minutes. The Boiler men now had a turn, but Appleton sent them back again. The home team again got on the attack, and Houghton scored the fourth goal. No more scoring took place before the interval. Restarting, Albions resumed the attack, and Woods s or I the home team's fifth goal with a good shot. Shortly afterwards, Bullen pat Albions further ahead, giving the visitors' custodian no chance whatever. The visitors got to the other end, but their stay was only of short duration, Appleton sending them back again. Toe Albions broke away,and Woods scored their seventh goal. The Boiler men broke away and the right wing scored with an oblique shot. Result : Albious, seven goals; Crewe Boiler Works oae.</p>
        <p>CROSFIIELDS' V:STAC ItTON H. 4.T11. Played at Stockton Heath on S tturday. The visitors were early aggressive and Monks was tested several times. Ultimately Ball scored for Crosaetes. Ha' r- time : Crosfields' one goal; Stockton Heath, nil. In the second half both sides scored twies, the re-tolt being: Cros fields, three goals; Stockton Heath, two goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DISEASED ANKLE AMR FEVER.</head>
        <p>DISEASED ANKLE AMR FEVER.</p>
        <p>Another Great Zam-Bak Cure.</p>
        <p>Many cases of painful skin disease can be traced back to some illness that has left the system in a weakened state. Mrs. Dyson, of 5, Firebrick Cottages, Washington Station, Durham, had a diseasel ankle after the fever, and this is her account of how Zatn-link cured her com- pletely :—" About 3i years ago I was laid up with rheumatic fever and on getting about again I felt a weakness in my ankle, which got worse and worse until the pain was simply unbearable. I could not do my work. Then it broke out into running sores. There were five wounds round about my ankle and the doctor sclid I should have to lay up again. I did so, but the wounds would not heel. Another doctor whom I consulted said I would have to go to the In- firmary and have some veins taken out of my leg. Whether that is potaible, I don't know, but those veins used to stand out like whip-thongs. The doctor said I would have to be careful lest I should get a ' bat ' on those veins ; if I did it was very likely I should bleed to death. I did not care to go to the Irtirmary.l went on getting worse end worse until my ankle near the top of my font was like a burning fire. The burning and itching was almost unbearable. I continued in this condition for nearly three ye as, sometimes a litde better and sometimes worse. The wounds themselves would never heal though I tri: I almost everything. Then a friend advised Zatu Buk. Alter I had been applying Zan:l-Buk for a little while, I noticed how the wounds were heating anti nen, skin being formed. "Now after persevering with Zins-Buk. the places are perfectly. healed and f can do my work without a twinge of p Lin or any discomfort whatever. I find that the cure is permanent. There has been 113 return of the complaint what 'oever. I feel myself a new woman. I I have to thank Z un-Buk for this. I felt some relief with the first box, but it was only by applying it con- stantly that my long-standing diL oase was finally over come. I thank you for the great boon you have placed in the hands of humanity." Wherever Zarn-buk is applied,disAase is surelyexptllcl and new healthy tissue is grown. Zam-Buk is unequall-; for diseased ankles, bad legs, ulcers, piles, swollen knees, boils, eczema, sore br- •.sts, festering 601 poisoned wounds, cuts, bruises, burns, scalds, spre'ns, colds, scalp irritation, chapped hands, chilblains bad circulation and nerve pains. A box of Zarn-Bull may be had of all chemists or direct iroin the Zain-Bak Co., 4, Red'Cross street, London, E.C. for Is. Id. or 2s. 91. for large size, (2s. 9d. size contains nearly 4 times the Is. lic1;. Every Warrington mother should keep Zam-Buk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>LOCAL HARRIERS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>SANKEY A.C. v. LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Sankey Harriers visited Liverpool on Saturday, to decide an engagement with the Liverpool Harriers. The trail was laid by Messrs. Kelly, Ellis, and Ql4lO. of Liverpool, and Messrs. Edwardson and Thomas, of Sankey, over sven miles of varied country through Broad Green, Caildevall, Roby and. Knotty Ash. Toe pack consisted of 11 Sankey and 10 Liverpool. Joe Riley took the lead from the start, bat owing to a slight injury to his foot, he had to yield to defeat by E. Waterson in the last half mile. Sankey won by five points. ' Details : SANKEY. LIVYAP )01.. Joe Riley 2 E. Waterson 1 John Riley J. Stamps 6 A. Pennington 5 T. Devlin S. Winstanley 9 J. Baines A. Barton lO W. Trotter</p>
        <p>_ 37 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Church Pastoral Aid Society. A meeting on behalf of the Liverpool Diocesan branch of this society was held in the St. Peter's school- room on Monday evening. The Hon. and R3V. C. F. Cross, vicar of Strettor, p esided over a large gather- ing, and spoke of the value of the society, its claim upon the Church and th 3 duty of churchmen to support it. The Rev. J. Davies, vicar of St. Ann's, gave a very instrustive and interestingaddrns, and asked all present. to unite and s:rongly support the society. Captain Hickson and toe Rev. D. R. James also addrecs3,l the gatheting. Miss Glover and Mr. Heron contributed solos, Miss Stanley end Mr. Webster accompanying on the piano.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church. The Rev. William Young, i'ormerly of Higher Brough- ton Presbyterian Church, and now a representative of theßeligioua Tract Soeety, preached at St. John's Church on Sunday even'ng. Tiler° was n large congre- gation. Tak'og for his text the words : "He is despised and roj !tel of men, a man of sorrows and acquainted with grief," Mr. Young delivered a sermon which was listenNi to attentively throughout. The anthem," I will extol Th-s," was rendered by the choir, Mr. J. W. Jowett presiding at the organ. At the close of the service Mr. Young made an appeal on behalf of the funds of the Religious Tract Society, which, he said, did a good work on a very large scale, in the produc- tion aid distribution of Gospel literature, in a great many dilerent langrages, and to the total value of £25,000 per annum. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. There was a good attendance at the meeting on Sunday. Mr. C. Cotton presided, and said he was always willing to do what little he could to help on that very good work. He hoped others would do likewise. Mr. G. Smiley, in the absence through indisposition of the speaker from Manchester, gave an address on God's question to Adam" Where art thou" Adam, he said, instead of getting near to God, tried to get far away from Him, but God saw Him, and knew him. He fell into the hands of an offealsd God. The speakeralluded to the foimality and c_Lreler.ae'.3 of many professing Chris Cans, which was not good for themselves nor yet for others. While people were sleep'mg men were perish- ing. He exhorted fathers to be careful in their example to their sors and daughters, lest any wandered away from Chrisi-. Christ spoke to the Christian backslider and sinner " Where art thou." The Curistian WO9 to be revived, the backslider brought back to Christ, and the sinter saved.—Tae Secrebry welcomed one new mem- ber, making a total 'of 579. Miss Lucy Travis sang two hymns, and the men heartily joined in the choruses. Mr. Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ.—Mr. D. Owen and the Secretary Mr. B. Farrar took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>The- Rev. John Yonge and the Revival. The Need for one in Warrington.</head>
        <p>The- Rev. John Yonge and the Revival. The Need for one in Warrington.</p>
        <p>At Wycl tie -Cu-arch-on Sunday evening th;Rev. John Yonge preached a special sermon on the Welsh revival, taking for his text the words "The Lord bath done great things for vs whereof we are glad,"Plalna 126,v. 3. He said these words beyond doubt were exprrasive of the ve:y wip33pread, and still (spreading, feePag among the churches in Wale-, and dr Ay were they becoming appropriate lattausgc; for some churches in England. However unwilling any of them might fe3l to pay any heed to it, however ready they might 13 to doubt and to di count what they heard, the events in Wales compelled, the cooside atioa of all intelli- gent Caristians, even as they were elso constrain- ing the attention of multitudes who, vs yet, held aloof from the Christian Church. ' Tne work was of such reality, cont7muity, and extent that it could not be ignored, and those who were ready weeks and months ago to predict its speedy evanescence were faced now with its continued prog:ess and increasing develop- ment. Its effects were to patent, radical and practi- cal to be disputed, and it hid become certain that the revival of religion in Wales must take rank with the great historic religious movements of p-at ages, which left their indelible mark not only upon individuals,but upon peoples and even upon the world at large. Mr. Yonge proceeled to quote from a recent a!aicle in the Review of Reviews " in which it was stated that the history of the prog. eel of the world was largely made up of the record of succeesive revivals. The revivel of religion had beea the invari- able precarsor of social and poi it:cal reform. Tae real revival of religion in an individal, continued the preaceer, mee at that he was spiritually awakened to the reality and supremacy of its deepest end truest life. It changed and transformed, it upli!ted and enabled him. A religiously revived Church manifest i simile r exalted chaiacter'stics, and a nation that :fad been rc-011ed and re-animated with the spirit of true religion would be similarly uplifted and reformed It was an old saying that the "darkest moment of the night is that which eimediately precedes the dawn." Now, without being uaduly p.-asimistic, or deeming Wage to be worse than had really L:3?.(1 the case, was it not simp e trutn te say that the condition of religion among all classes of the nation of reeeet years had been exceediraly low? Evidences had been iurnished by the frequent statistics which lied been pubLshed as to the decreneing attendance upan public worship. There were present also in the manifest c.-cvla.rizat:on of Sunday, the devotion of it, both amoral' upper classes and lower alike, to pletraire aid prettme, and sccial functions. Evidencee might be see a also in the depreelated -rt:mate end belief of the Bible, in the spirit of cynicism and scepi ic'sin whit which the Christien faith wrs re g !rend, ia the mad pursuit of pleasure ad sate t, in tile extended prevalence of evil habits, gambling and frivolity. They might also he traced in the unscrapuloonness of pol.tics, in the lust for gold, in tbe p. oud oppression of the poor by the rich, in the gigantic 'tone-Vre tat hid baan revealed and in other taings beside. Did not tile time seem to I have come for some divi me intervention? WI s not the I need real, true and g-eat? Did not the revival in Wales awaken the hope that this di Jin 3 in; ereention was at the door? Asse:idly was it not only at the door, it was la the ca i:ch, ta 3 hones cad the streets and shope, and ii tile very ruiees ii Wales, and the prayer of every Cerist'a a heart might well go up " Wilt thou not revive us again that Thy peapie inay réjohi in Dr ?" The preacher procer ted to narrate in !'dmis of the revival, rein-irking that copies of Welsh newspapers which have devoted special attention to the reporting of the mo bE n kindly furnished him by the editor bf the ‘• Examiner." Some idea of the harvest of souls that was being gathered id could_ be gained by the statement that in severrl hundred churches of !various denensioations, rt:ame Novenber Bth when the !revival began, down to the dal a of the return ia the end of December, over 24,000 conver 9 had teen, or were about to be, received into church fellowship. Aid still the work went on, showing no tokea of decline or exhaustion. Ole Ohre was perfectly certain—there was no longer any religious apathy or indiffer 'ace in Wales. Th‘ revival had net been brought about by novelties or popular themes (so called), or by the often difficult, if not incongruoes admixture of social problems with gospel preaching, and had not been by big manes and blazing advertisements, but by the force of tha gospel spoken by inspired hearts to awakened souls. Iu con- clusion, the preacher appealed for serlousnees, thought- fulness, preyerfuluess and wi !lingne3 to work for the Master. He believed the hour of God's re- vival was at bead, eyed it it be not striking at that moment Religious revival was in the air, and 1905, if he mistook not the signs and portents,was to be a great year in this county among the churches. Already in Blackburn, Ashton, Stockport, Hardee, Liverpool, and London there were signs of iavival. If ever there was a place where the interventiou of divine grace was needed, was it not Warringtonaind surely of all churches in that town where it was needed, was it not there t If they felt that they needed deeper, further sense of spiritual religion, let them ask for it in order that they might receive it ; but lot them ask in faith, nothing doubting. And let them ask persistently, constantly, expectantly. Religion revived would be religion en- joyed, and proved, and promoted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>REV. G. W. DICKINSON'S DEPARTURE.</head>
        <p>THE REV. G. W. DICKINSON'S DEPARTURE.</p>
        <p>Presentation from the Manes Bible</p>
        <p>Glass. I A successful social in connection with th:Narring- ton Parish Cauroh Mea's Bible Class w‘is he'd i i Fair- field Schools on Tdesdty evening. There w:•s a large attendance includinm the Rector (Raw% C mon ‘Villisi Rev. A. S. Camerou,'Pov. G. W. D'eciusoa, gay. T. J. Ridpath, &amp;c. Th 3 first part of the programme oonsis' el of cleacieig interspersed with pawl, &amp;c. Mr. Poole, acoomp t flied by Mr. Darby, kindly gave two songs. At the interval supper was p a,. Iken of, Mr. J. Atitinsoa pro vid:ng an excellent menu. Mr. J Hai, the chtir r IR, then c tilcd upon the Reeler, who lduriug the C 011.3 of a brief address co rnmentsd oa he specie, intr....el; the gad:, re lug had ins-,much as it took the fonn of a fvewell to their deer friend, Mr. Dickinson. He asked those present to set an example to all by attending Caurch more regularly and so snow their love for the Church. Mr. R. W. ANTIVZS, on behalf of the class made the presentation. During the six years Mr. Dickinson had been with them be had worked very hard and conscien- tiously for the welfaiu of the class and de;erved more than their thanks. He had won the esteem of them all. In conclusion he asked Mr. Dickenson to accept a beautiful gold watch and a framed photograph of the class as a token of the respect the members bad for him. Mr. J. HILL, Mr. T. HUGIIKS and Mr. b'. HARDING endorsed all the re.narks ,he previous speakre,e had made. The Rev. G. W. DICKINSON in reply said he came to, take charge of the class in October, MS, and he Calls, as one of the committee had s tid, a raw redruit, a raw Christian, but he thought that some of the corners bed been rubbed off•hint by the hspiration and influence of their kind Rectoh. He would ink them to centre their love on the class awl give the same encouragement and help as showa to him to hi successor, Mr. De3rdon. 1 M., T. HASLANI, in a brief speech propsed the usual vote of thanks, the ladies also being thanked for the decorations. The rest of the evening was spent in dancing,and con- cluded with singing "Mild Lang Syne."'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PERUVIAN HEAD BINDING.</head>
        <p>PERUVIAN HEAD BINDING.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Anthropological Society of Washington, Dr. Hardlicke shewed crania from Peru and Vancouver Island deformed artificially, that from Peru by bandages round the child's head. He remarked that of all the American Continent it is only in Peru and the north-west coast that such deformation of the head is found.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>CIVILISATION AND STATURE.</head>
        <p>CIVILISATION AND STATURE.</p>
        <p>Tim average height of man, according to Mr. A. Dastre, has continued the same for some thousands oY years, as shewn in primitive man, prehistoric man, and historic man. The great size of ancient man is imaginary. Nevertheless, says Science Si fangs, we think it can be shewn that at all events the more civilised races are increasing their stature, and this is more especially the case with women, such as the English and American.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>AUSTRALIA'S LOST RIVERS.</head>
        <p>AUSTRALIA'S LOST RIVERS.</p>
        <p>Several rivers of Australia's interior sink into the earth and are lost. A recent discovery in the district of Eucla showed that they form subterranean lakes 25ft. or 30ft. below the surface, and these lakes, if they prove to contain sufficiently drinkable water, are expected to lead to the development of new territories in the arid region in which they occur. Indeed, large tracts of land have already been rearacti from deserts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ARE ANIMALS CANNIBALS?</head>
        <p>ARE ANIMALS CANNIBALS?</p>
        <p>Cannibalism appears to be unknown among the lower animals in a state of nature. In India, some instances ,of snakes devouring one another have been collected, but it has been pointed out that in every case cited the snakes were of different species. This, it is declared, is no more an act of cannibalism than the devouring of a field-mouse by a sat. Unquestionably, cannibalism was noted some years ago in a London menagerie, when a python ate another of its own kind, but this was under the unnatural conditions imposed by life in captivity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>TESTING AIR FOR CARBONIC OXIDE.</head>
        <p>TESTING AIR FOR CARBONIC OXIDE.</p>
        <p>M. Armand Gautier has presented an important scientific novelty before the French Academy of Sciences. It was an apparatus for effectively testing, by simple means, the presence of carbonic oxide in any atmosphere. The researches leading to the discovery have been carried on under the auspices of the water committee of the municipality. The apparatus is based on the fact that carbonic oxide is a solvent of substances containing iodine. Air is drawn into a receptacle where it mixes with oxide of iodine heated to 60deg. If there is carbonic oxide in the air, the iodine is set free, and on the air being passed into a tube containing chloroform colours it a rose tint, deep in proportion to the quantity of poison contained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>REIYTHM IN RAILS.</head>
        <p>REIYTHM IN RAILS.</p>
        <p>In an article in the Railway Magazine, Mr. Robert Weatherburn, M.1.M.E., makes some interesting</p>
        <p>remarks as to the sounds emitted by the wheeis and the rails on our railways. Every railway, owing to the differences of track and equipment, has its own particular method of expression when carefully noted. Were it possible to take an old and well-seasoned railway traveller, one with the faculties of time and tune moderately developed, and place him blindfold on one of the long-distance railways of the country over which he had been in the habit of travelling, he would sub-con- sciously be led to determine, by the peculiarities of the sound emitted and the involuntary associa- tion of their rappel with some old tune or melody; on which railway he was journeying.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LUCKY ASTRONOMICAL FIND.</head>
        <p>A LUCKY ASTRONOMICAL FIND.</p>
        <p>Professor H. H. Turner, in his interesting vel.unie on Astronomical Discovery, tells us how an astronomical discovery may be a piece of luck. While photographing the stars, the Oxford observers discovered one morning that several plates used during the previous night were failures owing to defective sensitiveness. The Professor thought he would see whether anything could be saved from the wreckage, and during his examination came on one which had been taken at a later hour, but rejected because the instrument had been wrongly pointed. Comparison with a corresponding plate among the failures shewed a bright star on the former which was absent from the latter, and had thus appeared in the interval.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>EXPERIMENTS IN OXIDATION.</head>
        <p>EXPERIMENTS IN OXIDATION.</p>
        <p>According to experiments of M. Schlmsing, common salt accelerates, and lead, zinc, or tin retards, the rusting or oxidation of iron. Arsenic and its salts moderate the rusting action, but•are unsuitable for preserving iron from rust. Sugar, phenol, benzine, and resorcine accelerate, while alcohol retards, the oxidation. It may be argued in this connection, 'rays the Globe, that if lead retards the rusting of iron in certain circumstances, it appears to facilitate it in others. We have repeatedly observed that iron railings fixed into stonework by lead decay much at the lead, perhaps because of chemical or eletro-chernical action there between rain-water and the two different metals. It is a point worthy the attention of plumbers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>INFLUENCE OF CLIMATE ON RACES.</head>
        <p>INFLUENCE OF CLIMATE ON RACES.</p>
        <p>The infiuence of climate on races is illustrated by a paper to an American scientific society by the Hon. A. R. Spofford owthe "Spanish Race of To- day." The Spaniards are polite, observant of etiquette, and sober. The cold of the North, he says, has made the people austere, but the heat of the South has made them lively and fond of pleasure. The natives, though illiterate, are shrewd, agriculture is neglected, lotteries are fostered by tie State, and begging is common. The austerity of Northern as compared with the gaiety of Southern people has also been remarked in the case of the Koreans, and the like difference has been pointed out by Alexander Smith between Scotch Highlanders and Irishmen. Highland humour, according to Alexander Smith, is rather strenuous than playful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MOTIONS OF THE MOON.</head>
        <p>MOTIONS OF THE MOON.</p>
        <p>The moon, as everyone knows, is the greater cause of the tides, the sun's influence being not more than half of our satellite's, because of the extreme nearness of the latter body. In distant ages the. moon spun round as the earth still does, but the tidal action of our world on the moon has so stopped the spin that she now always turns the same face to us. This 4,idal action acts like a brake on a revolving wheel, and the time will come, hundreds of millions of yearsehence, if the, solar system lasts so long, when the earth also will turn the same face to the moon, and our day be at least a month long_. But the interaction of tidal forces tends to drive our satellite further and further from us. Year by year the moon is getting a few inches more distant: and reversing the argument, year by year, in the great past, that the moon was nearer to us.</p>
        <p>THE FROIMMI OF AERIAL NAVIGATION.. In a paper dealing with the difficulties of aerial navigation, Professor Calvin 11. Woodward, of Aawrica, declares that there are two lines of attack, which, while differing in aspect, have very much in common. One seeks to devise methods for navigating the air as birds 'do, relying for sulport solely upon Mechanical and muscular energy, while the other depends,, more or less,upon tee -buoyancy, oi hydrcgeu gas while securing! roi:tdsion by means of propellers. The sup- 'killers of the former method draw their analogy iron, the propelling of ocean ships by mechanical Tower. But, as Professor Woodward points out, the case is hardly analogous for whereas, When it was first proposed to propel an ocean ship by means of mechanical powa, it was assumed as a matter of course that the ship itself could Boat tii.on the water, so that the sole purpose for ioiploving mechanism was to drive it forward and w:eer it, in aerial navigation the ship is not only to he driven iorwaro, but it must be supported. ihi analogous case, therefore, would be that of a :wavy swimmer who must both support and drive iinself forward, and hence the problem of aerial acigation is only to be accomplished, says the ()lessor, along the lihe,ti furnishing a temporary st vrt or buoyancy while mastering the difficulties If I....cement along desired paths by mechanism.</p>
        <p>,e 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>HOLD ON TO YOUTRTULNESS.</head>
        <p>HOLD ON TO YOUTRTULNESS.</p>
        <p>Old age cannot be called an evil when it is taken as Miss Mitford, the authoross of "Our Village" took it.. She retained her goo I tem- per, her enjoyment of the simple commonplace pleasures of life, and her literary tastes, with such a liveliness of spirit and such a tenderness of heart that it seemed as if time could not touch her, and that, notwithstanding her seventy years, she could not grow old. She took as keen an interest as ever in the new poet, the new painter, the new flower. There are some people who are only too ready to grow old. Instead of this we should try to keep young in thought and sympathy. This is a very difierent thing from trying to play the rOle of youth after decay has taken hold of us, and decrepitude set in. It is a sad sight to see an old lady, wrinkled and withered, dressing. talking, and acting like a very young oise. Old boys disguise this foible a little better, but they are equally, ridiculous. When people said to Diogenes, "You are an Ald man, and should rest for the remainder of your life." "Why so P" he replied ; " sup- pose I had run a long distance, ought Ito stop when I was near the end and not rather press on 2" People are quite right in these days of struggle to leave off money-making when they have got enough, and give the younger genera- tion a chance. In every town and parish, how- ever, there is much work, needing to be done by !someone, that would save the old from the pains and ugly otfiects of idleness, and remunerate them largely though not in - materiel coin. If the man who does not play whist or golf is pre- paring at miserable old age for himself, this is much more true pf him who has no hobby. Some people profess to wish for a sudden death, by which it is to be supposed that they do not mean an unprepared death. !Stoat a us, however. would prefer a short period of waiting, as did one of the Captains of Charles V. of Spain. When old and infirm be asked tke Emperor to discharge hull from public sarvice beatsv " there Ought to 'be a pause haw et-ii the tumult of life and the day of dealli." The best thing is neither, to love our lives nor to bate them, but to lire what we live well; and at be anxious either way as to their duration. We know; ~everal old people who are quite willing to leave life but, who are coutefited to wait ..s long rt. they are of any use. And they are of use. .yinpathise with and give the benefit of their experience to the young, and they teach to nil a, est.on of acertul iepigraticn. Aitcr the day ie. over, the sun seems sometimes to ham; tor Liar an hour in the horizon, as if to show how glorious it can be, So God lets some people, when theh duty in this world is done, hang in the West. 80 to speak, that men may look upon them, and see how beautiful is the ending of a well-spent life. A friend told the writer recently that the</p>
        <p>EINEM</p>
        <p>last days on earth of his agen father quite as- tonished him, so happily and pencefully were they spent. Having made his will, and arranged all his business, be said, " I am :just waiting," with as much satisfaction as a schoolboy says, " I am going home." Tllis reminds us of some words of the authoress of Uncle Tom's Cabin," quoted in her biography :—" I feel about all things now as I do about the things tha happen in an hotel after my trunk is packed to go home. I may be vexed and annoyed, but what of it ! I am going home soon." When asked what age he was, a man answered " The right side of eighty. " I thought - you were more than eight);," said the enquirer. " Yes ; I am beyond it,' he replied, " and that is the right side, for I am nearer my eterna rest."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>GROW OLD GILA.CEFULLY.</head>
        <p>GROW OLD GILA.CEFULLY.</p>
        <p>• If we cannot help growing old in years, at least we can all try to grow old gracefully. " Do you think I'm a fool, sir ?" thundered a fiery Scotch laird to his new footman. "Wed, sir," replied the canny Scot. " I'm not long here and cannot ken yet." If we ought not to call a man happy till he 'die, it is equally pre- mature to call him wise till we see how he man- ages his old age. One is not. growing old grace- folly, who becomes slovenly in dress, acquires odd tricks, makes disagreeable noises' praises and thinks of nothing but the past. From an old f gey like this, the young, not unnatural- keep sway. Querulousness and pessimism should always be fought against. The old have had their good things, and instead of expecting much now they should be ready ta jump for joy if nothing ails them. The way to grow old wisely, is the way of self-control, the way of sympathy and the way of unselfishness. The grand essentials of happiness in every sage of life, but particularly in the last, are having something to do, something to love, and some- thing to hope for. " Let those love now, who never loved before, Let those who always loved now lovetthe more.'• In Cicero's famous discourse on old age, the. dialogue opens with the request which a young man makes to Cato, to tell him how it is that he finGs old age FO pleasant, while to other men it is a burden, lie first answers, generally, that it is true tint tomiany old men age is a wretched condition, and one which is deservedly cantemp- tible as well as wretched. And he gives instances • both from history, and from his own observa4 tion of living, men,—but he says that tide is not fault or defect of nature, but of the men • themselves. And if old age is pleasant to him-; rlf, and if he is at all worthy (as he desires to be) of the name of wise, which his friends are wont to give him, it is because he has always obeyed the laws of nature, and sulanitted to her guidance as to that of a god. It is not likely that she, who has brought us well through all the stages of life, should, like an indolent poet, fail in the last act. .Al things must have an end, and for man to be dissatisfied when the fruit is ripe, is, like the giants, to war with the gods. 'When a certaiu Frenchwoman, eighty years old, was running over the catalogue of her ailments, her physician at last said to her, " What would you have, madam ? I cannot make you young ag,aiu !" Ordinary practi- tioners cannot do this ; but there are four fam- ous doctors who, if they cannot make us young UM give us an old age not frosty but kindly. Their antes are Temperance, Exercise, Good Air, and Early flours. Many people do not believe in these physicians, because they are cheap, un- affected end truth ; bat when they are obeyed old age stays away far longeri. and when it does come it is far less burdensome! E. J. HARDY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>COST OF STREET PAVING.</head>
        <p>THE COST OF STREET PAVING.</p>
        <p>Property Owners and the Corporation.</p>
        <p>The magistrates at the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday were engaged in deciding a disputed apportion- ment of the cost of forming and paving the passage behind the houses numbered 16 to 38, Manchester-road. The executors of the late Mr. Joseph Harrison com- plained that the charge of £2B 18s. alloted to them was excessive and they, were represented at the hearing by Mr. E. A. White. Mr. Stanley Wilson, deputy town lelerkoppeared on behalf of the Corporation, and Mr. W. J. Forshaw represented the trustees of the late Mr. IJohn Appleton who were also interested in the case. The magistrates adjudicating were Messrs. J. W. Has- ward (presiding) and J. Rymer Young. Mr. WILSON said the only question before the Court would be as to whether the Corporation were correct in apportioning to the exors of the late Mr. Joseph Harrison a sum of £2B 18s. as the proportion of the cost of the paving of the passage behind houses 16 to 138 in Manchester-road. The total cost amounted to £l2O 16s. 4s. There were four ownersaffected, namely, Mr. John Appleton to the extent of £5B I.os. ld. ; Mrs IHarriet Booth, £l4 its. • executors of the late Mr. J. Harrison' £2B 18s. ; and Mr. Benjamin Jameson, £lB 117 s. 3d. The objectors owned six houses which fronted land abuted on the passage ir question with a frontal of 821 t. lin. At the present time they had no entrance into the pa.ssage, and the only way of getting to tne back of their premises was through a covered passage Mt. wide leading into a common yard to all the six houses. That was the only means of getting to the back of the premises, fo,. bringing in coal, &amp;c. When the works had been completed and the streets properly formed and paved the passage wouH be taken over by the Corporation and for ever maintained. The objec- tcrs would have the advantage of an open space right behind their premises, and also .the advantage of a properly paved passage which would convey away all the surface water and prevent any water percolating into the yard below, as well as odpitional air space and means of access to the back premises. He asked the Bench to refuse to make any alteration in the proper- tionment which bad been prepared by the Borough Sur- veyor. The principle involved was of some importance to the Corporation, and if altered would mean no end of difficulty to them from time to time Mr. Forahaw supportad Mr. Wilson's remarks. It was he contended, a right and proper apportionment, and anything the magistrates took off would probably be put upon his clients' shoulders. He did not think that would be fair, considering that they had given the land for the passage and were plying the ground rent and half the cost of the apportionment, to interfere with the decision of the Corpora"on. Mr. T. Lose DIN, borough surveyor, gave evidence and said the apportionment was right and proper. In answer to Mr. White he said that for upwards of 100 years the propertyhad existed and had had this particu- lar access to Manchester-road. There WAS a difference of level of Mt. 41n., and he suegested that if they wished to make an access into the new passage they Icorld do so by making four steps. Mr. WHITE asked,the (Bench to bear in mind tha section 7of the Private Street Works Act said that in settling the apportionment they might take into account the greater or less degree of benefit to be derived by premises from such works. He was not going to argue that his clients ought to be charged nothing. It was clear at some future period they might, or their successors might, pull all the property down and build something —hich might get a benefit from the passage, but at the present time to ey were not getting the bene- fit that other people were. His clients were at present wailed off from the passage of a 9in. wall. If they con- sidered they could use the passage to any advantage they must pierce the wall in one, two, three or half-at dozen places, put a door in and also at least four sters to get to the level of the land. Mr. White argued the point further, and contended that on the evidence of the Snrveyor there were no facts in dispute so tar as he knew ; and he asked the bench in coming to a dec- eon to ,give his clients some little compensation for thi serious drawbaas they were labouring under as com- pared with the other property owners. After considerable legal argument the magistrates retired to consider their decision, and on returning the chairman said they had decided that the apportionment should be .£2O instead of £2B 18s. 9d. Mr. WILSON said that the whole of the apportionment would now be affected,and he asked the bench to adjourn the case so that the adjoining owners could be served with notices ,ncreasing their assessments, as they had power to do under section six of the Act. Mr. WHITE objected, and said the Bench had decided the case already. The Bench decided to adhere to their decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
