      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CHAMPION MOULTS.</head>
        <p>CHAMPION MOULTS.</p>
        <p>merit in Russia, one thing seems tolerably assured Kuropatkin has probably seen the last of his reinforcements. If the example of St. Petersburg should be followed by the workmen in other_ industrial centres, the " Little Father " will Leed pretty nearly all his troops who are still at home to continue his paternal benevolence to his beloved subjects. Even if the people be cowed for a time, it will not be safe to send to Manchuria the regiments which are being used so at home. It will be interesting to know whether the Russian soldiers on the Sha-ho feel a glow of pride in the achievement of their comrades in Sc. Petersburg, and whether this will animate them with fresh vigour for the next' -ncounter with their victorious enein .</p>
        <p>We are sure we hardly need remind our readers of Mr. Lloyd George's visit next week. The posters on the walls proclaim the fact it eveey corner,and the matter has been the subject of gossip for week's past.lf there is one man whom the people of Warrington have been specially anxious to hear,it is the Welsh Parnell,as the member for Carnarvon Boroughs is often termed.llis unswerving adherence to prineiple,his determined and forceful opposition to measures which imperil the religious freedom of the people, and his intrepid bearding of Mr. Chamberlain, have fired the public imagination so ' much that there is now no man more assured of popular acclaim, or more looked to for a lead on ' questions of the day. We hope the Warrington Liberals will give Mr. Lloyd-George such a recep- tion as will cause him to bear an ever-grateful recollection of his visit to South Lancashire.</p>
        <p>It is now assured beyond a doubt that War- rington will have a suitable memorial to the me of the South Lancashire Regiment who fell in the South African campaign. The Workingmen's Committee which has worked nobly for a worthy cause, wound up its affairs, so to speak, on Wednesday 'evening, although subscriptions will still be gladly received. Notwithstanding hard times the committee has succeecbd in raising nearly £2oo—a result which speaks volumes for the energy of the officials. The total sum in the hands of the general committee is now just over £l,OOO, which is expected to defray the cost of the :monument itself. We feel sure the townspeople in general will endorse the workingmen representatives' sugges- tion that Sir Redvers Buller should be invited to unveil the memorial. Probably no military man is more popular with the bulk of the populace than the General who had the hardest task of the campaign, and the ungracious treatment meted out to him excited genuine sympathy in Warring ton as elsewhere. Should he visit Warrington— and we hope he will—Sir Redvers would have a magnificent greeting from the town which sent forth many of the gallant men who fought under his command.</p>
        <p>There is no checking the ebb of the Ministerial tide. Mr. Balfour still endeavours vainly to per- suade the country that his policy of retaliation will bring about a closer commercial union with the colonies ; and that home prices will not be raised for the purpose of aiding home productions, but the country is turning a persistently deaf ear to him. Now North Dorset has gone the way of East Dorset and many other constituencies which have been contested since Free Trade was assailed. The Tory majority at the last election in the district was MO, and on Thursday this was replaced by a Liberal majority of 909. Mr. Wills has indeed won a great victory. Even his supporters antici- pated a close contest. Sanguine as they were they must have under-estimated the strong feeling against anything in the shape or guise of Pro- tection. No wonder Mr. Balfour was not happy on the platform at Ardwick on Thursday evening. He must have had the dread presentiment of an additional calamity. Nevertheless he set his face unflinchingly against any voluntary rAsignation. Practically, he says that so long as he can command a majority in the House he will "hang on" to office. But the march of events may become too rapid for Mr. Balfour. The unmistakable fact that the nation is nauseated with the present Government is bound to have its effect even on an obedient Parliamentary majority.</p>
        <p>Mr. Shackleton M.P., who occupied the chair at the opening of the annual conference of the Labour Representation Committee in Liverpool on Thursday, made some interesting and sensible remarks in regard to ,the policy of the L.R.C. in constituencies where there is no Labour 'candidate. He is one of the group of I M.P.'s who refuse to be hide-bound to the exact letter .of the L.R C. constitution, though at the same time pledged to its principles. Moreover, Mr. Shackleton believes in the homely axiom that " half-a-loaf is better than no bread," and in this respect differs somewhat from some of his fellow L.R.C. members in Warrington. They apparently would rather starve, in a political sense, than obtain labour reforms piecemeal. We hope Mr. Shackleton's statement that" they would always be ready to co-operate with any party which for the time being might be engaged in promoting legislation in the direct interests of Labour" will be weightily considered and endorsed by the L.R.C. in Warrington. Such import- ant measures as the Trades Disputes Bill, the Compensation to Workmen's Amendment Bill, and the Miners' Eight Hours Bill, cannot be held over until Labour has an authoritative and com- manding voice in Parliament backed by a majority, and it behoves Labour men to accept help wherever it is oftered. Here in Warrington they know that the:Liberal candidate is prepared to discuss with them any question affecting the interests of Labour, and in many cases guarantee theta his support. Seeing that direct representation is at present quite out of the question, they will be ill-advised, and acting contrary to the advice of an experienced politician like Mr. Shackleton, if they do not get all they can out of Mr. Crosfield and back him against an opponent pledged to Protection and the buttressing of class interests.</p>
        <p>Apart from its being economically unsound and commercially bad,there is another side toProtection which, it is imperative, should not be lost sight of —and that is the fostering of trusts, combines and other selfish interests. Once the principle of pro- tection for ,certain commodities is agreed to and the clamour for special treatment becomes general. Every manufacturer who sees a sure road to wealth through the restriction of foreign competition goes cap in hand—and perhaps cheque in pocket—to ttio legislature, and the war of tariffs—a war as ruinous as that of arms —has commenced' The certainty of capital exer- cising its persuasive power on the legislature, for its own selfish ends is one of the strongest possible arguments against Protection and for an open Fr.le Trade market. Workingmen should be the first to realize this ; it affects them most. They have only to look to America for some sinister examples of what Protection and capitalistic combination mean. in another column we publish an article by Mr. H. Whither° —than whom tbere is no one better qualified to speak to workingmen—on "Frenzied Finance." It well repays perusal by those who are anxious to obtain an insight into the hold the almighty dollar has on "the press, the books, the stump, and the halls of statesman- ship" of the great republic. Mr. Whitham quotes from the series of articles written by Mr. Thomas Lawson, a man who for a number of years has had close acquaintance with the great Ameri- can trusts and is now devoting himself to an exposure of the way they " rig " the market and influence the vote of the people. The minority report of an inquiry into the United States Mer- chant Shipping Question says:—" It has been con- clusively shown by testimony before this commission that materials which enter into the construction of ships are sold by our factories and laid down in foreign shipyards for a price far below that charged at the mills to our own people—a condition that would not exist but for the unjust provisions of our tariff laws." The system is one which breeds the multi-</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>A • ' "Ij !:1). .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>JANUARY 1905.</p>
        <p>The reports from St. GOVERNMENT BY Petersburg go to show. BUTCHERY, that some sort of military</p>
        <p>rule has been established over the citizens of St. Petersburg by the brute force of arms. But Europe has meanwhile been forming a judgment which will, whatever happens, be a great accession of strength to the Reform Party in Russia, whenever they hear of it. The figures of the crime which have been issued offici- ally will fail altogether of their intent if it was thought by the TSAR'S advisers that they would serve to belittle the affair into one of mob-riot. The TSAR has made a momentous choice. Rather than discuss reforms with his people, he decided to rule by butchery. Thousands of unarmed citizens have been killed or wounded in the streets of St. Petersburg. Women and children have been slaughtered by the soldiers of the " Little Father." The Cossack, who has distinguished him- self in Manchuria, by retiring from the Japanese, wins undying glory at St. Petersburg by trampling down the workmen who had no weapon but a petition. Rifle and bayonet have prevailed for the time, but it may prove in the long run that the petition is the most formidable weapon.</p>
        <p>There can be no mistaking the depth of the feeling which has prompted the thousands of Russians to offer up their lives for an idea. Whether the peasantry will be affected by the revolt it is impossible to say; but industrial R.lssia has declared war on the Bureaucracy, and the movement which began as a simple demonstra- tion in the streets may proceed b organised con- spiracy. It is incredible that the blood already shed will remain unavenged. The TSAR might have taken a different course. He might have received the petition which, after all his people had a perfect right to sub- mit, and he might have answered that. The political changes demanded were incompatible with his autocracy, but that the grievances of tee workmen about their own condition would be made the subject of inquiry. If this had been done, the demonstration outside the Winter Palace might have passed off harmlessly. But the autocrat decided to teach his people that they had no right even to approach him. For the pre- sumptuous folly of having wrongs and assembling to demand redress,they must be shot down, young and old,in a vast undiscriminating massacre. The "Little Father " has the pleasure of knowing that the blood of helpless children stains the snow lin his capital. It may be said that autocracy is fighting foi its life, and that no concession can be made without undermining the whole future. This is true. But when autocracy takes to butchering its citizens, the plea of its own security is not accepted by civilised mankind. Not a voice beyond that of here and there an eccentric is lifted in Europe to palliate the crime at St. Petersburg. The French I are profoundly shocked. They have watched with amdzement the misadventures of their Ally in the East, and the revelations of the corruption and incapacity which have made the success of the Russian army well-nigh impossible. They I have seen the enormons mass of discontent in Russia brought to a head by the unpopularity of a disastrous war. And now they see the troops who have suffered nothing but defeat at the hands of the Japanese, turning their weapons against their own compatriots. The autocracy has ignominiously failed to conquer Japan, but it has the consolation of conquering Russian women and children, and strewing the banks of the Neva with their !corpses. What little sympatny with the TSAR may have survived in France is extinguished by this spectacle. Whatever I may be the fortunes of the revolutionary move-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>1.-:,-'•:</p>
        <p>;,',ii.'Ji.:'','..,7.::%i•',-...,,,k,,',;:::,...4,..t r...i'..4'.i,i,'.:::',..:.:•'-'i',.,.-,.;;,.''.';.:...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>millionaire and prevents entirely the distribution of wealth into channels which are open to the 'working-classes. There is little of this kind of thing in Free Trade England, but it would spring np immediately under that Protection which is— thank Heaver —receiving its death blow at every by-election in the country.</p>
        <p>Than the Boteler Grammar there is no more honoured educational institution in Warring- ton, and the desire of the authorities and the governors that the school should take a worthy place in the complete scheme of education which is aimed at, will meet with general assent. The Board of Education have already been consulted upon the matter but although there has been considerable discussion no final arrangement between the governing body and the Educatica Committee has yet been arrived at. With the object of facilitating pro- gress a conference between the governors of the Grammar School and the School Manage- ' ment Sub-committee of the Education Com- mittee was held at the Town Hall on Wednesday morning under the presidency of Mr. A. Cardew, one of his Majesty's inspectors, who was supported by Mr. J. Parry Laws, M.EI I, for the district. The Board of Education propose to give a new scheme for the management cf the school, and the points under discussion were: • firstly, the constitution of the governing body, and secondly, the tuition fees. It is proposed to increase the number of representative governors to be appointed by the Education Committee and, if possible, to lower the fees payable for the educa- tion of the students. There has, of course, been no definite outcome of the conference up to the present, but we hope that a satisfactory under- standing will not be long delayed. -- Hardly has the case of one London public official who embezzled been cleared from the Old Bailey list before the Town Clerk of Holborn walks into the dock at Bow.street. His con- fession is to a sum of £10,000; and, from what the Mayor of the borough has stated, it seems that this admission is only too true. It would be exceedingly wrong to comment on acase which has still to be tried; but it is fitting to remind public bodies of their duty alike to the ratepayers, them- selves, and their servants. Holborn is not alone among Corporations in its uncertainty as to what emoluments a Town Clerk is entitled to receive, and the question of commissions is not specially a London trouble. Mr. Justice Darling, in the affair of the Guardians' Clerk to which reference has already been made, pointed out that but for a quarrel of that official with his clerk illicit takings could have gone on without discovery. Now there are several prit.ciples which every authority should be willing to recognise. The first is to pay their officials liberally. Toe second is to have a clear and perfectly agreeable under- standing that staffs must expect supervision. The best work that Town Councillors and Guardians can do in this respect can never be known to the gallery; and such investigation should not be factious or in suspicion. It should be a business- like scrutiny and wideawakeness, such as a big business man would have in regard to the conduct of his own manager. Conscientious public ser- vants will welcome such a guard over duty. If it is not done, they may have reason to doubt, while keeping to paths of rectitude, whether honesty is the best policy. Few of the defections come to light.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. WEEK ENDING JANUARY 21st, 1905. During the week, the following births and deaths •were registered :—Warrington Registration District births, 50, deaths, 25. The excess of births over deaths was 25. _ _</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. WEEK ENDING JANUARY 21st, 1905. During the week, the following births and deaths •were registered :—Warrington Registration District births, 50, deaths, 25. The excess of births over deaths was 25. _ _</p>
        <p>• The birth-rate was equal to 37.6. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 18.0 per 1,000 against 22.6 the average of the previous 4 weeks and against 175 the corresponding period of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to nil per 1,000, against •7 the average rate of the Previous 4 weeks and against 3.0 the corresponding period of 1904. The ages of mortality were : '3-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65—up. Total</p>
        <p>8 3 3 7 4 25 The deaths included-1 from influenza, 8 from lung diseases, 1 from heart disease, 3 from phthisis, 1 from accident, 11 from all other causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during Vle week :—Scarlet fever, 8 cases ; enteric fever 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending 21st January, 1905, 74. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 33 Fab. and therefore 7 deg. lower than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 42 F., was recorded on the 15th inst., and the minimum temperature 22 F., on the 22st lust. The rainfall measured .29 inch. The general direc- 'lion was S.E. J. GUEST GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
