      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>PARR S BANK. Advice to the Cotton-Growing Association.</head>
        <p>PARR S BANK. Advice to the Cotton-Growing Association.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of Parr's Bank, Limited, was held in London on Thursday. Mr. Cecil F. Parr, the chairman, in moving the adoption of the report, said that those who had studied the financial papers would not have expected an increase of dividend, but some of them must have been agree- ably relieved when they found that the dividend was going to be the same as in the preceding year. The diminution of profits had to a great extent taken place in purely London institutions and was not so marked in the country. In a bank like theirs, with as large a business in the country Re in London, the profits were steadier all the year. It was true they were more fortu- nate than some of their neighbours. That was owing to the Japanese loan and some other circumstances. They had also seen a change come over the cotton industry. He hoped the newly-formed Cotton-growing Association would not relax their efforts to provide a new field for growing cotton and make themselves inde- pendent of the American supply. It was rather disap- pointing to find that the appeal for funds had in some directions been somewhat feebly responded to, especially by those who were directly interested in cotton cultivation. During the past year there had been a new departure in the matter of municipal banking in regard to municipal loans, and those who were the best able to judge were doubtful of the wisdom of the step. Out of 100 municipalities who had been consulted in the matter 62 had decided against this extension of banking, and out of those who had tried it 11 had proved an abso- lute failure, although the interest was as high as 31 per cent. The movement, therefore, had not shown any great vitality. They were entering on a new year with much hope. In agriculture, which was one of their greatest industries, they could not expect much improvement, but there would be in commercial matters, especially in the cotton mills and the iron and steel works, which would be working full time. Mr. Nix seconded the report, which was adopted.</p>
        <p>"A Cruise In the Western Fjords of Norway." The delights of a holiday in romantic Norway were pictured to a large audience in St. Paul's schoolroom on Monday evening by the Rev. R. 13. de Wolf, of Liverpool, who lectured on " A cruise in the Western Fjords of Norway." In his visit to this land of mountains, glaciers and water falls some years ago, The rev, gentleman was accompanied by the Rev. W. .Bracecamp and the account which be gave of their "dventures was highly interesting. The lecturer ,possesses a rare fund of wit and his way of relating • necdotes created no little amusement. A series of ..;beautifnl limelight views was exhibited, the Rev. W. ,tracecamp manipulating the lantern. The audience was thus enabled to obtain some idea of the grandeur and magnificence of Norwegian scenery, and the even- mg passed very pleasantly.—On the proposition of ^ Mr. J. H. Smethurst (vicar's warden), seconded by thei ' Rev. H. H. Bowen, Mr. De Wolf was heartily thanked for his services.—ln reply, he said it had afforded him great pleasure to come to Warrington because there Was no clergyman in whose work he was more interested than in that of the Rev. W. Bracecamp. (Applause.)—lt was announced that Mr. Do Wolf will deliver another lecture in the parish at an early date.</p>
        <p>The Waifs and Strays Society. At St. Paul's Church on Sunday the annual sermons on behalf of the Waifs and Strays Society ef the Church of England were preached by the Rev. Hawley Sharpe, organizing secretary. The service in the afternoon was principally for children, and was known as "Pound service." Contributions such as pounds of tea, rice, sugar, flour, coffee, &amp;c., were wrapped in white Paper, banded to the clergy, and deposited within the communion rails. The collections amounted to the handsome sum of £ll lie. sd.</p>
        <p>'p</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>Progressive Labour League. The 'weekly meecing of this league was held at the Liberal Club on Monday evening, under the presidency of Mr. J. Elwell. A paper on " A national system of education " should have been submitted by the Rev. H. Dunnico, but owing to indisposition he was unable to be present. A discussion on various political topics took plaoe. Mr. Minnie() will read his paper at a meeting on Tuesday week. PUBLICMENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Catholic Young Men's Dance. Gratifying success attended the first annual dance promotea by the members of the Catholic Young Men's Society and held in .the Parr Hall, on Monday evening. Nearly 500 persons were present including the Mayor and Mayoress of Warrington, the Mayor and Mayoress of Widnes,as well as several councillors and their wives from Warrington, St. Helens, and Widnes. Excellent music was provided by Mr. A. Starkey's band. The duties of M.C. were ably dis- charged by Messrs. P. Delaney and E. W. Kea.tinge, who were assisted by Messrs.o wen Toole, M.Cunning- ham, J.Fagan, T. Newns, T. Mather, J. Gleeson, andj H. Houghton as stewards. A ladies' committee had charge of the catering. " Our Navy." A lecture on this subject arranged by the social club of Messrs. Crosfield's Fire Brigade, was delivered in the Assembly Room at the works on Tuesday evening by Mr. Kermode, engineer, of Liverpool. Mr. Holman Kingdon presided over a large attendance. —The lecture was exceedingly interesting, being illustrated by lantern slides, depicting all 'kinds of! warships, and also the interior and exterior parts , during construction. All the bad points of former years, said the lecturer, had now been done away with, and to-day we had a first class fleet. He alluded to the war between Japan and Russia, and said it ought to make everyone interested in warships. On the motion of Mr. Dale, seconded by Mr. Knowles, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer. Funeral of Mr. H. N. Hartley. At St. Thomas's Church, Stockton Heath on Mon day the remains were interred of the late Mr. Herbert N. Hartley, youngest son of Mr. Hartley, of the Inland Revenue Department, Warrington, the Limes, Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath. The chief mourners were :—Mr. Hartley, Mr. J. J. Hartley, Mr. Percy Hartley, and Mr. Ernest Hartley, and Messrs. R. Oldgate, J. Clarke, Walter Longdin, Price, and Dr. Parker. Wreaths were sent by the following:— Father and mother, sisters and bothers, Mr. and Mrs. J. J. Hartley, office staff, Mr. and Mrs. Newcombe, Mr. and Mrs. Johnson, Mr. and Mrs. Studer, Mr. Walter Longdin, Mr. and Mrs. Findlay (Aberdeen), Miss Stewart (Inverlonry, Aberdeen), Mr. and Mrs. Wise (London), Mr. and Hrs. J. A. Thompson and family (London), Mr. and Miss Galpin (London). The whole of the funeral arrangements were in the hands of Messrs. J. and W. Dutton, Sankey-street. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. Railway Staff Dinner.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE 2. Warrington Guardians. Runcorn Guardians. Women's Chat and Fashions. " A Microbe Maniac," by Fred Whishaw.</p>
        <p>3. "From Strenuous Youth to Gracehl Age," by Rev. E. J. Hardy, M.A. Our Local Churches. Science Notes. Street Paving Case. Football. il Education Committee, Increase of Salaries. £2OO Compensation to a Workman. Police News. 7. Billiards. Runcorn Rural Council. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the itING OF TONICS.—G 3, SANKEY ST., WARRINGTON.—Advt. Coming Events. Warrington Baptists will be honoured with a visit from the President of the Baptist Union (Rev. John Wilson) to-morrow. The rev, gentleman will preach at Golborne-street Church in the morning and at the Parr Hall in the evening. The Rev. John Yonge will preach at Wycliffe Church to-morrow morning on "Fishers of men" and in the evening on "The search that satisfies the soul." At the Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay, this a!ternoon the new organ will be formally opened, and Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., will give a recital. In the evening the congregational tea and concert will be held. Mr. A. H. Crosfield will preside at the latter gathering. The services will be continued to-morrow. An open meeting of Wycliffe P.S.A. Society will be held to-morrow afternoon when Mr. J. Walley, of Manchester, will be the speaker. Mr. J. Keir Hardie, M.P., and Mr. J. A. Seddon (Labour candidate for Newton division) will be the principal speakers at a Labour demonstration to be held in the Parr Hall on Monday evening. Mr. D. Lloyd George M.P., will address a great mass meeting of Liberals in the Parr Hall next Friday evening. A sacred concert in aid of the widow and children of the late Mr. H. Whitehouse will be held in the Royal Court Theatre, on Sunday, February sth (by permission of Messrs. Johnson and Potter, pro- prietors). An excellent programme will be sub- mitted by local artistes and such a deserving effart should command generous support. The fourth annual concert arranged by i the War- rington Male Voice Choir will be held n the Parr Hall on Saturday, February 11th. A number of well known artistes will appear. In connection with the forthcoming gatherings of the National Council of Evangelical Free Churches in Manchester, a local meeting will be held in Parr Hall, on Thursday, March 9th. ASK for CIINNMGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Personal, The Rev. Alfred Barratt has just resigned the pastorate of Ebenezer Church, Buttermarket-street, after having had charge of the work for several months.</p>
        <p>The members of the clerical staff of the London and North-Western Railway goods department held their thirteenth annual dinner on Saturday evening at the Lion Hotel, when upwards of 30 members sat down to an excellent repast provided by the host, Mr. Lee. After dinner the chair was taken by Mr. Townley, the agent. The toasts honoured were The health of the , King and the Royal Family," proposed by the Chair- man, and received with musical honours ; " The London and North-Western Railway," by Mr. H. C. Kite ; " Town and Trade of Warrington," by Mr. F. T. Newbould ; and "The donors," by Mr. R. Sumner. The programme included songs by Messrs. Dutton. Crompton, Devonport, Jenkinson, Johnson, Watkins, Downie, Wood; duets by Messrs. Warburton and Jenkinson and Crompton and Davenport; a clarionet duet by Messrs. Limb and Davenport, and a humorous sketch by Mr. J. J. Kite, entitled " Our James Edward.' After a vote of thanks to the host and the pianist, a most enjoyable evening was brought to a close by the singing of" Auld lang syne." Farewell Gathering. A large gathering of members of the Methodist Free Church, Buckley-street, with their friends met in the schoolroom on. Monday evening. After tea the party adjourned to the church to wish God-speed and a pleasant voyage „to three of their members (Mrs. Freeth and her daughters, Mary and Martha) who set sail for America on Wednesday. Mr. E. E. Pester presided over the meeting, and after the choir had rendered choruses, asked the president of the church (Mr. J. Adams) to present a beautiful family Bible to Mrs. Freeth. The President expressed the sorrow of the church members to part, with such valuable servants as Mrs. and the Misses Freeth had been to the church since they had been connected with it. Mr. A. Jennings (school superintendent) presented on behalf of the Sunday school scholars an album to Miss Mary Freeth, and said he trusted that she would still continue to work for God after she arrived at Philadelphia. The Secretary of the school, Mr. J. Ryder, then presented Miss Martha Freeth with a teacher's Bible, and the class leader, Mr. H. Woolley, prpsented one to Miss Mary Freeth. Mrs. Simpson also spoke words of farewell, and Miss L. Plinston rendered " The last milestone." At the close the con- gregation haartily sang " God he with vou till we meet again." BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J.S. GARLAND, Furnishing ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Photographic Society. The fortnightly meeting of this society on Tuesday took the form of a musical evening under the direction of Mr. H. Bond, and the room at the Old Academy was filled with members and friends. The programme comprised some trios for piano, violin and 'cello in which Mr. T. R. Ford, Mr. R. H. Pearson' and Mr. Bond took part, and two interesting violin solos, well played, by Mr. rt. H. Pearson. Some excellent songs were contributed by Miss M. W. Collin, Mr. J. W. Bibby and lir. J. Rabbitt, each of the vocalists being in turn, recalled for the tasteful rendering of some item which particularly pleased the audience who showed high appreciation of much of the vocal and instrumental music performed during the evening. At the next meeting of the Photographic Society Mr. Harrison of Manchester will speak on the "Retouch- ing and remedying of defective negatives." Rylands Bros. Limited—Celebration of the Centenary. Last evening a dinner was held at the Lion Hotel in celebration of the centenary of Messrs. Rylands Bros. Limited. Members of the staff and friends to the number of about seventy partook of au excellent Mr</p>
        <p>Presentation. Mr. C. H. Bickford, late district agent for the Great Northern Railway Company, was on Monday the recipient of &amp;handsome silver inkstand and penholder from the Warrington staff of the company and a few friends, on the occasion of his retirement from the service.</p>
        <p>PHOTOS 12 for 6d.—MANDEEN, 37, Sankey-st.—Advt UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. Made Himself Comfortable. - •</p>
        <p>-. A fine of 20s. and costs was on Saturday impose. on a tramp named Joseph Sullivan, who appeared before Mr. T. Halton at the. Occasional Court for a impudent offence at Fearnhead the previous evening Mr. James Mason, Fearnhead House, noticed a light in an unoccupied house belonging to him, and on making an investigation found the prisoner lying at full length on the floor. He had broken clown some drawers and cupboards, and had kindled a big fire with them. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC IS the KING oF TONICS. 63, SA.NKEY-STREET. Virarrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Butchers' Ball. Success attended the annual ball in connection with the Warrington Butchers' Association,which was held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening. Excel- lent arrangements had been made by a committee, and the affair proved highly enjoyable, about 200 persons being present. The duties of MO's were ably dis- charged by Messrs. H. Mather and Lashford. The band which the late Mr. Percy Randall formerly conducted,provided the music, Mr. G. Anderton,being at the piano. The catering was in the hands of the Warrington Cafe Company, and gave complete satis- faction. FOR GLASS AND CIILNA of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>St. John's Literary and Social Society. One of the most successful and numerously attended social gatherings ever promoted by this society was held in tha schoolroom , John-street, on Tuesday even- ing last the chair being occupied by the hon. President, the Rev. J. L. Milne, B,D. The evening's programme opened with a song by Miss Bather, a youthful vocalist who acquitted herself well. Songs were also sung by Mrs. Prentice and Mr. Hunter,while Miss Green contributed a recitation, Miss Dewar a , reading, and Mr. E. Sanders to highly amusing humorous sketches. The Misses Chesworth favoured the company with some excellent instrumental selec- tions for which they were heartily encored, as was also Miss Wright, Dumfries, for her fine rendering of Scottish airs on the violin. baring an interval in the programme tea, with accompanying good things, was handed round, and later in the evening parlour games were indulged in, all present thoroughly enjoying themselves. PLTBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE &amp;OCR FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Presentations at St. Clement's Mission. A successful concert in aid of St. Clement's Church Lads' brigade was held in the mission, Bank street, on Tuesday evening. There was a largo attendance and an interesting and varied programme was submitted in a manner which gave the greatest pleasure. The artistes were : Miss Mabel Henshall, Miss Whittle, Miss Adams, Mrs. Jackson Holmes, Miss White, Mr. J. Rabbitt, Mr. T. Poole, and Mr. F. M. Darby, Mus. Bac., all of whom acquitted themselves most creditably. During the evening , the Rev. A. S. Cameron, (curate-in-charge), who will shortly be leaving Warrington for Weston, Spalding, was the recipient of a handsome solid silver inkstand, suitably inscribed, which had been subscribed for by the officers and lads of the brigade. Opportunity was taken to hand over merit medals, gained by three of the boys, and to present Sergeant- Instructor Sweeney with a Gladstone bag and case of carvers.</p>
        <p>Workhouse Inmates Entertained. The inmates of the Workhouse were entertained at a concert provided by local artistes on Saturday evening, the affair being a great success. A mixed quartette party, consisting of the Misses L. and M. I, Davies, Mr. W. A. Clay, and Mr. A. Griffiths, visited I the hospital wards and rendered a number of selec- tions and solos to the delight of the patients. They then repaired to the dining hall where the inmates were assembled and where in the meanwhile Mr. Grounds, the well-known local humorist, had given humorous songs, and Mr. Forster had contributed several clever exhibitions of his skill in " sleight of hand" tricks. Mr. Griffiths opened the musical pro- gramme with a feeling rendering of "River of Dart." Miss/Lucy Davies followed with the song "On the Zuyder Zee," and later with the " Toreador" (the chorus being taken up by the quartette) for which she received an enthusiastic recall. "Longshoreman Billy" was given with fine spirit by Mr. W. A. Clay. Mr. Grounds submitted for a second item the sketch " Sloshton-on-Sea," and Mr. Forster caused much merriment by his demonstration of making "Hare soup." The programme was brought to a close by tge quartette rendering in a highly finished manner the song cycle "Flora's Holiday" (H. Lane Wilson). The duties of accompanist were discharged by Mrs. M. Davies. The proceedings closed with a very hearty vote of thanks to the artistes which was moved by one of the aged inmates.</p>
        <p>College of Preceptors. At the recent examination held in Warringtou in connection with the above examination, the following pupils of the Misses Partington' High School, 23, Wilson-Patten-street, were successful in the Junior Form : Annie Kenyon, Gertrude Wilson, Emily Browne, Elsie Bennett, Lily Rercival, Florence Orrniston, Annie Hignett and Dorothea Bracecamp.</p>
        <p>Cairo Street Literary and Debating Society. At a meeting; of the above Society on Tuesday evening, at cvhich the Rev, C. Harvey Cook occupied the chair, the members discussed some of the contents of the local newspapers. National Defence was one of the subjects touched upon, and led to an interesting discussion on discipline. References were also made to the numerous advertisements of money-lenders appearing in the local newspapers, and to the applica- tions of publicans for billiard licenses. PHOTOS 12 for 6d.--MAtionv, 37, Sankey-st.—Advt. Cheshire Fox on a Roof.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the Cheshire Hounds met at Waver- ton, near Chester, after ten day's idleness owing to frost. A small field included the Duke' and Duchess of Westminster, Lord Cholmondeley, Mr. George Cornwallis West, and Lady Lettice Cholmondeley, Saighton Gorge was drawn successfully, and after running up Eaton drives the fox turned left-handed and was hunted slowly into Sir Watkin Wynn's country, hounds losing him near Alderley. Handley yielded another fox, which ran fast to Crow's Nest and along the canal towards Waverton, bat doubled back to Handley and was lost. It was an exciting chase and lasted fully sixty minutes. A third fox was started in Waverton Gorse and led hounds to Cotton, where he hung for some time. He then provided the field with another run to Waverton and back. Cross- ing the brook he headed for Tarvin, but broke his journey at a farm. A search of twenty minutes resulted in his being found hiding on the roof of the dairy and hounds quickly dispatched him. FIRE GRATES of every description.—l. S. GAR LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, &amp; 55, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt. Parish Church Choristers' Association.</p>
        <p>Tue 20th annual gathering and dinner was held at the Lion Hotel on Friday last, under the presidency of Mr. Frederick Howard, when up- wards of 50 members and friends sat down to a well served dinner provided by the host Mr. John Lee and done fall justice to by those present. After dinner the usual business of the Associulon was transacted. I The reports of the treasurer and secretaries were read and adopted shewing a steady increase in finances and membership. Mr. Alfred Percival (one of H.M. inspectors of schools) was elected' president for the ensuing year. Mr. .J. H. Clarke was re-elected treasurer, and Messrs. F. J. Dell and Pt. T. Harding were re-elected secretaries. The committee and also several new members were elected, after which loyal toasts, The Church, &amp;c., were duly honoured. A high-class musical programme was gone through and enjoyed by all present, and one of the most successful gatherings of the Association was brought to a close at 12 o'clock Amongst those present were Messrs. C. E. Rowley, J. Scotson (Manchester), Alder- man Evans, J. •Dewhurst, Councillor Hulse, F. M. Darby, F.C.0., Has. Bac., and W. H. J. Jenkins.</p>
        <p>repast served by Mr. Lee. r.J. Paul Rylands pre- sided. After dinner the following toasts were honoured : " The King," proposed by the Chairman; " The Queen, Prince of Wales, and the rest of the Royal family," proposed by the Chairman; " The At the meeting of the Social Service League last firm," proposed by Mr. John J. Bleckly and responded evening Mr. E. England submitted an interesting paper to by Mr. W. Peter Rylands ' • "The Chairman,': pro- on the subject "Is it the duty of the pulpit to deal with posed by Mr. W. Henry Bleckly, and responded to by political questions?" It was, he said, the opinion of Mr. J. Paul Rylands ; " The staff," proposed by Mr. many people that political questions had no W. Peter Rylands and responded to by Mr. H. H. Place in the pulpit. So widespread was that view that of fh his hearers, geraeraecrhse, raapl:or?isteicaheil what question, said tt the vieww as Field and Mr. J. J. Nield ; " The London office," pro- posed by Mr. J. Paul Rylands and responded to by forgetting his "high calling." It mini be remora- Mr.C.Aubrey Tatham; "The visitors,"proposed by Mr. bored that Government was a divine institution Thomas J. Hulse, and responded to by Mr. J. W. essential to the improvement of our nature, Bindloss. A capital musical programme was sub- the shield of life and property, the refuge mitted, songs being rendered by Mr. T. Humphreys, of the weak and oppressed. Government was in- Mr. W. 'Moores, and Mr. T. Reddish. Mr. T. Taylor stituted with the single end of benefltting the individual pur- gave a pianoforte solo, and Mr. J. Stanley an oboe posesf!'ndwho7 perverted or ro otherrt objects,—to h spirit—weects,— were t o solo. Humorous songs and sketches by Mr. William navitarTcaes authorised and even bound to mikeysuch opposition as Whittle were well received, was suited to restore it to its proper end. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL Mr. England contended that there could be no WATERS.—Advt. separation between the Christian religion and so- -1.0.G.T. Annual Juvenile Treat. called politics, for in his opinion they were part and parcel of each other. No religion could be worth On Saturday last the members of the Young Mission anything unless it dealt with every question that Temple held their annual treat and Christmas tree in I ; affected the life and welfare of the individual. the Mersey-street Mission Room under the direction He felt it was the duty of the pulpit to take of the superintendent Miss Ada Littlewood. At four lin hand every question of the day, and he o'clock the adult members and parents of the children cited instances in which under the influence of the sat down to a well spread table and after partaking preachers in the pulpit great improvement: oiiitn the of the (rood things made room for the children. At also He lfe of d tibhea t Fteowpalse had been the duty o thbermihsttianb pulpit to 5.30 about SO boys and girls partook of cakes and tea denounce war if the preachers were to be the expounders and spent the evening in various kinds of amuse- ,of the truths as inculcated by their leader who was the meets, Mr. P. Smith (Winwick) giving some splendid Prince of the Peace. selections on the gramophone. During the evening i An interesting discussion followed. about 40 of the children received from the hands of Mr. F. Merlin book prizes as rewards for their good conduct and regular attendance durng the / past year, many of these books being the gift of Mr. A. E. Eccles,Chorley to whom thanks were given. The event of the evening was relieving the Christmas tree , of its load and amid much amusement each child ; received some token of the good-will of Santa-Claus Oranges and nuts then being distributed the children I were dismissed while the adult members and friends remained and finished up the evening with games, 87. c. Thanks are duo to the superintendent Miss A. Little- : wood and the following for the admirable arrange- ments, Mrs. Brandwood, Mrs. Hughes, Misses E. Parker, E. Littlewood, N. Bradbury, L. Brandwood. G. E. and J. Hughes, Messrs. J. Brandwood, C. J. Sheath, C. Smith, T. Hughes, W. Jackson and P. Bate. This is one of the most successful treats ever held in he annuals of the Young Mission Temple. •</p>
        <p>MUNE</p>
