      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 28, 1905</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>A gentleman, signing himself" Pedestrian,"—although it is certain that no one would have judged him from his letter to be a motorist—has written to a Manchester paper to observe that a speed limit "ought to be adopted at once." "Pedestrian" is just a trifle behind the times with his thoughtful suggestion. Prior to 1896 there was a speed limit of four miles per hour, flx9d by law for all mechanically-propelled traffic on the highways. In August of that year of blessed memory an Act was passed raising the limit to twelve miles per hour, whilst the Motor Car Act of 1903 per- mits twenty miles per hour as the maximum to be attained upon the high roads under any circumstances</p>
        <p>"Pedestrian "thinks the plan he suggests would be good protective and a preventive of danger. He may be surprised to learn that the best protection for the public would be the abolition of the speed limit entirely. Leaving motorists to be dealt with, as stringently as possible when the case against them had been clearly proved, by that section in the present Act which has reference to driving recklessly or to the danger of the public. The man who is not a motorist finds some difficulty in understanding that whilst in the hands of a capable driver a motor car may be driven at twenty five or thirty miles an hour on a straight country road with- out risk, it might, when steered by a novice or a bad driver, prove an absolute danger to the public when proceeding at five miles an hour. We are pleased to note, at the time of writing. the 12 h.p. Siddelev has covered nearly 3,000 of the pro- posed 5,000 miles trial, and is still going strong. But when are some of the manufacturers going to take a leaf from Mr. Moffot Ford's book and try for a real. non-stop run, during which neither engine nor road. wheels shall be brought to a standstill throughout the whole of the trip?</p>
        <p>A motor 'bus is also now undergoing an olficial relia- bility trial, and this should certainly direct public attention to the intense advantages possessed by this type of vehicle when compared with its rival, the elec- talc tram. For the motor 'bus no alteration of existing roads is necessary, whilst the fact that it can be steered as the tram cannot be, is another very great point in its favour. Moreover, when a tram breaks down, the whole of that line speedily becomes blocked with other cars following on behind ; but a motor 'bus that is tem- porarily rendered hors as combat can be quickly dragged out of the way of traffic.</p>
        <p>So far as we have progressed at present, the French Club show no signs of giving way in the matter of running the Grand Prix International at the same time and over the same course as the Gordon-Bennett. England and Germany have entered a strong protest and there is some talk of withdrawal should the Auto- mobile Club of France persist in the proposed scheme. It is rather odd that se little has been heard from Mr. James Gordon-Bennett with regard to the matter, but evidently he has no intention of becoming involved in an International dispute.</p>
        <p>It seems to be a great pity that the small twin- cylinder car, or volturette, is not receiving a great deal of attention from the French manufacturers just now, for undoubtedly this type is the car for the immedi- ate future and it is with this that the most profit will be made. Still the old adage that it is an ill wind which blows no one any good very actly applies in the circum- stances, for many of our English manufactures have given especial consideration to the light car lately, so it may be that we shall reap the benefit of France's oversight in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PULPIT AND POLITICS.</head>
        <p>THE PULPIT AND POLITICS.</p>
        <p>Paper at the Social Service League Meeting.</p>
        <p>FRENZIED FINANCE.</p>
        <p>A WORD TO PROTECTIONISTS.</p>
        <p>[Br MR. H. WiimiAm.]</p>
        <p>Six months ago there commenced in one of the American magazines a series of articles entitled "Frenzied Finance." The writer, Mr. Thomas Lawson of Boston, is one who for a number of years has had close and,intimate intercourse with the great American Trusts. As a Wall-street Financial Magnate he has taken a hand in the great financial gambles and speaks with a thorough knowledge of the doings of the greatest moneyed combination the world has ever seen, or is likely to see—the Standard Oil Company. This company stands for a great deal more ' than may appear to the uninitiated. Oil is but one of ' the very many money-making enterprises it controls. Railroads, mining of all descriptions, steel and iron, grain and everything else wherein large investments are called for, and shares, stocks &amp;c. give to men that peculiar dollar-getting type of brains that enables them to say "the earth is mine and the fulness thereof." He names men who are members of colossal corporations in which unmeasured success and continued immunity from punishment have bred an insolent disregard of law, of common morality, and Apublic and private right, together ,with a grim determination to hold on to, at all hazards, the great possessions they have gulped or captured. While naming these men he insists that there is nothing personal in the work he is doing. It is the " system "he is fighting, for, as he says, if these men had never been born the same good and evil would to-day exist. It is the same "system," Lawson says that has taken from millions of our people billions of dollars and given them over to a score or two of men with power to use them and enjoy them as absolutely as though these millions had been earned dollar by dollar by the labour of their bodies and minds. So the men are merely individuals ; the " system" is the thing at fault, and it is the " system" that must be destroyed. " If this' system 'is allowed to continue it surely will in time, short time, destroy the nation by precipitating fratricidal war." Mr. Lawson's base of operation is directed towards what is known as the "Amalgamated Copper," and its thousand and one octopus tentacles that draw every- thing within sight into its rapacious jaws. He says— " The enormous losses, millions upon millions—to my personal knowledge over a hundred millions of dollars —which were made because of Almalgamated ; the large number of suicides—to my personal knowledge over thirty—which were directly caused by Almolga- mated; the large number of previously reputed citizens who were made prison convicts—to my personal know- ledge over twenty—directly because of Amalgamated were caused by acts of this system." Here Mr. Law- son gives the names of a number of men, who stand before the public as the chief controlling power, of the Standard Oil Company, and yet these men, Lawson goes on to say, "to whose eyes I have seen come the tears for others sufferings, and whose voices I have heard grow husky in recounting the woes of their less fortunate brothers—these men under the spell of the brutal code of modern dollar-making are converted into beasts of prey, and put to shame the denizens of the deep which devour their kind that they may live." lii the foreword Mr. Lawsoi gives to his readers, and of which I am now giving a resume to the readers of the " Examiner," he goes on to say " I shall describe such parts of the general financial structure as will place my readers, especi- ally those unfamiliar with its more complicated con- ditions, in a mental state to comprehend the methods by which the sayings they think are safely guarded in the banks, trust and insurance contpanies, are so manipulated by the votaries of frenzied finance as to be in constant jeopardy. I will show them that while the press, the books, the stump and our halls of statesmanship are full to overflowing with the whys, wherefores 1 and what-note of " tariff," "currency, " silver," " gold," and "labour," while our market systems are perfected educational machines for dis- seminating accurate statistics about the necessaries and luxuries of life, the water and land carriers, real estate and other material things which the people have been taught to believe are the only things that vitally affect their savings ; that while they imagine ' they understand the system by which speculation and investments are controlled and worked, and that the causes and effects of this system are at all times gat- at-able by them through their bankers and their brokers, there is a tangible, complicated, yet simple trick of financial legerdemain, operated twenty-four hours each day in the year, and which the press, the books, the politicians and the statesmen never touch upon—a trick by means of which the savings of the people and the public funds of the Government whether in the national banks, savings-banks, trust o' insurance companies, are always at the absolutr service and mercy of the votaries of frenzie e finance." _</p>
        <p>While Mr. Lawson knows that there will be running through the land, contemporaneously with his story, actions at law which will compel him to be unusually alert in " hewing to the exact line of facts" his mind is thoroughly made up, let what will happen, to make the picture so clear that the'' people shall be able to understand it. He anticipates no I,tw busines3 bat knows there is a possibility of guns being around. He gives bits out of the nation's history, in which within a', few days of the national election of 18D1 hurry-up call for additional funds to the extent of one million pounds was so promptly tnet as to overturn the people in, five States and thereby preserve the destinies of the Republic an party. Tais statemalt has a direct bearing up en the tariff aal protective question, as the Republican stands for all that pro- tection meaus. A commission of the Merchant Marine has given in a report touching the Merchant Shipping question of the United States. The minority says : —" We cannot help but see the 'trail of the serpent' ' that has been over all other bills on this subject." This serpent is what Lawson is after, Merely saying that this story shall he continued hereafter. Let me here say that I think there is a lesson to be drawn from it applicable to the workingman and his vote. Commenting on the material differenoe between the cost of construction in American and foreign ship• yards, the minority declares that the hearings con- ducted by the conmission showed that the greater cost of labour in the American yards is largely over- come by the greater efficiency of the American artisan and the use of improved tools. The greatest impedd ment to cheaper ships in American yards is declared by the minority t.e be "the unnecessary and exorbitant tariff imposed upon steel and iron products." The minority says further : "It has been conclusively shown by testimony before this-commission that materials which enter into the construction of ships are sold by our factories and laid down in foreign shipyards for a price fir below that charged at the mills to oar own pe )pla —a con- dition that would not exist but for the unjust pro- visions of our tariff laws."</p>
        <p>Mr. Poultney Bigelow, historian and traveller and colonial professor of the Boston University, speaking on the question of Protection says,—" Commerce requires freedom quite as much as a great university or religion does. We have stifled our foreign Com- merce by so-called protection and the sooner we let in a little fresh air the sooner will we kill a groat deal of the poisonous weeds which have flourished under a system that has been very good to tho trusts, bat not of benefit to the people at large, in spite of all the recent stump oratory to the contrary." " From the foundation of the thirteen colonies we have beeh a nation of sailors ani shipbuilders —aid we ask no artificial protection for this industry. We only ask the nrotectionist octopus to withdraw some of his tentacles from our throats ;we beg only for a fair field and no favor." .</p>
        <p>The " protectionist octopus "is what Lawson is after—the multi-millionaire breeding system controlled by the anarchists of Wall Street. H. WHITHAIL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>775-14th Street, San Francisco. January 7th, 1905.</head>
        <p>775-14th Street, San Francisco. January 7th, 1905.</p>
        <p>A mysterious affair is reported in the Knutsford dis- trict. A valuable horselbelocging to Mr. H. Pemberton was left in a field near Northwich Road, and neat morn- ing the animal was found seriously injurnd in the road- way, and it afterwards died. Broken glass was strewn about the roadway, which suggested that the vehicle which killed the horse was either a motor or a cab. Inquiries have been made, but no definite information can be obtained.</p>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT AT WIDNES. At Widnes Hospital on Wednesday afternoon, before Mr. S. Brighouse, the jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death" in the case of Alfred Ernest Mercer (18), of 15, Davis-street, who was killed whilst drawing up coal waggons at the depot of Mr. Thomas Cooper, coal merchant, of Ditton-road. On Monday the deceased and his employer were engaged palling waggons into position, when he was badly crushed between the buffers. The ambulance was quickly in atteadance, but on the way to the hospital he expired. Mr. Dale solicitor, who represented Mr. Cooper, expressed his sympathy for the parents.</p>
      </div>
