      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>LOCAL OBITUARY.</head>
        <p>LOCAL OBITUARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry milling. Warrington has lost another of its most honoured townsmen by the death of Mr. Henry Milling, head of the firm of Henry Milling and Company, Limited., wholesale and retail grocers, Horsemarket street, who passed away at his residence Summerville, Latchford at noon on Monday. The deceased gentleman, who was sixty seven years of age, had not enjoyed the best of health for some years. About two months ago he became seriously ill and Dr. Fox attended him. Heart and lung trouble was the cause of death. Born at Peel Hall, Kingsley, Frodsham, the late Mr. Milling had throughout his business career been connected with the grocery trade in Warrington. In 1351 he entered the service of the late Alderman Peter Smith who then occupied premises at Market Gate. He soon gave evidence of his ability and aptitude and about forty-sir years ago he became a partner with Mr. Smith, the firm being styled " Smith and Milling." In 1879 Mr. Milling took entire control of the firm and transferred it to the present largo premises in Horsernarket-street Eventually a limited company was formed under the title of Henry Milling and Co. Ltd., and the fact that it carries on such an extensive business in Warrington and district is a tribute to the energy with which Mr. Milling devoted himself to the management. Latterly delicate health had prevented him from taking an active interest in the concern. He was of a somewhat retiring disposi- tion and the only period during which he shared in the management of town's affairs was from 1876 to 1879 when he served as a member of the Town Council. He was a churchman and formerly was prominently associated with St. James's,Latchford, having held the office of warden. Of late years he had attended St. Wilfred's Church, Grappenhall. The news of his death was received with deep regret by the people of Warrington, for his many good qualities had gained for him the respect of a wide circle. He leaves a widow, three sons, and a daughter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>FIINEP.AL.</head>
        <p>THE FIINEP.AL.</p>
        <p>- - The funeral took place in the quiet churchyard at Grappenhall on Thursday afternoon in the presence of a large gathering. The officiating clergy were the Rev. R. Greenall 1 (Rector of Grappenhall) and the Rev. S, Wilkinson (vicar of Timperlev). The chief mourners were :—Mrs. Milling, Mrs. H. G. Roberts (daughter), Messrs. Henry, Frank, and Torn Milling, (sons), Mr. H. G. Roberts (son-in-law), Mr. James Hatton (brother-in- law), Messrs. F. Gandy and H. Moss (nephews), Dr. Bowden, Mr. Robert Smith, (Liverpool), Mr. John Dodson (Liverpool). Others 'present were :—lessrs. Wallop, Horton, and Lowe (directors of H. Milling arid Co. Ltd.), Mr. H. Roberts, Mr. R. M. Roberts, Nurse Dakin (Manchester), Dr. Fox, Mr. T. J. Ridgway, the Rev. E. E. Farrer, and numerous friends. Carriages were sent by Mr. F. Monks, Mrs. Pickmere (Thelwall), Mr. W. Fletcher (Whitley), Mr. John Fairclough (Latchford), and others. By request there were no wreaths, but a beautiful cross from the family was placed on the coffin.</p>
        <p>Mr. Percy Randall. A painful sensation was created in Warrington on Monday when it became known that Mr. Percy Randall professor of music, of 7, Winmarleigh-street, had died very suddenly. With the exception that he complained of slight pain in the head, the deceased gentleman was appareatly in his usual health and spirits on Sunday, land at the morning and evening services at Holy Trinity 1 Church he presided at the organ with his accustomed skill. On awakening at eight o'clock on Monday morn- ing he chatted cheerfully with his wife, but suddenly she was alarmed to see him gasping for breath. The members of the family ware quickly summoned, and in la fevr minutes he passed away, the cause of death being cerebial hemorrhage. He was forty-four veers of age It is about twenty-five years since the deceased gentleman, who was a native of Pontypool, came to reside in Warrington. His real name, it may be stated, WAS William Thomas Waite, that of Percy Randall being assumed for professional purposes. He was a very accomplished musician, the instruments on which he most frequently performed being the piano and organ. On many occasions he had delighted social gatherings with vocal items, and he was particularly happy when giving pieces in the humorous vein. Soon after coming to Warrington he took up the duties of rhusical conductor at the Theatre Royal, and also officiated as organist at St. Ann's Church. On yelinquishing the latter post about nine years ago he was appointed organist at Holy Trinity Church. He brought tact and ability to bear on his work, his genial disposition not less than his devotion to duty gaining the esteem of the clergy, choir and congregation. The annual musical service on behalf of the choir fund owed its inception to him, and he went to no little pains in arranging acceptable programmes. He had organised a capital band which during the season was always much in evidence at balls and similar functions. Mr. Randall had on behalf of his band accepted an engagement to play at the ball in connectien with the Butchers' Association, held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening. His services were readily placed at the disposal of workers in charitable causes, and at the recent treat to the aged people in Parr Hall he delighted the assembly with selections on the organ. As a com- poser he achieved some success, several of his dance pteees, which were dedicated to Warringtoaians, being very popular. In addition to training the choir of Holy Trinity Church he undertook the tuition of Hill Cliff Baptist choir, and his valuable efforts called forth the warmest praise. By his death at a comparatively early age many Warringtonians stiffer the loss of an esteemed friend, and wide and genuine is the regret which has been occasioned. With the widow and family (two suns and a daughter) the deepest sympathy is expressed. THE FUNER AL. "LThere was a striking demonstration of respect at the funeral which took place at the Cemetery on Thursday afternoon. The first part of the service was conducted in Holy Trinity Church, a very large congregation assembling. The full choir was in attendance, and Mr. T. D. Amos presided At the organ The 39th Psalm was chanted, and afterwards the ROT. N. Inglis (cunitte of Winwick) and! formerly of Holy Trinity) read the lesson. The congregation then joined in singing "Christ will gather in His own "and the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton read the {prayers from the burial office. The Rev. EL R. Wild also took part in the service. The concluding hymn was "Art thou weary ? " and as the mourners left the church the organist feelingly played "0 rest in the Lord." The cortege then proceeded to the Cemetery where the last rites were performed by the Rev. E.C.E. Carleton. The choir attended and sang the hymn" Now the labourer's task is 'oar." The 1 chief mourners were—Frederick and 13rtram (sons), Messrs. %V. H. Harrison, j. E. Harrison and J. G. Harrison (brothers-in-law), Mr. H. F. Steele, Bradford ' (nephew), Mr. James Harrison, Altrincham and Mr. James Harrison, jun., Warrington (cousins). Amongst those present were : —The Revs. NV. Bracecamp and James Davies, Messrs. .J. H. Halton and T. Welsbv (wardens at Holy Trinity Church), William Gill, G. Dykes, A. Walsh. H. W. Naylor, James Harding, 1 George Gill, and E. Rose (sidesmen), W. H. Payton Mus. Bac., (organist at Wycliffe), 17. M. Darby, Mrs. Bac., (Parish Church), Frank Hickman (Bold-street), E. A. Padmore (St. Ann's), B. Wadsworth (Bewsey-road), and J. Eckersley (St. Mary's, Buttermarket-street), Alderman Bennett, Dr. Joseph, Messrs. R. H. Pearson, J. Hankinson, Herbert Woods, A. Jolley (representing the Aged People's Treat Committee). Peers Brimelow, Councillor W. J. Forshaw. Councillor Ashcroft, Francis J. Dell, Frank Turner, George Parr, George Hoult, Wm. Monks (Moore), W. Scotson, W. S. Howard, F. Taylor, T. Stringer, A. Walsh, G. .Watkins, E. Coates, J. Critchley, Pickles, S. Barlow, W. Holbrook (Conserva- tive Association). Peter Leigh. AV. Walker, F. G. Whittle. G. H. Holford, Arthur Wright (Padgate), and W. NV. Whittle.</p>
        <p>The coffin bore the inscription :—" William Thomas Waite died January 23rd, 1905. Aged 41 years." Wreaths were placed over the grave from the following: —His sorrowing wife and children, his sorrowing father, Holy Trinity Choir, Rev. and Mrs. E. C. E. Carleton, Holy Trinity Young Men's Associa- tion, Mr. Herbert Woods, Mr. and Mrs. Forshaw and family, Dr. and Mrs. Shores, Dr. Joseph and family, Miss Clarke (a pupil). Mr. B. L. Pierpoint and family. "Joe and Winnie," Mr. and Mrs. 4 Huggett, L. and E. Pooley, Mr. James Harding and family, Mr. and Mrs. %V. Monks and family, Mr. and Mrs. Watkins Mr. and Mrs. Pilkington and family, Mr. and Mrs. Dunbabbin and Amy. Mr. and Mrs. B. Wads- worth, Mr. and Mrs. H. Ferguson and family, Miss Mathers, Mr. and Mrs. John Swinton, Mrs. Joseph Harrison and Miss Atkinson, Mrs. Loy and family, Mr. and Mrs. John Hankinson, Mr.and Mrs. Thomas Welsby, Mr. W. 11., Mrs. and Miss Arline Harrison, Lilian Lowe and John Dean, Mr. and Mrs. C. H. Honit, Ernest L. Warburton and Ernest Hall, Mr. and Mrs. A. Walsh, Vernon Ashton, Lymm (a pupil), Mr. and Mrs. S. G. Davey and family, "John and Jennie," Misses and Mr. T. L. Woods, J. H. Halton and A. Leigh, Mr. and Mrs. T. Hunt and family, Mr. and Mrs. George Gill, "Annie and Ralph," Mr. and Mrs. H. Sutton, Mr. and Mrs. G. A. Ward. Miss Robinson, Mrs. Alice A. Waite (Westbury Park), Bessie and Edith Forshaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Mr Thomas Grooves.</head>
        <p>Mr Thomas Grooves.</p>
        <p>Many Warrington people will regret to hear of the death of Mr. Thomas Greaves, a former well-known inhabitant of the town, who passed away at his resid- ence, Sandholm, Marine Terrace, Waterloo, on Monday at the age of sixty-eight years. The aeceasod gentle- man came to Warrington from Liverpool twenty-three years ago and carried on the business of a pawnbroker in Church street. He may be said to have been practic- ally the first tradesman to help forward the Bridge. street improvement. He acquired a shop in that thoroughfare but ultimately it was pulled down and Mr. Greaves erected the commodious premises known as "The Tower" which stood considerably further back and greatly improved the appearance of the street. The shops was opened about eighteen years ago, and continued in Mr. Greave's occupation until about the time of his departure fromWarrington—two</p>
        <p>years ago—when Messrs, J. M. Greenhalgh and Col, acquired it. During the latter period of his residence in Warrington Mr. Greaves lived in Salisbury-street. He suffered a severe bereavement by the death of his wife and he left Warrington soon afterwards. He never took any active part in political affairs, although on one occasion he was invited by the Liberal Association to contest St, Austins ward for municpal honours. He could not, however, see his way to stand. In religion he was a Roman Catholic, and attended St. Mary's Church, Buttermarket-street. His many charitable acts caused him to be highly esteemed. An honour was conferred upon him by the members of his trade when they elected him to the office of chairman of the Liverpool Pawn- brokers' Association, the duties of which he discharged with ability. At one time he had several establish- ments in Liverpool. He leaves a son and a daughter with whom much sympathy is felt. The funeral took place on Wednesday at the church of St. Peter and St. Paul, Great Crosby, the last rites being preached by a requiem Mass.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. Stephen Dean. On Monday afternoon the funeral of Mr. Stephen Dean, whose death we announced in our last Saturday's issue, took place. The cortege left the deceased's residence, The Poplars, Orford, and proceeded to the Padgate Wesleyan Church where the first portion of the funeral service was held. The Rev. .T. Manning who officiated gave a very appropriate and sympathetic address on the Christian life of the deceased. Prior to leaving the church, the organist (Mr. W. Kenyon) played the Dead March in "Saul." The funeral then proceeded to the Warrington Cemetery where the deceased was borne to his last resting place by Messrs. J. Gleave, S. Dawson, D. Hughes and J. Gilbert. The mourners were Mrs. bean (widow), Mr. Henry Dean, Thomas H. Dean, William P. Dean (spas), Mr. Joseph Dean (of Ilkley), Mr. Barnard Dean (of Blackburn), brothers, Mrs. B. Dean (sister-in-law), Mrs. Henry Dean (daughter-in- law), Mr. and Mrs. Culshaw, Arthur Pearson, Cissie Dean. Jane Sims, Arthur Dean, Stephen Dean (grand- children), Mr. and Mrs. Charles Healey, Mr. T. S. Steel (deceased's solicitor), Mr. J. Hill, Mr. Taylor. At the graveside were Mr. William Bennett, Mr. John Bennett, Alderman A. Bennett, J.P., Mr. and Mrs. Smith, Mr. Thomas Norcott, Mr. H. Ward, Mr. J. D. Fryer, Mr.and Mrs. NV. Newton, Mr. J. Newton, Mr. T. Leech,and Mr. J. Caldwell. Many wreaths were sent amongst which were "from his loving wife and family," Mr. T.J.Dean, Mrs. and Miss Richardson, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Bennett, Mr. and Mrs. Charles Healey, Mr. and Mrs. James White, Mr. and Mrs. .J. Antrobus, Mrs. J. Houghton, Mr. and Mrs. John Bennet'. Mr. and Mrs. W. Bennett, Mrs. Harris, Mr. and Mrs. Walter Bennett. Mr. J. Antrobus had charge of the funeral arrangements</p>
      </div>
