      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SEQUEL TO A TRAP ACCIDENT. '. Unsuccessful Claim against a Motorist</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A TRAP ACCIDENT. '. Unsuccessful Claim against a Motorist</p>
        <p>at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday the hearing was resumed of the action in which James Shepherd, horse and general dealer, Bridgewater street, Lymm. sued Norman Hollinger, 355, Moss lane east. Moss Side, Manchester for .-£5-4) damages for personal injuries and damage to a pony, trap, and harness, sus- tamed through the defendant's alleged negligence in driving a motor cycle at Higher Lane, Lymm, on Sep- tember 14th, 1901. Mr. Arthur Browneappeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. Cuthbert Smith (instructed by Messrs Jeans and Son) defended. Plaintiff's case, as stated by Mr. Browne at the last hearing, was that he was driving slowly along Higher- lane, Lymni, when without any warning defendant passed on his motor cycle before the pony's head, caus- ing the animal to plunge wildly. Plaintiff was thrown to the ground, the trap was smashed, and the pony was injured. Defendant, it was further alleged, drove on for 150 yards before he was stopped by some boys who shouted that they thought Mr. Shephard was killed. Plaintiff had been practically helpless, said Mr. Browne, ever since the accident, and the consequent loss in busi- ness had been very serious. Mr. Browne now called a boy named Samuel Haven,</p>
        <p>port, son of Reginald Daveno;rt, plumber, Higher-lane- Lymm, who sMd that on the evening of the accident he land some friends were standing outside Oughtringtons Otturch. They saw Mr. Shepherd thrown out of his trap, and as they were running towards him a motor cycle passed them. Mr. Shepherd was lying uncon- scious on the ground. Gavin Cook, clerk in the estate department of Mr Dewhurst, spoke to finding the plaintiff lying on the ground. Judging from the marks which plaintiff pointed • out as having been made by the trap wheels, there was ample room for the motor to pass. Annie Adams gave evidence as to the effects of the accident upon the plaintiff. On several occasions she had had to prevent him from falling, and once he narrowly escaped falling,into the fire. For the defence, Mr. znith contended that plaintiff's version was absolutely untrue. Defendant never swerved across the road in the way described; he never left the right hand side, but simply went on in the ordinary way. On passing the trap defendant did what every careful motor cyclist did in passing horses, and" throttled" the engine down. The effect was that a noise, less than the ordin-try explosion, was made. Before the defendant overtook the plaintiff, the pony was lively, but when he had passed it became livelier still. Plaintiff commenced " sawing" at its head and the animal went from side to side of the road and began to kick. it was in that way, defendant con- tended, that plaintiff was thrown out. The pony turned a complete somersault. Plaintiff's evidence, said Mr. Smith, was contradicted by every witness he called. Norman Hollinger,the defendant, then gm e evidence. On the dite mentioned he was riding from Penketh to Manchester and on passing through Lymin he was going at the sate of about 10 or 12 miles an hour. The width of the roadway from kerb to kerb was 31 feet. Witness passed the trap in the ordinary way, and did not blow his horn because there was plenty of room to pass. In passing a horse the blowing of the horn had a tendency to ; make a horse more restive than not doing so. When he overtook plaintiff witness had about three feet of room. He did not rush before the pony's head, neither did he strike it with his shoulder. If witness had attempted to cross the road in the manner des- cribed by the plaintiff he would have gone into the kerbstone. The bicycle did not emit any peculiar noise beyond the ordinary exhaust. When he had passed witness shut off some gas which minimised the noise. When he had gonsa short distance he heard a crash, and stopped his machine to sea what had happened. In reply to Mr./Browne witness said the horse was not kicking when he passed, but afterwards it com- menced to gallop. The reason witness went back was to see if he could render any assistance. He admitted that he offered £4 to the plaintiff,though he denied any liability, but it was refused. • By Mr. Smith: The action of shutting off the exhaust made no noise. George Frank Turner, a cyclist, stated that when the defendant passed the plaintiff he (witness) could have ridden between the space. The driver of the mo,or did nothing wrong. In reply to !qr. Browne witness said he could not say what caused the pony to plunge.. Mrs. Fogg, Higher-lane, Lamm, described the acci- dent. No one was to blame for it but the defendant' who began to get excite-land pull the pony about. She denied having told an entirely different story to one of Mr. Browne's clerks. - _</p>
        <p>His Honour said his opinion was that the affair was an accident, and he did not think either party was res- ponsible. He believed Shephard had told what he believed to be the truth, and defendant had done the same. Plaintiff, however, had not established his case. He was very sorry plaintiff had met with the accident, , and he thought he was a little unwise in not coming to some arrangement with the defendant who, although denying liability, was willing to make a small payment. Mr. Browne : We wrote, without prejudice, offering to accept RIO. His Honour gave judgment for the defendant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NORTH DORSET ELECTION.</head>
        <p>NORTH DORSET ELECTION.</p>
        <p>GREAT LIBERAL TRIUMPH.</p>
        <p>The result of Thursday's polling in North Dorset was made known yesterday morning at Sturminster Newton, as follows Mr. A. NV. Wills (L) Sir Randolf Baker (C)</p>
