      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>INFLUENZA INCREASING NEWTON CRICKET, BOWLING, AND TENNIS CLUB. MRS. SPETTIGUE. Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING NEWTON CRICKET, BOWLING, AND TENNIS CLUB. MRS. SPETTIGUE. Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the country. The visitation of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainly on the increase. The origin The annual meeting of the above club was held at the of the disease or rather the circi ntstances which bring Pied Bull Hotel, Newton-le-Willows, on Thursday night, it about is as much a mystery as ever. You cannot Mr. C. R. Pilkington presiding over a large attendance trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervoas of members. Alter last year's minutes had been taken System to a greater extent than almost any other as read, the balance sheet was submitted, the receipts disease and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia for the year, including a balance of £64 85.10 d. brought strikes the man who imagines he has only a mild form forward, were £2Bl Os. Bd., and the expenditure, after a of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual, donation of £5O to the new pavilion fund had been It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence of Chest added, £222 10s. Id., leaving a balance in hand on the and Lung Complaints must be ascribed to the baneful se son's work of £3B 10s. 7d. The balance sheet was influence of Influenza, whose after-effects so frequently adopted. It was decided to ask the following gentle- take this form. It was a bad day for this Country men to act as patrons :—Lord Newton, T. J. Gillespie, whea this malignant scourge first visited our shores. Esq., J.P., A. C. McCorquodale, Esq., G. F. McCorquo- Year after year it has never since failed to revive dale, Esq., Colonel it. Pilkington, M.P., and R. Stone; at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Esq., J.P. Having observed its ravages, and its dreadful effects Mr. C. R. Pilkington was re-elected president, and upon the constitution on the occasion of its former Messrs. J. Fairclough, J.P., H. J. Longton, W. Longton, visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much J. Simpson, L. E, Pilkington and Dr. H. E.