      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>BATCHELORS' BALL AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual ball run by a number of local bachelors was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown on Wednesday. The hall was tastefully decorated by Mr. E. Evans and about GO couples danced to the strains of Mr. J. Walton's band. A programme comprising twenty-fourdances was snccessfully negotiated the proceedings terminating at about 4 o'clock': The arrangements were carried oit by a committee consisting of Messrs. .1. Eaverty,B. Southern C. Poi ter, W. Strange, E. Walker, C. A. Owen, H. Owen, W. Naylor, J. Quirk, V. Vardey, H. Walton and A. It. Clegg (Secretary). Messrs. C. Owen and H. Walton were the M.C's. The refreshment were ably provided by Miss Stratham. EARLESTOWN GOOD TEMPLARS. The usual weekly Session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge was held in the Lodge-room Bridge-street, on Monday last, when Bro. George Clarke C.T., presided over a good attendance of members. Bro. J. Woods was unanimonsly re-elected, Lodge Deputy for the en- , suing rear; and Bros T. Woods L.D. George Clarke, E. Superintendent, T. Warburton, T. Dodd. and W. Rose were appointed Representatives to No. 4 Conven- tion for the next half year. A Committee was also appointed to arrange the Lodge programme for the new quarter. The programme for the evening " Pictorial Post Card Night " was in the hands of Bro, George Clarke and E. Newman and they had pleasure in report- ing that upwards of 48 Pictorial Cards and the letter had been received, covering a mo,t interesting variety of subjects, and conveying messages of fraternal greet- in good wishes and words of an encouraging character from members of the Order as far North As Carlisle and Newcastle-on-Tyne,to London, Portsmouth, Eastbourne and Yeovil in the South of England, and from numerous other places between interesting con- tributions were also used from brethren at Gibraltar, Tangier, North Africa and Boston Mass. The interest- ing messages were read out by Messrs. G. Clarke and E. Newman, and the picture cards were then passed round for perusal by the members, and were heartily enjoyed, Thanks were very freely accorded those responsible, for their successful efforts. The cards were afterwards dis- tributed amongst those present, who are to respond to the kindly messages received.</p>
      </div>
