      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>£2OO COMPENSATION FOR A WORKMAN.</head>
        <p>£2OO COMPENSATION FOR A WORKMAN.</p>
        <p>Award at the Warrington County Court.</p>
        <p>His Honour Judge Bowen Rowiands, KC., made an important award uuder the Workmen's Compensation Act at the Warrington County Court on Tnursday. The Warrington Wire Ropery Company Limited, carrying on business in Bowsey-road, sougnt for the redemption of an award made by his Honour for the payment of 10s. per week to one of the Company's workmen named Charles Thomas Rigby, 14, Pierpoint-street, who was injured whilst following his employment in June 1903. Mr. F. B. Smith, barrister, Liverpool (instructed by Messrs. Field, Son and Bromfield, Liverpool) appeared for the company and Mr. A. Browne, Warrington, for the respondent. Mr. Smith stated that an award was originally made for 15s. per week, but it was reduced to 10s. per week in December, 1903. Rigby was now employed by Mr. Joseph Hughes, Arpley, and Mr. Smith contended that he was able to earn more than he was at present receiv- ing. So long as he was in receipt of the present com- pensation award he had no incentive to improve his position. On behalf of the Company he offered to find Rigby employment at 3s. 63. per day, and it would not be necessary for him to go near machinery. Mr. Browne intimated that Rigby declined to go back, and po nted out that his Honour had held, in December last, that he Was not compelled to do so. Mr. Smith thereupon suggested that as they had already paid £163 to Rigby in yfeekly payments he was only entitled to such a slim a 5, would set him up in a small business, more especially as his earning powers had not been entirely taken away. Mr. Browne said the payments made up to the pre- sent had nothing whatever to do whit hts Honour's power of redemption, and read a letter from Messrs. Field, Son, and Broomfield, in which they had offered to pay £l5O and failing that they would apply for the redemption. The offer was declined by Rigby who said to him (Mr. Browne) " Tnat means six years payment of 10s. por week, and as I am only 28 years of age, at 311 should have nothing left and be minus a leg." Mr. Browne, continuing, said the offer ought to be greatly increased and quoted a case heard before Judge Stonor at Southwork County Court in which his Honour redeemed an award under the Workmen's Compensation Act at £6OO where the payment was ti a week. The employers went to the Court of Appeal and the Judge's decision was upheld. Taking the present ease, if Rigbv lived until he was 55 years of age, 10s. per week would mean over 1:700 on the actuarial basis, and this was the basis upon which Judge Stonort went. If Rigby lived to 50 years of age it would mean £5OO and even if he lived only to the age of 45, the payment should be £290 at the least after a suns had been deducted, to meet contingencies, such as prior death. His Honour said he did not agree with any strict rule being adopted in all cases, but thought each action should be decided on its merits. He had listened to the very able arguments of Mr. Smith and Mr. Browne, and he thought an award of £2OO with costs on scale e for Rigby would meet the case.</p>
        <p>- --</p>
      </div>
