      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>DRUNKEN PARENTS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>JAD CASE OF CHILD NEGLECT AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>JAD CASE OF CHILD NEGLECT AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>-4</p>
        <p>A sad case of neglect was heard at the War ington Police Court on Monday when ,iames Harris, barber, Pierpoint-street,and Mary Harris, his wife,were brought up on remand charged with neglecting their five children. —Mr. Browne prostituted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., and stated that the Bench would remember that when the prisoners appeared in court last week, the woman was decidedly drunk and they all thought that the man was strongly under, the influence of drink. He had since ascertained, however, that that was perhaps not alto- gether the case, as Harris was suffering more from loss of blood than from the effects of drink, his wife having struck him a heavy blow on the head with a hammer, causing a severe wound. lAgain on the Monday morning she had reopened the wound by striking him with a chair. Everything possible had been done by various ladies and gentlemen to try and, get prisoners to reform, but without avail. The case was one of the worst ever brought into court, and in bringing it forward, the society hoped to put a stop to the state of things which had existed in the family for two or three years now. Various kinds of cruelty had been committed on these children. They were filthy, the rooms of the house were filthy, the children bad been kicked and turned out of the house I at night only partially clothed, and kept out all night and had been taken in and fed by neighbours. There was no doubt whatever, that they had been grossly neglected.—William Smith, a voluntary missionary, P.C. Reddington and Mrs. Ward gave evidence in support of this statement.- -Inspector. Cox. N.S.P.C.C., and P.C. Sankey both deposed to visiting the house and finding defendants under the influence of drink. The kitchen was very dirty and there was a foul smell upstairs.—Asked if she had anything to say, the male prisoner said r " Well, if drink is the cause of it, I can at);) it, rather than be scandalized by cases of this sort."—TheChief Constable said the woman had been before the Court several time The Bench sent Mrs. Harris to gaol for one month without the option of aline, and fined the male prisoner 20s. and costs (or a month's imprisonnrmt in default).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>VbMilgtoll BWOUgh PAO Court'</head>
        <p>VbMilgtoll BWOUgh PAO Court'</p>
        <p>Mho? Gases.</p>
        <p>SATIIRDAY.—IIefore Mr. J. NV. Hayward (presiding), Alderman Bennett, Councillor Tinnion Dr. Fox,</p>
        <p>Messrs. R. T. Fait ciough, and A. J. Jolley. THEFT FROM A CLOTHES LlNE.—Alfrel.gdward Allen Dial-street, was charged with stealing from a clothed dine at the rear of 5, Roleston-street, on Wednesday aftrnoon a pair of men's woollen drawers, of the value or 3s. 6d ,the property ofJamesLingard.—lle was fined 10s, including costs,</p>
        <p>Most)lv.—B-efore the Mayor (presiding), Aldermen Smethurst and Francomb, Councillor Wilkinson,. Messrs. S. Wright, J. W. Hayward, H. Thornton, W Roberts, and G. Pilling. A NoToutous SHOPLIFTER.—Mary Robinson, Clara- street, was charged with having stolen two rabbits, of the value of 25.4 d„ the property of William Simpson, general dealer, Lovely-lane.--The Chief Con- stable sta,ed that the prisoner was the "celebrated" M ,re Robinson, one of the cleverest and most cunning shoplifters the district htd ever pridirel; She seemed to have desceaded rather low in her calling on Saturday in appropriating rabbits. She went to Mr. Simpson's shopqat 10.34 in the evening and made a small purchase. After she had gone .11rs. Simpson missed the rabbits and in tormed the police. Stiff-Sergeant Barlow investigated the case, and on hearin; that Robinson had been to the shop went to her house and there found the rabbits. Prisoner had just served a term of nine months imprisonment for theft. Since 1983 she had been repeatedly conv:cted. The worst feature was that she was training her two children in the same way and they had already been convicted.—Prisoner was sentenced to a months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Btfore the Mayor (in the chair), Councillor Wilkinson, Itlessrs. A. J. Jolley, H. Thornton, L. W. Reynolds, and 0. Pilling,. UNHAPPY MarattED LlFE.—Ceorge Leonard Heath, 4, Earle-street, was summoned by his wife Annie Heath, 135, Ellesmere-street. for persistent cruelty.—Mr. Steel appeired for the complainant and Mr. Browne defended.—Mr. Steel said the defendant had been guilty of many acts of cruelty towards his They were married so recently as in June 1903, but their married life had been very unhappy. On one occasion he put three razors in his pocket, went to the complainant, and threatened to take her life. On another date he got a knife and said he would stab her in the heart. He had ) pushed her .out of bed, and bitten her on the cheek. She was afraid to live with him.—Complainant gave evidence in support of this statement, and sail her husbmd bad also struck her.—Mr. Browne still the cause of the unpleasantness was that defendant objected to his wife's sister coming to the house frequently and taking complainant to the theatre. He also objected to a woman whom she made a com- panion of. He admitted striking complainant on one occasion, but in regard to the razor incident he only put the razors in his pocket for fear his wife should attack him.—The case was, adjourned for a month to see if the pal ties coull arrive at a settlement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>MOTOR RECORDS.</head>
        <p>MOTOR RECORDS.</p>
        <p>At Ormond Fl-c;rl4;,--17...A.:,--oil-Wednesday, further speed records w:re made in the automobile competi- tions. The mile tec,rd was beaten three times. The best time was that of Mr. IL L. Bowden, the Boston millionaire, who m ith 103-horse-power Mercedes covered the distance in th:rty-four and one-fifth seconds. His car wai Over we'ght and the prize was won by Mr. Artaur MacDonald, a young Englishman, whose time was thirty-foor and two-fifth seconds. Though she has a banking account of £4OO, a woman has been staying in the Bath Workhouse, paving is. a week for the privilege. The guardians object to the 'Workhouse being used as a hotel, and on Wednesday zeferzed the case to the Removals Committee.</p>
      </div>
