      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>INCREASE OF TEACHERS' SALARIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. May. and the Accommodation Question.</p>
        <p>There was very little discussion at Monday evening's meeting of the Warrington Education Committee held at the Town Hall under the presidency of Mr. F. Monks. There were also present :—Alderman Bennett (vice-chairman), the Revs. Morley Stevenson, John Yonge, and Father Wilson, Councillors Evans, Tinnion, Sankey, Fulton Smith, Burton, Hulse, and Wilkinson Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. Bibby, R. T. Fairclough, C. H. Unsworth, T. May, J. D. Buckton, G. Plinston, G. Walker, C. E. Parker, J. Upson, and J. Moore Murray (secretary and director). FINANCE COMMITTEE. INCREASE OF SALARIES.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance Committee a letter was read from the Cheshire Education Committee, intimat- ing that, during the period from the Ist July, 1903, to the 31st March, 1904, there were 77 children in average attendance at public elementary schools in the district who resided in the area of that county, whilst there were 73 children in average attendance at the schools of that Committee residing within that district, and that that Committee is prepared to pay for, and accept pay- ment in respect of such children. The Committee decided that,as the'difference between the numbers was so small, it was unnecessary for any adjustment in respect thereof to be made.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the head teachers be instructed to see that no articles are given to children in attend- ance at their schools, unless payment is received for them at the time.</p>
        <p>The committee recommended that the salaries of the undermentioned officials be increased as follows, viz : Mr. R. B. Roberts, assistant director, present salary £175; £lO per annum to a maximum of 2,200. Mr. A. S. Elliott, stores clerk, present salary £125; £5 per annum to a maximum of £l5O. Mr. Ernest Haigh, secondary clerk, present salary £7B; 2s. Gd. per week to a miximum of 2120.</p>
        <p>_ In moving the adoption of the minutes Mr. A. J. Jolley said the only matter likely to cause any difference of opinion was that relating to the increase of salaries to several members of the staff. Perhaps the simplest statement he could make was that after very careful consideration the committee unanimously recommended the advances.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. D. BUCKTON seconded. Mr. PLINSTON asked for information respecting the minute relating to the supply of articles to the scholars. He understood that some time ago the committee passed a resolution to the effect that materials were to be given to the children free of charge. Some of the teachers, he believed, were in the habit of setting home-lessons and if materials were taken home for that purpose they were to be paid, for. Mr. JOLLKY said the objet of tie committee in allowing the sale of some materials was, if possible, to foster a desire among the children to possess books of their own. Children who began to have a spirit of proprietorship would take greater care. The amount of the sales was very Furtall but it was advisable that it should be a cash trade. In a few instances, teachers had given credit and that had led to little discrepancies in the accounts.</p>
        <p>The minutes were approved. SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the School Kanagement Committee, the annual reports of the Board of Education upon the schools were submitted. INCREkSE OF TEACHERS' SALARIES. -</p>
        <p>The School Management Committed recommended increases of salaries to teachers in the elementary soh ools in accordance with the Committee's scale. The total amount of the increase is 2820.</p>
        <p>The managers of the Wycliffe British School wrote drawing attention to the fact that the average attend- ance in the mixed department of that school had increased above the class in which the head master ha.l been placed for the purposes of the committee's scale of salaries, and requesting the committee to take the salary of the head master into consideration. The matter was referred to the sub-coramittee appoinqd to consider the scale cot salaries for head teachers. e PHYSICAL INSTRUCTION. At the request of the Gymnasium Committee, Mr. A. J. Jolley, Mr. C. U. Unsworth, and the Rev. J. Yonge were appointed to meet three members of that com- mittee to discuss the physical instruction given to the children in the public elementary schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>THE HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee recommended-- That the following be fixed as the holidays for the various schools under the committee during the present year, viz. : Public Elementary Slhools.—Easter: April 19th (evening) to May Ist. Whitsuntide: June 9th (even- ing) to June 19th. Walking Day: June 28th (even- ing) to July 3rd. Midsummer: July 21st (evening) to August 21st. Mid-term: October 27th (evening) to November 6th. Christmas: December 22ad (noon) to January Bth. School of Art and the Secondary School.—Easter : April 19th (evening) to3May 2nd. ‘Vhitsuntide: June 9th (evening) to 'June 20th. Wztlking-day: June 29th (mid-day) to Jay 3rd. Mid-summer: July 21st (even- ing) to August 28th. Mid-term: October 27th (even- ing) to November 6th. Christmas : Daccluber 22nd (mid-day) to January 9th. Evening Continuation Schools and Technical Insti- tnte.—Easter : April 19th to April 25th. Christmas : December 22nd to January Bth. , THE FUTURE OF THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL. A letter was submitted from Mr. A. Cardew (H. M. Inspector), intimating that he had been instructed by the Board of Education to hold a conference between representatives of the Education Committee and the Governors of the Grammar choot with rererence to the committee's letter in regard to that school, and it was noted that 11 o'clock on the morning of Wednesday the 25th January, at the Town Hall, had been fixed as the time and place for the proposed conference.</p>
        <p>THE SECONDARY SCHOOL. The Headmaster of the Secondary Scheel having appeared before the committee and presented his term- end report, it was resolved :—" That arrangements be made for the appointment of a clerk to carry out the clerical work connected with the Secondary School and Technical Institute, in order that the head master may be able to devote more time to teaching and to the supervision of the work of the school, such clerk to take the place of the present office Loy " ; also " chat an additional laboratory assistant be appointed at the Secondary School." The Rev. MORLE ' STEVENSON, in moving tho adop- tion of the minutes, alluded to the conference re the Grammar School: It would not, he tsaid, be proper for him to make any remarks on the matter as it was sub jadice. He would, however, like to say that Mr. Cardew was a gentleman of very great experience in these matters, and he was sure the.whole committee would agree with him in expressing the hope that the conference would lead to the satisfactory arrangement which they all desired. The Rev. FATHER WILSON seconded. Mr. MAY said they generally obtained a very lucid explanation of the minutes of this particular committee and it so contrasted with what was said by the chairmen of other committees that it rather suggested a con- spiracy of silence. There was one point, however, which appeared to him to require both explanation and inquiry, and that was contained in the report about St. Barnabas' school. The Inspector said that if the average attendance was allowed to exceed 237 the grant would be entirely endangered. At the present time in that school the grant was at the lower rate, which meant that they were losing 287 shillings, and the committee were warned that the school was over full and not sufficiently staffed. If they next turned to the list of increases of salary, however, there were increases to the staff of St. Barnabas' school amounting to £3O. He , thought the state of affairs was most unsatisfactory. 1, and seemed to imply a serious want of oversight and efficiency both on the part of the committee and of Or managers of the school. V' The CHAIRMAN : Can you suggest a remedy? Mr. MAY announced his intention of moving that tin minute involving increases of salary to the staff at St. Barnabas' school be not passed. The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON said that at tho time of the visit of the Inspector to St. Barnabas' School there was a vacancy on the staff, but it had been filled up. As regarded the increases of salary they were only the normal increases according to scale, and even supposing that the school was overcrowdokhe thought it would be very hard to visit the teachers with the punishment which ought to be meted out to the managers, if to anybody., It was no use to go over the tale of over- crowding, as they knew all about it and were aware that the Sites and Buildings Committee Nias doing all it could to push forward the erection of a new school. Councillor EV ANS as one of the managers of St. Barnabas' school said they had no alternative but to continue the present conditions. They could not extend the school so they must either turn the children away or remain as they were until extra proyision had been made by the committee. Councillor TINNION said he was pleased to see that a 11 committee had been appointed to go into the question I of physical instruction, and hoped that it would include the consideration of the fees paid, as the amount paid at present was inadequate. The DIRECTOR said the resolution had been so worded as to embrace every side of the question. Mr. MAT said that with reference to the Grammar School conference he would like to know if any mem- bers °lithe Education Committee who were not members of the sub-committee would be allowed to attend to listen.</p>
        <p>Mr. FAIRCLOUGH calledattention to the report on the Warrington Parochial School, in which the inspector alluded to the noise caused by pissing vehicles. Jr thAre was no improvement, and the committee still repudiated responsibility, it seemed to him that they stood a chance of being brought down from the higher grant to the lower grant by it. He thought the :committee ought to take responsibility. Councillor TINNION : As chairman of the Paving and Sewerage Committee I might explain.</p>
        <p>The CH kIRMA.N : We can't allow you to keep "popping up "like this. Councillor TINNION : lam not in the habit of popping up. Surely you cannot describe it as popping up, when I ask the privilege of the committee to explain a matter. Nobody else can make the explanation except myself, but it does'nt matter. The Rev. M. STEVENSON, in reply t•-• Mr. May, said he did not suppose for a moment that the members of the School Management Committee would do other than welcome the presence of any member of the Education Committee. Of course matters must ultimately come before the General Committee itself. They must remember that the present arrangement was that the School Management Committee should have a confer- ence with the governors of the Grammar School, and it was quite out of the power of the committee to say that other people would be welcome without reference to the authorities of the Grammar School. With regard to Mr. Fairelough's remark upon the Parochial School, he was afraid that if the alteration was to be made it would be a matter for the managers and not for the committee.</p>
        <p>The minutes were then approved. SUGGESTED TENIPOR AltY ACCO.IIADDATION. At a meeting of the School Attendance committee a letter was read from the managers of the St. Ann's School,draw- ing attention to the overcrowded state of that school, and suggesting the desirabiliy of arrangements being made for the provision of temporary school accommo- dation in that neighbovrhood o relieve that school until such time as the proposed new school in the Orford Ward is erected. The committee could not see their way to take au 5 action in .he matter. The minutes were agreed to without commmt,on the motion of Councillor ELLISON. seconded by Mr. BIBBY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>MR. MAY AND OVERCROWDING.</head>
        <p>MR. MAY AND OVERCROWDING.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Sites and Buildings Committee, the members considered a letter from the Board of Education—intimating that the _Hamilton-street National School is still overcrowded, and requesting that the plans for the new school to be erected in the Orford Ward may be submitted with as little delay as possible. Mr. It. T. FAIRCLOITGII, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said a number of inquiries had been received from thearchitects with regard to the plans and competition, and all had received attention. Matters were going forward in an admirable manner. Councillor EVANS seconded.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : Have you got the sites ? Mr. PAIRCLOUGH : Yes. Mr. MAY expressed the opinion that the committee, ought not to allow that opportunity to pass without calling attention to the deficient school accommodation in Warrington. It had been publicly stated that there were a couple of thousand vacancies every morning in the schools but, as the members present were aware, most of the scholls had more scholars on the books than there was accommodation for. The Government Inspector had withheld part of the grant owing to the want of accommodation, and the School Committee had been suggesting temporary measures and economical methods which would have saved them voting imme- diately large sums in the cost of new schools; that was if they had temporary schools up and down the town or one temporary school that could be transferred. Then they might choose their own time for goinr, into the property market instead of being hurried, and obliged to buy at the time of the seller. He wished it to be put on record that the committee had made a great mistake iti not providing temporary accommodation, and that they would be in the wrong if they did not provide it for the poor children. There were people who objected to pauperization by the granting of out-door relief, and yet parochial relief was being granted on an enormous scale out of the rates to all sorts of people. Everyone was being tau ,ht to get what he could out of the rates, both by precept and example. The CHAIRMAN: What has that to do with the minutes?</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY: And poor children are being left to wander in the streets.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: Are you going to move an amend- ment? _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY moved that the minutes be not approved until temporary accommodation had been provided in the neighbourhood of St. Barnabas' schools and other schools which wore overcrowded. The chairman of the School Attendance COmmittee had suggested from time to time that rooms be engaged in the neighbourhood where there was a deficiency of accommodation. Why should they pet off from time to time providing for the poor when they were spending such enormous sums for .the benefit of those who .vere better off ? _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRUAN: The effect- of your amendment might be that it would be ten years before any school was built. Mr. loAtucLouGH, in reply, said the matter had been considered before. Mr. May must be extremely ignor- ant—(laughter)—or ho would have known that the inspectors would not allow children to go into rooms of the deScription which had been named. Certain sani- tary measures had to be adopted, and they could not be adopted until proper places were provided. He was very sorry Mr. May should bring such—well, he called it ridiculous—a proposition before the meeting. It was more economical to nut up good schools than temporary ones for the children. (Hear, hear). The amendment was not seconded, and the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>Ma. JOHN BURNS AND THE VICES OF PROTECTION.</p>
        <p>In the domain of ethics Protection was petty and selfish; in religion and morals it was everything that was bad; in t4ade, instead of being Imperial, it was parochial: in international relationships ,it was provin- cial t iii Impsrial policy it Was cUsrapting; in legis- lative methods it would be corrupting • in industry it would lead to lethargy, dependency, and inellciency. 4</p>
