      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <p>troughc,nnon.-Red ball 1 inch from .the</p>
        <p>_Jr, cushion a.-nd right behind the spot. Cue ball ktud object ball both 3 inches from the top cushion. Id both 13 inches from the red.</p>
        <p>Diagram 54 shows an example of a class of run- throughs seldom or never attempted by the ordinary player, anV yet these most useful strokes, whilst not exactly easy, are not nearly so difficult as they, appear to the majority of players. If a moderate player will place the balls in the positions given under Diagram 54 and try the tun-through cannon a few times he will very quickly find that he is quite able to get the stroke. The cue ball should be struck witk plenty of running side (right hand side in the diagram), and attention should be centred solely on sending the red hall between the white ball'ami the cushion without disturbing the white ball. When this is accomplished the cannon will almost slways be made. for the running side on the cue ball not only brings the ball off the bushion at the correct angle, but also assists ix the running of the ball. It cannot, however, be too strongly impressed upon players that the slightest carelessness when playing this stroke will almost certainly cause the object balls te kiss. Even good amateurs often miss the stroke through want of care. It sometimes seems ba easy to send the first ball between the cushion sad the second ball that enough attention 'is not riven to the stroke: result, the balls kiss, ne tannon is made, and perhaps good position is left for the other player. On the other hand, with the balls in the position shown in Diagram i 4 the making of the =non generally means very good position for the next stroke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>DIAGRAM 55.</head>
        <p>DIAGRAM 55.</p>
        <p>gh cannon from Ade an drag.—lted ball 43} inc rnahon and throe-eighths of an ir t!ushion. Object white 24 inch.' aushion ad 5 inchea from the at</p>
        <p>Diagram 55 shows a run-through cannon of tho same nature as that on Diagram 54. In this case, however, the stroke is far more difficult, owing to the cue ball being so much farther away (the striker being in hand). This cannon is best Played by hitting the cue ball low down, as by this means the running side with which the ball must, of course, be struck will be•retained muck longer. Also, as already explained in the chapter 0.1 SIDE, this bottom or drag on the cue ball will prevent the deviation from the line of aim which wauld otherwise take place to a greater or less extent as the result of side working in conjunc- •tion with the nap of the cloth. As in the stroke shown in Diagram 54, the player's attention must bo centred on sending the first object ball between the cushion and the second ball. A successful stioke will generally leave a good position, as the first object ball will come back off the top cushion k join the other balls.</p>
        <p>ST. CLEMENT'S V. HOLY TRINITY.—At St. Clement's Club, January 19th. ST. CLEMENT'S. iHOLY TRINITY. J. Wolfenden P. H. Simkin E. Jones H. Broadhurst lOO E. Hesketh B5 NV. Holmes lOO B. Walsh NV. Carter lOO W. Cooper 62</p>
        <p>' Majority for -- St. Clement's 83 ST. CLEMENT'S V. HOLY TRINITY 2ND TEAMS.— Played at Holy Trinity Club, January 19th. HOLY TRINITY. ST CLF</p>
        <p>—.... -......_ .. Crompton 75 H. C. Rhoden W. Worthington 75 A. Evans 72 S. Jenks P. B. Gandy 63 J. Burrows 75 George Hunt J. W. Dyke</p>
