      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Mortuary for Weston Point.</p>
        <p>The above Cquncil held its fortnightly meeting at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday under the presidency of Mr. S. Woodward. There were also present :—Messrs. G. Pilling, T. Baxter, C. E. Linaker, T. Wright, J. Stokes, W. M. Morris, C. Ro7. lance, J. Whitley, J. Mounfield, C. E. Linaker jun., J. Hallows, J. H. Davies, H. M. Davies. J. Spruce, A: G. Guest, J. L. Jones, G. Egerton Warburton, H. Eyes, T. Antwis, A. Broome, J. Hallows, P. Mounfleld, 3. Whitlow„ J. Smith, H. Mawdsley, W. Harrison, J. Butter, G. F. Ashton (clerk) W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical eider), W. Farrington (inspector of nuisances), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyors).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>-T-HE )4A-ITIitiANCE- OF LUNATICS.</head>
        <p>-T-HE )4A-ITIitiANCE- OF LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from Mr. Alexander Hornby clerk to the County Asylum Committee, Chester, inform- ing the Board that at a meeting of the committee of visitor° held on the 17th inst., the rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics in the Asylum for the quarter ending 31st March next had been fixed at Bs. 9d. per head per week. REVIBY WAIER SUPPLY.-PECULIAR SITUATION. At a meeting of the Helsby Parochial Committee the Clerk produced a resolution ihat the Committee be requested to instruct their engineer to revise his specifl tions and quantities of the Helsby Waterworks number I one contract in accordance with the terms of their reaolution of November 7th, and to then advertise for fresh tenders for the work.—The Clerk reported that he had ascertained that the extra cost of altering the speci- fications would be :—Engineer's fee, £5 126. 6d. and fresh specifications and advertising £9. The Committee resolved that having already made two recommendations to the District Council, both of which kad been referred back, they could not see their way to give any further recommendations beyond what they had sent in, as they could not see that any saving would be effected. I Mr. Guest said he was sorry Helaby was not repre- sented that day by its newly elected representative who was unfoitunately indisposed. It was a new experience, since he had been a member of the Council for a resolution sent by the Council to any Parochial Committee should simply be referred back with the statement that they declined to act upon it. He would leave it with the Council to do what they considered proper to roaintain the honour of the Council and do justice to Helsny. Since the meeting of the Parochial Committee the voice of the ratepayers had been heard against tbe policy set forth in the resolution, a District Council election having resulted in the return of the candidate who was in favour of economy. He moved that the Parochial Committe's action be not confirmed and that they be asked to adhere to the recommenda- tion of the bistriet Council as stated in the previous resolution.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling seconded. Mr. J. H. Davies pointed out that seven members of the Parochial Committee voted for the resolution and only one against—that one being the newly-returned District Councillor for Helsby. The matter was purely local, as the Helsby people would have to find the money, and he considered the Helsby people ought to be trusted by that Ccuncil to manage their own affairs. Mr. Pilling said he understood the District Council election was fought on the question. The inhabitants generally did not believe in that large expenditnre when they could yet the work done for much less,,, Mr. Guest's proposition was carried.__</p>
        <p>110Td•RY FOR WISTON. POINT. The Clerk reported that at a meeting of the Weston Parochial Committee it was recommended that the District Council, subject to the approval of the Weaver Navigation Trnstees,approve the erection of a mortuary at Weston Point between the Weaver Locks and •the Manchester Ship Canal in accordance with specifica- tions and plans produced by the Inspector of Nuisances. It was also recommended that tenders for the work be advertised for. Mr. Guest moved the confirmation of the minuter,and Mr. J. H. Davies seconded. The Clerk pointed out that the erection of the mortuary would be a charge on the whole district. The cost was roughly estimated at about £65. Mr. Wright said there were probably more inquests at Weston Point than in the whole dist' ict combined, and the matter of mortuary accommodation had been brought forward by the Coroner time after time. It had been a scandal quite long enough. The Chairman said there could be no question as to the necessity for proper arrangements, the only matter for consideration was to the possibility of reducing the cost. The land had been given by the Weaver Trustees at the nominal rental of 2s. Bd. per annum. The minutes were approved, the Clerk being instructed to forward plans of the proposed mortuary to the Weaver Trustees for their approval.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>STOCKTON_ IlltiT-11 MATTERS.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON_ IlltiT-11 MATTERS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Com- mittee, it was decided to recommehd that the Surveyor be paid 5 per cent. on the contract price for services rendered him in connection with the making of Kimber- ley-drive and Condoneld's-avenue. It was further recommended that the Surveyor appointed to prepare plans and specifications for the making of Bedford- street, Cawdor-street, and Carlton-street, be requested to use such existing material in the streets as he con- sidered suitable, and to specify for the paving of the footpaths with checked tiles, laid herringbone-wise. In reply to Mr..Brootne, the Clerk said the contract price men tioned in the first resolution was £60% The minutes were confirmed. PROVIDING FOR HEAVY MOTOR TRAFFIC. Mr. .1. Mounfleld said the Parliamentary Committee had met to consider the strength of bridges in the district and their adaptability to receive heavy motor traffic. According. to the new byelaws the Council was required, if certain bridges were not strong enough to carry a weight of eight tons per axle, to erect notice boards stating the weights for which they were adapted. If motor owners did not heed the warning andlany damage was done,the responsi- bility rested with them. In the Budworth district there were several bridges which were not fit to carry the heavy weight and notices would be provid ed. The</p>
