      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR. 1 THE SOLDIERS' MEMORIAL.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR. 1 THE SOLDIERS' MEMORIAL.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENT. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases seal their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matters inteaded for publication must be written on side only of the paper and sent in, if possible, not later than Thursday.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. Percy Randall. Sir.—The lamentably sudden death of Mr. Percy Randall has elicited a remarkable display, of sympathy, which has been by no means conkned to Warrington,and I am inclined to thinkthat a movement in the direction of a Memorial Fund would meet with general support. The value of such a fund to tie members of his family, when a blow like this falls upon those who could not ; he expected to be pre/pared for it, must be obvious to everyone. He was himself a generous ' sympathetic man, always ready to give his services ia a 'good cause; and no one knows better than I do, that it was sterling worth till', won for him his general popularity. There are few districts round Warrington, that have not some pleasant recollection of visits from him, on the various occasions of festivity, which at times serve to brighten ow lives, and Where friends of Mr. Randall will not he found who will be glad to respond to the appeal that is now made. I may say that the churchwardens of Trinity Church desire to associate themselves with me in this appeal, and to them as wet: as to myself donations may be sent for the time +being. The official receipt will be signed by Mr. Thomas Welsby the hon. treasurer (pro tem). tam ours, &amp;c. E. C. E. CARLETON, Holy Trinity Vicarage, 27th January, no. P.S.—The names of the wardens are J. IL HALTON, 73. Lovely-lane, Warrrington. TIKS. VVELSBY, 13, Froghall lane,Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rebert Pierpoint's Defence of the Sugar Convention. Sir,—ln we 'report of the proceedings of the Junior Conservative" Smoker," I notice that Mr. Pierpoint essays the difficult task of defending the Sugar Conven- tion. Tho in,:mber for Warrington remarked that "their adversaries said sugar had risen in consequence of the Sugar Convention. That he absolutely denied." (Applause). It is somewhat singular that while absolutely deny- ing that the Convention has raised the price he attributes the increare of the production in the West Indies to the encouragement which these islands have received from sugar being at a " fair market price " the result withowt76.cubt of the Convention. Mr.Pierpoint cannot have it Loth ways. Either" the Convention did contribute to the rise in the price by the abolition of bounties on exported sugar and so established a better market for the production of the West Indies, or it did not, and the rise is due simply as Mr. Pierpoint says" to the fact that there had been a want of rain for the beet crop." If the latter and thelrise were due entirely.to nature,then there is no " enceuragement " given to these islands, the main reason why we entered into the Convention. It will baffle the wit even of an M.P. to hold both positions at one add the same time. The truth is (and it would have been 'wiser for Mr. Pierpoint to accept it, and make the best Af it that the rise In sugar is due to two causes, first the dry season, and second the Sugar Convention, and the rise due to the first cause has been intensified by the second. In gauging the part of the rise due to tile action of the Convention, we can best assess this by the relative prices, and consequent consumption in the countries, members of the Convention' before and after the arrangement came into force. It is difficult to give comparative figures, because the prices have been so irregular, but broadly the prices in the countries that have abolished the bounties have gone down nearly as namh as the prices have gone up here. To use a figure Mr. Pierpoint will appreciate, this conats two in a divisitm. If Mr. Pierpoint were unfor- tocate enough to losehis seat for Warrington it would not only mean a loss of one seat to the Conservatives, ' bat would count double because of the gain of a seat t&amp; the Liberals. What we have lost by the Convention our competitors have gained. The result of the double effect on prices upon the consumption of sugar'in the principal countries effected is as follows :</p>
        <p>,I' 'Consumption of sugar, September, 1903, to August, 104. , (The sugar. year is from September to August). I Tons. In. De- . 'cNO2-3. 1903-4. crease. crease. 'United Kingdom' '1;460,450 1,409,737 50,713 France '371,119 699,030 327,911 / Germany 740,193 1,126,432 386,239 Aust-Hungary Belgium Holland . 77,934 94,437 16.503</p>
        <p>,5,228 3,924,634 889,119 50,713 'Less decrease 50,713</p>
        <p>Net increase</p>
        <p>British consumption 'decreased 3 per cent.,'while Con- tinental consumption increased 54 per cent. Pertinent to the question of the portion of the rise rine to the dry seasonjit isbvell to remember that the un- restricted power to bay in the cheapest market steadies the price of any article, and the effect of a failure of crop in one part of the world is generally neutralized by an abundant croursomewhere else. By shackling onr freedom to buy and shutting out the sugar from Russia and the Argentine because they were selling us sugar too cheaply, we subjected ourselves to the full effect of ,the failure of the crop over the area of the limited sources of supply. Much might be said regarding the aspect of the ques- tion, as it affects the West Indies.and this I may return to, but it is unnecessary until Mr. Pierpoint abandons • the untenable ground 'hebas taken up, ztz :—" That the price of sugar has simply risen from the fact that there had been a want of rain for the beet crop," and that he "absolutely denied It was in consequence of the Sugar Convention. Tenketh, January 25th. 1905.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>CONCERT -IN CAIRO STREET SCHOOLROOM.</head>
        <p>CONCERT -IN CAIRO STREET SCHOOLROOM.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening asuccessful concert, arranged by Mr. Percy Mainwariag, was given in Cairo-street schookoom in aid of the local funds of the National ociety for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. Some pupils of Miss Sutton contributed some songs and piano 'items which were 'listened to with manifest pleasure by a friendly audience. Some violin and 'cello solos by Miss Bertha Bouchier and Mr. J. Jackson and two quartets for violin, mandoline, banjo, and piano by the Misses Warren and'Pooley.,r,ave pleasant variety to the vocal music, Mr. Hallard's recitals and mimicry also contributing in the same direction. The most im- portant items were the songs by• Miss Mabel Henshall and Mr. J. Rubbitt. The former in "If I had a dolly " and "A Cigna tragedy" showed how easily, in music as In all other of ,the arts, one's best work is always done. There is a naturalness and rutivetev about Miss Henshairs singing which, quite apart from the technical excellence of her vocal efforts, lends a distinct charm to everything she attempts. and particularly to songs of this character. Mr. J. Rabbit is always an attractive singer. The earnestness and soulfulness with which he attacks a love ditty while refreshing to note,-rarely fails to touch sympathetic chords in the mind of -the listener. In interpreting therefore such songs as "Till death" and "River of Dart" it is not surprising that Mr. Babbitt scored the most pronounced of successes, giving at the same time a maximum of pleasure to his audience. Miss Sutton, Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes and Mr. Underwood shared the honours of accompanying the various vocalists and instrumentalists. We understand that the concert wilt result in a considerable addition to the unds of the society.</p>
        <p>About six o'clock on Thursday evening the body of a woman wae found on the Wixral Railway between Hoy lake and West Kirby, having, it is supposed, been cut in two by a passing train. It is not known how she came to be on the line.</p>
        <p>Captain George Fraser Macnee, a well-known Border man, was on Thursday killed while rabbit shooting on a farm near hi house, at Corstorphine. in getting over a hedge his gun accidently went off, the contents of the barrel lodging in his stomach. . .</p>
        <p>.„ Among the wives of reigning sovereigns who shop by post or telegraph in London, no one is a more regular customer than the Getman Empress. It is fashionable in some shops to frame and hang up the telegraphic message received from great ladies. One haberdasher lately displayed a message from a Grand Duchess asking for a fresh supply of stockings ; "Please send immediately, as the Duchess has hardly a pair left" The Board of Trade has received through the Foreign Office, two gold cigarette cases for Captain H. E. Batt, master, and Mr. Keene, surgeon, of the British steam- Ship Ajax, of Liverpool, which have been awarded to them by the Emperor of Russia in recognition of their services to the Russian officers and men wounded at the battle of Chemulpho in February last.</p>
        <p>WIDNES YOCTIIS' TERRIBLE DEATH. A terrible accident occurred at Widnes about one o'clock on Monday. A young fellow named Alfred Ernest Mercer,aged 18, who resided at 15, Davies-street was working on some Ni•ag go ns at the railway siding of Mr. Thomas Cooper, coal merchant, in Dutton-road, when he fell by some means between the buffers of two of the waggons and was fearfully crushed. He was taken to the Accident Hospital on the ambulance but on arrival there iife was found to be extinct. ST. ffECENS PERJURY CHARGE. Josiah Bowden, collier, of 20, Abbotslield-road, St. Helens Junction, wars brought up at St. Helens on Thursday on the charge 4,f perjury. and was committed trial a th,eeasizas,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>WORKINGMEN WANT GENERAL BULLER TO UNVEIL IT.</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN WANT GENERAL BULLER TO UNVEIL IT.</p>
        <p>The Progress of the Fund.</p>
        <p>Thanks to the efforts put forth by Warrington workingmen, £1.83 3s. has been handed over to the fund opened with the ol)ject of erecting a memorial `to the men of the South Lancashire Regiment who fell in the Boer war. At the Town Hall on Wednesday evening the final meeting of the Workingmen's Committee was held under the presidency of the esteemed chairman , (Mr. D. Davies). There was a large and representative attendance, including Mr. W. Haigh (vice-chairman), Mr. H. Hope (hon. treasurer), Mr. C. 1 Newton (hon. secretary), and Mr. T. H. Spinks ,who has taken an active part iu the movement. The CHAIRMAN said that whilst that was the last formal meeting of the committee and they were retir- ing from the colours tinder which theyhad been serving for the past twelve months, they were not entirely dis- solved as a committee. Until the memorial assumed a definite form the committee would continue to exist, so that at any time the members might be called together for consultation on little matters which they had a right to have a voice in. The meeting assented to this course. • THE FINANCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOPE announced that since the last meeting he I had received £1 Bs. 2d. from Messrs. Crosfield's employees and ss. front Mr. D. Davies. The postal authorities were allowing the Post-master at Warring- ton to rieceive subscriptions oil one of the Executivel Committee's cards, and the proceeds would be handed over to the Borough Treasurer. The general statement lof account showed a total income of £194 ss. including the following items—Employees of Whitecross Co., Limited, £3O; Rylands Brothers Limited, £3O ; Fletcher, Russell and Co., £lll Os. ; Monks, Hall and Co. (forge dept.), £9 9s. 511 ; Monks Hall and Co. (tube works), £5; Longford Gas Works, £3 6s. 41; Warrington Corporation Tramways, £3; highways department, Os.; Warrington Wire Rope .Works, £5 4s. 9d.; Edelsten and Sons, £4 Os. 6d.; Reynolds and Co., (Mersey-street), £2 ; P. Walker and Sons, Limited (Dallam), £4 125.; Longford Depot. £1 2s. 6d.; J. Smethurst and Sons, Os. Bd. ; Longford Wire Company Limited, 10s. ; J. and W. N. Hutchings, is. Bd. : Crosfield and Sons, Limited, £1 Bs. 2d. ; Crosfield's Athletic Club (football match), £5 7s. 7d.; Crosfield's brass band (dance), £2 13s. ; proceeds of open air concert, 210 12s. 3d, : concert in Parr Hall, 226145.; Warrington Football Club £3 3s. ; Warrington Co- operative Society £2 23.1 The 4 expenditure amounted to £8 2s. leaving a balance to be transferred to the Executive Committee of £lB6 3s. The balance sheet was adopted, on the proposition of Mr. L. Davies seconded by Mr. H. Broadhurst. SUGGESTED SPECIAL MEMORIAL TO WARRINGTON MEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. J. Nrimp moved a resolution conveying to the Executive Committee the suggestion that any surplus which remained after providing the memorial to the men of the South Lancashire Regiment should be de- voted to the purchase of tablets to perpetuate the memory of Warrington men, irrespective of regiment, who fell in the war. No better way of utilizing the surpltes, could in his opinion, be suggested. Mr. WILLIAM CIURNOCK seconded.</p>
        <p>- 118 CHAIRMAN said that three months ago there was correspondence in the local press advocating the idea and it struck him, as an old Warringtoman, that it was a very proper thing to consider Outside the military element, not a single penny' had been contributed to the memorial from Widnes and St. Helens, although the committee might reason- ably have expected support from those towns. Many of the mut who fell in the war were Warringtonians, and it was natural that the people of Warrington should desire to perpetuate their memory in some special form. He mentioned the subject at a meeting of the Executive Corardittee and the members were almost unanimously in favour of something of the kind being done. Major Roome, however, very reasonably pointed out that as the memorial was distinctly for the South Lancashire Regiment the two schemes could not be run jointly without the authority of those who had subscribed. The proposition met with unanimous approval. It was stated that the cost of the South Lancashire memorial would be about £l,OOO, and in addition there vzonld be foundation and other expenses. The CHAIRM'AN said he would not like an impression to get abroad that there was already a surplus. Oa the contrary, they had not, as yet, sufficient funds for the memorial, but they were expecting more money from various sources. If he gauged the feeling of Warring- ton men correctly, he did not think they would lack funds for the special memorial to the local soldiers. THE UNVEILING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>SUGGESTION TO INVITE GENERAL BULLER. The CHAIRMAN said i t WAS the feeling of many of his colleagues on the committee that some distinguished soldier should be invited to unveil the memorial, and it was not too early tojinake a suggestion. There was a strong opinion on the committee, and he believed througheut thetown, that General Buller should be asked to perform the ceremony. He was a distinguished Englishman and a distinguished soldier, who had the South Lancashires under his command in South Africa, and they could not invite anyone who would be more popular, and more deservedly so. He moved that the Executive Committee be requested to send an invitation such as he had suggested, to General Buller. Mr. J. J. WIELD remarked that the late Colonel 0' Leary died whilst fighting under General Buller. I Mr. W. HAIGH supported. There were very few Warringtonians, he said, who had not taken the deepest interest in 'General Bullet's movements. They all heartily sympathised with him in the extreme difficulties in which he was placed during the war, and they would give him a cordial welcome to Warrington. Councillor WRIGHT supported. He believed General Buller had beeh victimised. and such little occasions as that on which they proposed to invite him helped to clear away .some of the mud which had been unjustly thrown upon him. He (Councillor Wright) had the pleasure of seeing the General on Salisbury Plains and he was a perfect gentleman to the troops under his command. The resolution was unanionsly passed. THANKS.</p>
        <p>Councillor WRIGHT moved a vote of thanks to the chairman, vice-chairman and officers of the committee for the splendid work they had done. Mr. L. DAvnts seconded, and Messrs. D)oley and Webb supported the proposition which was cordially approved. At this stage of the proceedings the Mayor entered the room and was heartily greeted. The CHAIRM AN, in returninr, thanks, said it was to the Mayor's forethought that the initiation of the memorial scheme was due. £l,OOO had been raised towards the fund, and if his Worship did no other work during his mayoralty he had done something which would be remembered as long as the memorial stood. His Worship had promised to entertain the company after the meeting, and he would move a vote of thanks to him for his hospitality. Mr. L. DAVIES seconded and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>..... —.— The CHAIRMAN paid a tribute to the services willingly rendered by the officials of the Corporation. Their work was particularly gratifying at the present time when they had had to undergo an ordeal for which they had not in any way been responsible. He would particularly mention Mr. T. H. Spinks, of the Town Clerk's department,and Mr. J. Houghton, of the Borough Treasurer's department. Mr. Hoes seconded and the vote was heartily accorded. Mr. SPINKS briefly returned thanks. THE MAYOR'S HOSPITALITY. After the meeting the company adjourned to the Council Chamber where, through the kindness of the Mayor, they were entertained to a hot pot supper. His Worship was unable to attend the gathering but ample provision bad been made for the enjoyment of the assembly, Mr. Powis catering in first class style. While the tables were cleared the members were each presented by the chairman with a print of the picture of the " Storming of Pieters Hill "suitably framed as a memento of their connection with the Memorial Fund together with a booklet of the doings of the South Lancashires in South Africa. The loyal toasts were afterwards given by the chairman followed by the toast of " The Mayor and Mayoress of Warrington " which was received with acclamation as was that of the Chairman which was proposed by Mr. Nield in a neat little speech in which he referred to the many good qualities possessed by Mr. Davies and particularly in I regard to the many movements in which Mr. Davies had taken part. Mr. Davies after thanking the members for the way in which his name had been received, referred to many of the objects in which he had assisted and expressed the hope that be would be snared to still further help in any movement which had in view as good an object as the one in which they had lately been engaged. The following toasts were also well received :—"The Vice-Chairman" (Mr. Haigh), "The Hon. Treasurer" (Mr. H. Pope), "The Hon. Secretary (Mr. C. J. Newton) and Mr. Tpinks, the proposers of the toasts referring in highly complimentary terms to the work done by these gentlemen. Interspersed between the toasts Mr C. J. Newton , gave the members lt number of gramaphone selections. the instrument being fitted with a funnel, the patent of Mr. E. Whittle which the members voted as a great success. Songs were ably rendered by the following members: Mr. J. B. oadhurst sang "The Sea is England's Glory" "The Skipper," and " Anchored " ; Mr. W. Ditchfleld "Sons of the Sea " ; Mr. J. Smellie "The Star of Rabble Burns"; Mr. D. Davies "The Plumber"; and several other members who were unable to entertain in this manner related some very amusing anecdotes. The evening passed all too rapidly and the singing of "God Save the King" brought the proceedings to a Iclose the members votieg the whole evening as very enjoyable and all joining is. n very hearty vote of thanks to his Worship the Mayor.</p>
      </div>
