      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 28,1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>(By " Ovalist.'",</p>
        <p>Warrington came very near to achieving greatness on Saturday and creating a mild sensation in League circles. They were only beaten at Bradford by a l goal-kick and ought certainly to have halved the points with the League champions. Only in Warring- ton was there much hope of anything like so credit- able a show. The local enthusiasts are, however, dauntless and they failed to see what there was to stop Warrington repeating their earlier success against the Bradfordians There was nothing much in the way except the Bradford men themselves, and they form rather a big obstacle among the Park Avenue hurdles. At half-time Warrington were leading,and the disap- pointment when thn news of thA final defeat by 5-3 arrived was keener than if the locals had been getting teadily thrashed throughout the game.</p>
        <p>Only a goal kick! A.nd right in front of the posts again too. Who was the criminal? The " Chronicle " said Hockenhall, but no, 'twas his partner D. Davies. Fish, for once, is innocent. " Dai ' did a remarkable thing, for which he earned and received the apprecia- tion of his comrades in eminently suitable terms_ When the two half-backs and Kenyon made the, run which got Warrington the try, the remainder If the players were left far behind, at the other end of the &amp;4d. Captain Hallam even had not reached half way when the ball was grounded. Davies immediately brought the oval out, popped at goal, missed, and lest the match before anybody could say "Jack Robinson," or even "Jack Preston "—which is shorter and more to the point.</p>
        <p>lam afraid the incident is rather a reflection en the management and captaincy of the team, though Hallam was helpless in this case. Still if any player thinks himself entitled to take an all-Important place-kick at goal without anyone's permission there is evidence of con siderable slackness. There have been frequent remarks of late as to the haphazard way in which the team is led. Hallam is too unassuming. As captain he ought to take the reins in his hands and insist on holding them. He is the man who should direct a player to take penalty and place-kicks, and if he habit- ually did so there would be no danger of such an absurd fiasco as at Bradford. ' The cup-ties will very soon be on us, and I hope by then that Captain Hallam will have found means to assert his authority. That there will be an improvement in results when the team is properly captained I am convinced.</p>
        <p>The Bradford ,onme was as good a one as need be I wished for. Bot:h teams played fine football and carried on operations at an exhilarating pace. Early in the first half the home team looked certain to score for the burly Decharn was given a pass 1 at centre when well-placed. Fish was yards behind, but he threw his head back with a characteristic jerk and went after the Scotchman like a streak. The race was a great one and Fish captured his man a yard from the line to the relief of the Warrington men. They buckled to with renewed ardour after this, and a misdirectedHradford kick enabled Davies, Hockenhall and Kenyon to draw clear away, the wing man out- pacing the opposition and scoring finely under the posts. The goal-kick I will say no more about. Bradford made great efforts, led by the indefatigable Marsden, but Warrington maintained the lead until the interval.</p>
        <p>Daring breathing time, the tip was given to War- rington to bring Thomas out when near the line for the purpose of keeping an eye on Marsden, but the advice was neglected and the Brad- ford skipper in consequence managed to beat Warrington off his own bat. Several times he tried to break through and was captured, but he did the trick eventually, and himself adding a goal won the match. Warrington did not show good tactics, or they would have kept the lead once they got it. They could easily deal with the Bradford passing, but they neglected to strengthen the defence just where Marsden was severely testing it, and were beaten in consequence. It may seem a bit infra diç to bring a man out, but it would probably have saved the game for Warrington—and after all their business is to win matches. Thomas could have been retied upon to watch the " international," and he should have been given the chance !</p>
        <p>-- Fish, apart from his capture of Dechan, played an inconspicuous part in the game. Bradford had enough of him at Wilderspool, and they succeeded admirably in keeping play away from his quarter on Satur- day. The number of scrums on his side of the field was remarkable. Very rarely did it happen that he was on the wide side of the pack, and scarcely ever did be get a pass. Consequently the Park Avenue men had not so much to fear from him as usual, and a word of praise is due to them for what were unmis- takably clever tactics. Fish was looking for work and.in fine trim, but chances came not his way. Little fault could be found with any of the men. A lot of play took place on Brooks' and Kenyon's wing but these two players acquitted themselves well and confirmed recent good Impressions. Davies also showed better form, while the forwards were only beaten in heeling out. Of late they have been slow in this respect, and some new method needs to be tried. Weight and strength are not wanting.</p>
        <p>Oldhaua proved the quality of their reserve backs by defeating Hunslet by 20 points to 2 points, while Broughton Rangers successfully emerged from a severe ordeal at St. Helens. At the other end of the table, Batley nearly gave the Wiganers apoplexy, for iit was only n the closing stages that the home side won by a dropped goal to nil. Consequently more changes in the Central Park contingent! A little " revival " seems to be needed among the Welsh of that quarter. Leigh's bumptiousness was crushed at Hanson-lane, Halifax, and the interesting duel between Runcorn and Widnes had to be postponed to a future date.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows --For— —Against P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pts Pts. Oldham -. 22.-17.- 5.- 0.-33 43-195.-19 15-- 83-31 Bradford 22...15... 5... 2...25 44-182...19 24-110...32 Brou't'n R 21.-15.- 5.- 1.-24 42-174.-26 16-100_31 Leeds Hunslet Wigan 2l-.12- 3.- 1.-22 29-131.-23 10-103.-25 Warr'gt'n 21...11... 5... 2...23 29-133...17 18— 88...24 Hull K.R 21...11...10... 0...27 28-138-19 25-113...22 Hull 2l .10.. 2...20 31-133...22 27-125...22 Salford Leigh.— 21.- 9.-11.- 1.-12 24-- 96.-19 28-122-19 Swinton 22- 9.-12.- 1.-14 25-108.-27 29-141.-19 HaKfax - 8.-14 1.-15 21— 93-.18 26-114...17 Wakefield 22.- 1._27 10-- 81-.21 27--123.-15 St. Helens 21.- 7.-13.- 1.-26 25-127.-22 52-220.-15 Widnes Runcorn - 21.- G.-15-. 0.-21 21-105.-28 3G-164-.12 Batley 2- 4.-16.- 1.-18 15-- 81.-32 37-175.- 0</p>
        <p>Warrington" A" were again engaged in a friendly match, and beat Rochdale Athletic by 20 points to 5. There are considerable arrears to be made up in the combination fixtures. Berry and It. Bate played finely on Saturday,the former improving his point average by 4 goals and a try. W. Bate shaped well, but appears to require considerable coaching by brother Bob before he can think of higher company. Forwards and wings played poorly, and a lecture would not do young Short any harm. He ought to know by now that his place is behind and not before his centre.</p>
        <p>The combination table up-to-date is as follows P. W. L. D. C 4. T. Pts. G. T. Pts. Pts. Wigan (A)..13...11... 1... 1...34 50-245... 6 6 30...23 Chadderton.l2... 9... 3... 0...13 19— 83...14 16— 76-.18 W'gtou (A)10... 8... 2...0 ...13 30-1-16... 4 10— 33...16 R'gh'nlt(A)l4... 7... 5... 2.-11 25 97... 5 11— 43...16 Widnes (A).12... 6... 4... 2...11 13— 61... 7 19— 71...14 St.H. (A)...16... 6... 8... 2-21 25-115...17 34-136...14 Oldham (A)13... 5... 6... 2... 9 28-102...15 17— 81...12 Swinton (A)15... 5... 8... 2..12 22 90...11 26-100-12 Leigh (A)...14... 4... 8... 2... 8 15— 61...15 19— 87...10 Runcorn(e4)l2... 4,.. 8... 0... 6 15— 57...24 22-114... 8 R'd'leH.(A)ls... 2...10... 3... 9 9 40...24 51-201... 7 Salford (A)10... 2... 6... 2... 5 8— 34..18 31-129... 6</p>
        <p>The return gave with Leeds is down for decision to-day, and I hope to see Warrington atone for the severe reverse sustained at Headingley. They were more thoroughly beaten then —particularly forward— than in any other match this season. At Wilderspool, however, there should be no difficulty in putting paid to the Leeds account.</p>
        <p>-- Only one change has been made in the team from last week Shugars taking the place of W. Swift, an the fifteen will be : Hallam, full back; Fish,lsherwood Brooks' and Kenyon, threequarters ; Hockenhall and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <p>Davies, half backs ; Boardman, Preston, Shugars, Thomas, Belton, Jolley, Swift, and Naylor, forwards.</p>
        <p>I hear of no new men likely to be registered for the cup-ties, Widdeson of Broughton has been angled for, but the prospect of persuading the Rangers to release him is not good. He would certainly be an acquisition to any club. Welsh talent of a sort has been plentiful but the Warrington managers are not taking the bate so readily as some clubs. Possibly the landing-net may hold something substantial before the end of the season. There is plenty of room for another good three-quarter and half back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>Last week both the Stockton Heath matches were cancelled, as were most of the other matches. The most important match played was North v. West, which resulted in a win for the North by 4-2.</p>
        <p>This week the Stockton Heath Club play South Man- chester Ist and 2nd, when the following teams will represent Stockton Heath :—First (at home) : R. G. Pierpoint, goal ; E. E. Woods and L.Fairclough, backs; J. Richardson, Eric Fairclough, and J. G. Hatton, halves; C. H. Bond, S. Baxter, R. Pierpoint, W. Richard- son, and R. Fairclough, forwards. Bully-off, 2-45. Referee, N. W. Bellamy. -- Second (away) : F. G. Kertland, goal ; P. B. Hawtin and E. Fairclough, backs; A. Jones, H. Smith and L. Robertson, halves; D. Skelton, AV. Tinnel. W. Longdin, T. Crompton and A. Ferguson, forwards. Train leaves Central Station 1-55:</p>
        <p>As the frost is still keeping its hold, it looks as ii there will be another blank week.</p>
        <p>The match last week between Latchford and Club Moor resulted in a win for Club Moor by four goals to two.</p>
        <p>To-day Latchford first and second play Altrincham St. Margaret's first and second. The Senior team at Latchford will be: Goal, W. Unsworth ; backs, G. Heald, W. Knight ; half backs; W. B. Cobbe, J. B. Wilkinson, T. Dixon ; forwards, W. Spensley, E. P. Cliff e, W. H. Banks, J. Pearson. A. Albinson. Second tearn : E. Jones, C. Hale, T. Young, J. Wilkin- son, A. H. Rathbone, J. Rathbone, J. Callon, R. L. Jones, D. Knight, W. Smith, W. Pickles, at Altrincham. Train, 1.59 C.L.C. to West Timperley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE.</head>
        <p>PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE.</p>
        <p>[By Godfrey Dean.] --- Phyllis in the Dock.</p>
        <p>I might have known what would follow. 'Tis Phyllis's way. She lures one on and then--! To be explicit, when I was at Cadogan-square on the last occasion I thought Phyllis had repented of her ways. Her mood was so different from any other I have seen her in—though that does not signify. She was all demureness. .</p>
        <p>Yet I need not have made such a mistake. I wrote to Phyllis—of course, I had a reason, though it I was not necessary—and possibly—l say only possibly I —my letter breathed in the faintest degree that I was pleased. Quite enough for Phyllis, this. Promptly she replies, saying I must not see her for a week. Reason not stated. (Whoever heard of a woman offaring reasons of her own free will?) If I ever lose my fine balance of mind Phyllis will be to blame. She is the most disturbing element in 'my life,and the doubts and fears, alternated with hope and joy, with which she inspires me are really a serious menace to my reason. I endured three days of gloom, when lo ! word from Phyllis. Just this—" Why ?" ! I have said Phyllis is inconsequent. Nothing could Ibe truer. But I also can ask " Why ? " And will. I Phyllis received me with reproachful eyes. What I chance have 1 against such tactics? I've no redress, as Phyllis dislikes anything savouring of an explana- tion.</p>
        <p>Indeed, if I attempted anything of the sort she would immediately sly I did not understand her a bit. Which would be quite true, though I dare not admit it.</p>
        <p>Phyllis having unfeelingly consigned me to a chair at least six feet'away from her, I awaited her pleasure. "Why are people so literal ? " she asked. °1 like people with some inangination." I pondered, while I recovered from my surprise at this sudden attack.</p>
        <p>"Some people," I said, "have more than their share, which levels things. It is good that there is a leaven- ing influence on those mortals oppressed by a riotous imagination." Phyllis shook her head sadly. Only her profile was visible—that of a purely English type of face, with its clear complexion, softly-curving nose,nostrils delicately dilated, Tips well formed and sufficiently close, chin whose firmness was softened by its shape- liness and, above all, distracting brown eyes. "Circe !" I said it beneath my breath, but Phyllis caught it. Her hearing is inconveniently quick at times. She turned inquiring eyes on me. " It's as well," I said, to cover my confusion, "we are not living, say, one hundred years hence." Phyllis was interested. "Why ? " " When society is in a perfect state "—begging the question—" and there is a remedy for everythtng„with a little imagination "(Phyllis smiled) " you can see it is possible von might have to answer before a court of law."</p>
        <p>" For -;11-at ?" Phyllis was the , picture of I surprise. It was impossible for me to draw back now, much as I wished. Phyllis would never forgive me if I did. I assumed a judicial air. "Phyllis Vaughan, you are charged with disturbing the peace of mind of His Majesty's subjects by feminine arts and wiles. Are you guilty or not guilty ?" " Subjects ?" Phyllis asked. It was a pertinent question, I admit. " Presumably they are numerous, but as they clnnot be named I willlamend the charge to one of His Majesty's subjects." Who is the prosecutor ?" Phyllis is altogether too shrewd to put under cross-examination. " simple person who has never done you harm and who only desires your happiness —to wit, one Bertie Wilton! " . . .</p>
        <p>Phyllis was surveying the toe of her dainty shoe, and I perceived an access of colour in her face. It was my turn now. "Are you guilty or not guilty?" She looked up and smiled, and, as my severe aspect did not relax, smiled again. "You must not attempt to influence the Court," I said. "Whet is your plea?" "If I say guilty," she whispered. Her manner was very provoking. "You must not trifle with the time of the Court. If you say guilty "—I held her with my eyes—" I must pass sentence--" Phyllis divined my intention. c. No," she c3rre3tel "I plead not guilty.'" My disappointment amused her. "With intent," she added, in a whisper. Phyllis is difficult to deal with. "Very well, then, the information against you shall now be read." My procedure was hopelessly at fault, I'm afraid. Phyllis had now assumed an anxious expression. I produced a paper from my pocket, and pretended to read aloud from it. "It is alleged," I said, "that you, being a person possessed of great personal attractions and charm of manner--"</p>
        <p>Phyllis showed a disposition to interfere. "You must not interrupt," I said. "You will have an opportunity to speak later." Phyllis sighed. I seized the opportunity to repeat what I had said. That you, being a person possessed' of great personal attractions and charm of manner— ..._, My cunning was not lost on Phyllis. I continued: "Have, since his introduction to you, exercised the arts of your sex on the prosecutor to such a degree that his peace of mind is for ever disturbed; so much so that he is unable to fatal tile duties of a citizen in a proper manner." Evidence," I said, "will now be called in support of the charge. The first witness is the prosecutor, who, on his word of honour, states that he loves Phyllis Vaughan, has loved her for a long time, and, if it be possible, loves her more every day. I was by her chair now, and 'bending over her. "Phyllis, don't drive me to despair." "Is this a very serious case?" she asked. "It is."</p>
        <p>"Well—suppose we compromise it?" I assented. Even Phyllis's' elusiveness appears to, have its limits. (To be continued.) (These articles originally appeared in the " London Echo.")</p>
        <p>~.4':,,ct,e..:7v...rii.--,,=.,'-,:;-'1. ,-!,,...:L.,,,,,,;:....,..:,. 1 ~4_,„:.„....t.1.2,3,,. ' -':.;,..,,,..Z.,'.11i?';'. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>MR. LLOYD-GEORGE, M.P.</head>
        <p>MR. LLOYD-GEORGE, M.P.</p>
        <p>THE CHIEFTAIN OF WALES</p>
        <p>Visits Warrington Next Friday.</p>
        <p>CHARACTER SKETCH.;</p>
        <p>The projected visit of Dlr. D. Lloyd-George, M.P. to Warrington next Friday is arousing great interest locally. He is billed to address a mass meeting of Liberals, and there can be no doubt that an enthusi- astic t eception awaits him on the occasion of his first visit to the town of tan and wire. The fact that he is being oaaosed in his candidature for Carnarvon I Boroughs by a Warrington man, Mr. R. A. Naylor, adds a little spice to the attractiveness of his visit. Mr. Lloyd-George does not reckon much of his opponent, but he will doubtless derive some little satisfaction from taking the war into the enemy's camp. In the course of a very readable illustrated character sketch published in the " Review of Reviews" the story of Mr. Lloyd-George's life is told. We append the following extracts from the article : THE CREATION OF THE CHURCH. That Lloyd-George is the idolised leader of the Welsh people is due larrely to the folly, or worse, of an Anglican clergyman, which gave him his first great lift into political notoriety. If Church people writhe in Wales this Recess over the ascendency which the member for Carnarvon has gained over "Gallant little Wales," they have only themselves to thank. He is their creation. If his Nonconformist knife is sharp and his thrust remorseless, they are responsible both for the keenness of its edge and the ruthlessness of its owner. For they taught him in his early teens and in his early manhood how power is abused in the hands of an ascendant Church. The iron entered into his soul while he was a boy at school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>SEM' TO A CHURCFI SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>SEM' TO A CHURCFI SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Like most of those—from M. Combes downwards— who have arisen to be the scourge of intolerant Clericalism, Lloyd-George was educated at a Church school. He went there, as do most Welsh children who attend Church schools, because there was no other school in the place. He was Nonconformist born and bred, but they made him learn the Church Catechism. To this the lad did not object. On the contrary, he applied himself so diligently to its study that he was I able to enjoy the grim satisfaction of beating all the Church lads in the school when examined in their own Catechism. The teacher was a sensible man, but the clerically-minded managers were after their kind. It is the fatal weakness of clericalism that it can never be content with the substance of authority, it must insist noon what Stalky would call" doing a gloat" Lloyd- George, who took the Catechism without a murmur, might have become an Anglican if the Church managers would have dispensed with their gloat. But it is not in clerical human nature to exercise such self-suppression.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>HIS FIRST REVOLT.</head>
        <p>HIS FIRST REVOLT.</p>
        <p>When examination came round, the managers, or some of them, asked the lads questions obnoxious to the religion of the chapel folk,whose sons they were. Lloy- d Welsh blood took fire and, boy though he was, he raised the standard of revolt. He organised a boy- cott of the examinations. It was his first rebellion. He appealed for the first time to the spirit of implicit obedieuce;and the requisite passion for self-sacrifice, which he relies upon to-day. He did not appeal in vain. Not a boy would answer a question at the examinations. In vain the managers bullied and coaxed and insisted. The Young boycotters stood dumb. They stood by their leader, and would not say a word. They carried their point. No such insulting questions were put to the boys again. Lloyd-George himself was deprived of the prize for good conduct which he had otherwise earned. But of that he reeked little. He had tasted the joy of battle and the sweets of victory. ,11n4 THE STORY OP LLANFROTFIEN AND ITs VICIAR. This however, was not the illustration I was about to give as to the way in which Anglican intolerance helped the Welsh chieftain to the saddle wherein he now sits at ease—" the man on horseback "of the situa- tion. It is the first step which counts,and for it Lloyd- George had to thank a clergyman in a country parish in Wales. Some twenty or thirty years ago the Welsh country folk living in the parish of Llanfrothen raised a public subscription for building a wall round a piece of land which was used as the burying ground for the villao.e. The money was subscribed largely by Noncon- formists. The wall was built, and after some years the clergyman of the parish had it conveyed to the Church —a piece of smart practice very familiar to the people of the Principality who have built many sohools and laid out many cemeteries at their own expense,only to dis- cover that by some legal instrument all their interest in their institutions has been conveyed to the Church to which they do not belong. The only difference in the case of the Vicar of Llanfrothen was that with unusual negligence he had delayed several years in appropriating for the Church the proceeds of the sub- scriptions of his Nonconformist parishioners. Here, again, as in the case of Lloyd-George's schooling. the Church could have kept its ill-gotten gains if it had , been content to enjoy the plunder without adding insult to injury. Unfortunately for the establishment, some of its clergy, swollen with insolence and pride cannot content themselves with anything less than the infliction of pain and humiliation noon Dissenters whenever opportunity offers. His Reverence of Llan- frothen seems to have been one of these men, and that , fact launched Lloyd-George into the House o Commons. THE QUARRYMAN AND HIS DEAD DAUGHTER.</p>
        <p>The monopoly of the graveyard, so long defended .vith such passionate eagerness by the Anglican clergy, had been destroyed by the Burials Act passed at the initiative of Mr. Osborne Morgan. Under the new law, clergymen were compelled, on formal notice being given to allow Nonconformists to bury their dead in the village churchyard. This slight concession to justice and humanity was bitterly resented by the clergy, and he of Llanfrothen took a characteristic method of expressing his sentiments. An old qmarryman in the village with his expiring breath implored those who stood at his bedside to bury his body by the side of a daughter whom he had idolised during her life, and whose loss he had never ceased to lament. Nothing could be more natural than the old man's wish—nothing easier, it might be thought, than to comply with it. But the Vicar of Llanfrothen thought differently. He resented what he regarded as the outrage of being served with the legal notice under the Burials Act. and he took his revenge. He was unable to forbid burial in the graveyard, but he claimed the prerogative of deciding the precise spot where the poor old man should be buried. The dog of a Dissenter had wished to mingle his ashes with those of his daughter, therefore the pious cleric decreed that that was the one place in the churchyard where he should not be buried. Where, then; should his grave be dug? The vicar was at no loss for a reply. There was one desolate and sinister plot of ground sot aside by local custom for the burial of suicides. It was there where the vicar had the grave dug, and there the old quarryman was buried. Mother Church—Step- mother Church in Wales—had triumphed, and the Vicar of Llanfrothen gloated over the way in which he had paid the Dissenters out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>LLOYD-GEORGE TO THE RESCUE.</head>
        <p>LLOYD-GEORGE TO THE RESCUE.</p>
        <p>But like many a more exalted personage the vicar had reckoned without Mr. Lloyd-George. The outraged villagers, the quarryman's neighbours, were furious at what the vicar had done. Lloyd-George was then a rising country solicitor. The aggrieved villagers de- cided to consult him as to what they should do. He looked into the case, found that the graYayard had actually been purchased with the money of his clients, and that the wall which barred them out had been built out of their subscriptions. The vicar had had them con- veyed to the Church, but he had delayed so long in doing this, Lloyd-George believed he had failed to establish his right to regard the burying place as Church property. Lloyd-George, therefore, advised the villagers of Llan- frothen that they should assemble in force at the grave- yard, demand admittance in order that they might dig up the old quarryman from his dishonoured grave, and bury him beside his daughter. "But if the vicar should refuse to open the gates ?" "Then," said Lloyd• George, break down the wall, forceyour way into the church- yard, and bury the quarryman by his daughter." VINDICATMG LAW .BY FORCE.</p>
        <p>This.spiritE:ir iirci;ice commend-ea itself to his clients. They obeyed it to the letter. The vicar refused their demand. They broke their way through the wall,opened the daughter's grave,, and buried the father by his daughter's side. Of conrse, legal proceedings were taken, and equally of course legal justices imposed fines for trespass. Equally, of course, Lloyd-George appealed, and the case came before judge and jury at the County Court. The jury was a Welsh ,jury, and they returned a verdict of "Not guilty." The judge was one of Lord Halsbury's appointments, and he refused to pronounce judgment in accordance with the finding of the jury. Nothing daunted. Lloyd-George carried the case up to London. When the facts were laid before Lord Chief Justice Coleridge and Mr. justice Manisty. they at once pronounced in his favour. The Lord Chief Justice declared that he was scandalised by the conduct of the judge, and added that his arst duty would be to call the attention of the Lord Chancellor to his conduct in the case.</p>
        <p>SEWEL-LLOYD-G ED.IG E, M.P.</p>
        <p>Thus, after a prolonged litigation. in which the dominant sect and its agents on the Bench had exhausted every resource, not even stopping at illegality on the judgment seat, Lloyd-George emerged triumphant. The churlish priest had peen abased in the dust. The common people had been vindicated against the clergy. Their apparent lawlessness had been declared by the supreme tribunal to have been the legitimate assertion of their legal rights. Lloyd-George was the man who had first drawn up the plan of campaign, and had afterwards fought the legal battle through to its triumphant close. Small -wonder was it that after this Lloyd-George became the hero of the Prin- cipality, and at the first by-election he was returned</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <p>p -</p>
        <p>at the head of the poll for the Carnarvon Boroughs. The electors of Carnarvon sent him to the House of Commons, but his Grand Elector was the Vicar of Llanf rothen.</p>
        <p>HIS UPBRINGING.</p>
        <p>Lloyd-George was the son of a Unitarian school- master, who was master of Hope-street school in Liver- pool, of which Dr. Martineau was the manager. Of Unitarianism, however, Lloyd-George heard little, for his fatherdied in 1865, when Lloyd was only two years old. His mother, the daughter of a Welsh Baptist minister, took her children home to Wales, where they were brought up under the eye and with the financial assistance of her brother, who kept a shoemaker's shop in the village of Llanystumdwy, in South Carnavon.</p>
        <p>A DILIGENT STUDENT. Life was a serious matter to young George, and he was a diligent student. Being seized with an ambition to learn French, his uncle, the Baptist shoemaker, set himself to learn the language with the aid of dictionary and grammar in order that he might help the boy to read French. Like many Welshmen, the uncle had a passion for education, and being unmarried, he devoted all his time and his means to bring up and educate his nephews and nieces. He passed the preliminary examination necessary before entering the legal profession when he was four- teen. At sixteen he left school and spent a year or two in reading at home, and he was then articled as clerk to a solicitor in Portmadoc. There he remained until he was twenty-one. When he had passed his final examina- tion and become a full-fledged lawyer, his education and training had cost his uncle some £7OO or £BOO. In due course he began to practise as a solicitor in Portmadoc. and it was there where he had the good fortune to be the legal champion of the rioters for law and humanity at Llanfrothen. His brother had entered I into partnership with him, and this left Lloyd-George free to accept the invitation to stand in the Liberal interest for the Carnarvon Boroughs. His opponent was the squire of Llanystumdwy, the village in which Lloyd-George had been brought up. There was a still contest, but in April, 1890, Lloyd-George entered the House of Commons, winning the seat from the Tories by a majority of only eighteen. For a year or two he made little stir in the House. In those days, when Disestablishment was very much to the fore in Wales, Lloyd-George achieved great local requte by his campaign against the Bishop of St. Asaph whose Church Defence speeches afforded his young and agile opponent ample material for criticism and reply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>AN OLD STORY RETOLD.</head>
        <p>AN OLD STORY RETOLD.</p>
        <p>At one of Mr. Lloyd George's reply meetings in Flintshire the chairman—a Welsh deacon, with strong convictions but no sense of humour—introduced Mr. Lloyd-George thus : Gentlemen,—l haft to introduce to you to-night the member for the Carnarvon Boroughs. He hass come here to reply to what the Bishop of St. Asaph said the other night about Welsh Disestablishment. In my opinion, gentlemen, that Bishop of St. Asaph iss one of off the biggest liars in creashon ; but, thank God—yes, thank God—we hail a match for him to-night. The story is told with great gnsto by Mr. Lloyd. George himself, whose sense of humour is too keen to enable him to suppress a good story even at his Own expense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>IN PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IN PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>His first speech in the House was a protest against the appointment of Welsh County Court Judges, who could not speak Welsh; but he first distinguished him- self in the House by his persistent obstruction of the Clergy Discipline Billin 1892.</p>
        <p>HIS FIRST scoaE. This measure, introduced by Lord Salisbury's Govern- ment, was supported by Mr. Gladstone; but it was most pertinaciously opposed by Lloyd-George and a handful of Nonconformists. Mr. Gladstone smashed and pulverised his insubordinate follower, but, apparently not one penny the worse, he resumed his campaign. A contemporary critic who strongly con- demned the opposition to the Bill was compelled to pay a reluctant tribute of admiration to the skill with which it was conducted.</p>
        <p>SUSPENDED.</p>
        <p>The introduction of the Agricultural Doles Bill roused him to vehement activity. He fought the Bill clause by clause, and at last he achieved his first distinguished exploit by getting himself named from the chair and suspended for the rest of the session. This distinction endeared him still more to his constituents, and first signalled him out to the Radicals outside Wales as " lad of parts." It was not, however, till 1899 that Lloyd-George became as famous throughout the United Kingdom as he had long been in Wales. He offered an uncompro- mising opposition to the war in all its stages, and soon attracted to himself the honour of being the most intensely hatred pro-Boer in Parliament. Nothing daunted, he carried the war into the enemy's camp, and by his exposure of the connection between the Chamberlain family and Kynoch's firm for the manufac- ture of explosives,he struck a shrewd blow at the Jingo high priest. "The more the Empire expands, the more the Chamberlains contract," was a saying which crystallised into an epigram the general result of his foray into the Birmingham contracts.</p>
        <p>FELLED BY A JINGO BLUDGEON. Notwithstanding this, perhaps partly on account of it, he was made the victim of personal attack. An infuriated patriot meeting him in the streets of Bangor felled him to the ground by a blow on the head. The bludgeon was heavy and the pro-Boer was knocked senseless. It was a foul blow, but like everything else that is done to injure Lloyd-George, it redounded to his advantage. When Parliament was dissolved on the lying pretence that the war was over, a dead set was made against Lloyd-Geoage's seat by the Jingoes. Before the khaki cry Liberals went down all over the land. The Tories made sure that they would win back Carnarvon. And so perhaps they would have done but for the felon blow that stretched Lloyd-George flat in the street of his own town..</p>
        <p>When the poll was declared, and Mr. Lloyd-George was once again—for the fourth time—victor, the scene of popular enthusiasm beggared description. Mr. Harold Spender, writing in the "Manchester Guardian" on Octobec 11th, 1990, said:— Words fail me to describe the scenes of delirious enthusiasm which I have just witnessed in this little borough of Carnarvon. The welcome of a people to their triumphant champion, the expression of a great relief, the reaction from a great fear. Great joy some- times kills, and the delirium of the people became a positive peril to their hero who was almost sacrificed to the frenzied worship of his followers. Never do I remember such a scene of ecstasy. "Lloyd-George for ever!" "Lloyd-George for over !" " Lloyd-George for ever !"—that was the cry of these multitudes.</p>
        <p>lIIS VISIT TO -BIRMINGHAM. His fame as a pro-Boer culminated in the riot oi:ganised by the Chamberlain press, when he visited tim i Blrmingh just before Christmas, n 1901. He had previously been mobbed at Glasgow, where his carriage had been assaulted by Jingo rufflians, who maltreated everyone inside excepting himself. He knew that the Jingo myrmidons had been carefully organising for his murder. The Jingoes of the Midland capital, led on by their newspapers, had fully determined that Lloyd- George should never leave Birmingham alive. He took his precautions. He went down by an earlier train. got out at a wayside station, and was safe in the Town Hell an hour and a half before the meeting opened. The Chamberlainites were presEnt in force, and by persistent clamour prevented one word being heard. Outside the mob assailed the police, smashed the windows, and tried to sack the building. Lloyd-Georze dictated his speech to two reporters la an ante-room. For hours the storm 'of violence raged. The police frankly admitteil their inability to save his life if he ventured outside the building. At last the chief constable had the happy thought of disguising the doughty pro-Boer in a policeman's uniform. By this means he was smuggled through the crowd, and reached home safely. A LEADER IN THE HOUSE. We come now to the latest stages of Lloyd-George's public career, that in which he won from the Unionist Attorney-General the admission that he was the ablest critic of the Education Act; and from the Prime Minister (on the third reading of the Education Bill, December 3rd, 19920 this significant declaration : "There is the hon. member for the Carnarvon boroughs, who through these debates has played,in my judgment, a most distinguished part. I believe that In the opinion of both sides of the House, and of the country, that the hon. gentleman has shown himself to be an eminent Parliamentarian."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>TOE TMMIt OF T NONCONFORMISTS..</head>
        <p>TOE TMMIt OF T NONCONFORMISTS..</p>
        <p>When the Bishops' Bill was carried on its second reading by a majority of 237, it feli to Lloyd-George's lot to state the Nonconformist 'case. Of his speeches Mr. Massingham, the ablest chronicler of our Parlia- mentary debates, wrote in the" Daily News" of May 9th, 1902 : Until Mr. Lloyd-George spoke to-night (May Bth) Nonconformity, its intellectual attitude to education, its historical associations with the settle- ment of 1870, now being torn up, and its contri- bution to the religious problem, has gone without a recorder and a champion. Mr. Lloyd-George took the vacant place to-night. His speech was not only the most powerful he has ever delivered, but it had the high interest of being a complete and sincere self-revelation. Here was the Nonconformist attitude in its strength and its weakness. Here was an authentic voice, worthy in its way of the traditions associated with the great names of Bright and Spurgeon. A comparison with Bright's method and style—of course, with real differences—often rose to my mind as I listened to Mr. Lloyd-George —for here was Rright's simplicity of thought, directness of expression, and vivid, instinctive fighting quality. The fighting part of the speech was, indeed, splendid. It was full of quick flashing retort rapid cut and thrust dealings with the lead- mg figures in the Church party. The speech greatly irritated and excited this section. Member after member rose to contradict or qualify it. In each case the rejoinder was perfect, and the interrupter crushed. That speech probably represented the high-water mark of Lloyd-George's Parliamentary achievements as Nonconformist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>HIS ATTACK _ON MR. CHAMBERLAIN.</head>
        <p>HIS ATTACK _ON MR. CHAMBERLAIN.</p>
        <p>As a speaker he broke his own high record by the scathing attack which he made upon Ar. Chamberlain, May 22nd,1903,f0r his betrayal of the cause of the British</p>
        <p>, .</p>
        <p>poor. Lloyd-George is almost the only Liberal mem- ber who has never shown the slightest fear of Mr.. Chamberlain, and Mr. Chamberlain has always shown a significant dread of his indomitable assailant from. , Wales. Mr. Chamberlain is a very formidable opponent from whom to run away. He is not by any means a first-class fighting man when he is resolutely opposed. The fact that Lloyd-George was the only man who, had pluck enough to fight Mr. Chamberlain was in itself sufficient distinction for any man. For years Mr. Chamberlain had bull-dozed the Liberal Opposition. He bluffed them into surly silence. Lloyd-George. broke the spell. He bearded the Bramangem lion in his den, and proved to all the world that Joseph was only victorious in debate until anyone summed up sufficient pluck to stand up to him. Lloyd-George stood up to him, and Mr. Chamberlain's fatal ascend- ancy was shattered. He will always be a vigorous debater, but there is no longer the samo awe which he formerly excited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-01-28_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>THI FIGRT AGAINST THE EDUCATION ACT.</head>
        <p>THI FIGRT AGAINST THE EDUCATION ACT.</p>
        <p>When the Education Act r-eceiveithe4-12.-o-Yal-A-ssent, great responsibility was thrown upon Wales, and there- fore upon the Welsh leader. For Wales was the only district in the kingdom where the Nonconformists had a clear majority in all the County Councils, to whom was entrusted the admininistration of the Act. Wales, therefore, had to lead the van. What was to be done ? Were they to refuse to administer the law, or were they to carry it out submissively ? Both these alternatives were distasteful to the Welsh people. .11r. Lloyd-George hit upon a middle way. In his own picturesque phrase he propoundad a plan of campaign, the central principle of which was to carry the enemy's artillery and use it against his own position. His programme was at once adopted by all the Welsh counties except by Carmar- then, which has. subsequently come into line. Lloyd- George thus explained the policy adopted by Wales after the passing of the Act. Passive resistance was impossible in Wales, where the task of putting the law in motion would have fallen upon magistrates and local authorities who would them- selves have been passive resisters. Therefore the course was adopted of maintaining as far as possible the states quo before the Education Act of 1902 by continuing to distribute among the Church schools all the Parlia- mentary grants earned by them and by providing for the deficit out of the new grant-in-aid. . . . .</p>
        <p>Up to the present time i'he Welsh have been scru- pulous to obey the letter of the law. Their discipline is so perfect that out of thirty local education autho- rities in Wales only two—Carmarthen town and Wrex- hara—are not pledged to the policy of resignation if the Government should attempt to coerce them into levying rates for denominational schools. Again, to , quote the " Times,"" with exceptions so few as to be practically negligible, Mr. Lloyd-George is in a position to make the revolt of the Welsh Councils effective—given only the two conditions—namely, implicit obedience, and the requisite passion for self- sacrifice on the part of the individual councils." The two conditions will not be lacking. This being so,the Government is check Mated, and Lloyd-George is victor. THE MAN AS SEEN. At the close of the Session of 1901, a writer signing himself "M." contributed to the "Daily Mail " a very brilliant, although not very sympathetic, sketch of Lloyd-George as a twentieth century min. Considering the quarter in which it 11 )3 tred, the following extract from that article is wortn reprinting to-day "In the late Session Mr. Lloyd-George has flashed from the outer obscurity of local importance into the very zenith of the political heavens with the suddenness of an unlooked-for comet. And in reckoning the future of a public man the aptitude for attracting attention that Mr. Lloyd-Geege has shown he possesses so pre- eminently. There s a something that emanates from him that is as effective in catching the eye of the public as a red rag is in drawing the attention of a bull.'</p>
        <p>HIS OUTWARD SEMBLANCE. Yet there is nothing remarkable about the outer man to reveal this special power. A short, somewhat commonplace-looking individual, he would by most be described as of insignificant appearance. With his fresh coloured complexion, his slightly unruly dark brown hair combed up from the forehead, his small dark moustache, his sharp features, and his quick eyes, he- suggests nothing more important than a very ordinary member of the middle-classes ; he might, with his straight unremarkable frock-coat and dark uncreased trousers, be doing well in a small way of business, or else be taken for a valuable assistant in a large concern. Certainly there is nothing about his exterior that would have led one to prephesy that he would so soon makc. his mark in the councils of the nation. His manner too—the jaunty self-assurance, thoroughly in keeping with his appearance—is not one that in itself would assure him success in all circles.</p>
        <p>Yet even in private intercourse one's first impression of the man immediately gives way to a feeling that there is something about him out of the common. He speaks with a decision and a certainty that carry con- viction. You feel as you talk to him that he has some- thing worth listening to—that he is not, after all, of the common ruck.</p>
        <p>YOUNG JOE RA:DIVIVLTS.</p>
        <p>This faculty of impressing on the crowd will carry Mr. Lloyd-George far, just as it has carried Mr. Chamberlain far. There is, indeed, a very remarkable similarity between the Lloyd-George of to-day and the "Joe " Chamberlain of twenty years ago. Like his prototype of two decades ago, Mr. Lloyd-. George began his Parliamentary career careless of personal appearance, and with side-whiskers. With the advent of fame—or notoriety—the side-whiskers vanished, the hair was cut and groomed, and a band-box smartness replaced the careless neglige. Look at him in debate, leaning forward, eager, keen,. I alert, hand to ear, ready to spring on his prey and rend him to pieces—t..e reflection of what his great adver- sary once was. His very manner of rising is reminis- cent—the sudden leap to his feet, the momentary pause. to shake out his coat-tails and stretch his shirt-cuffs ; it was done just so almost in that very place years ago. The moment he opens his mouth to speak the• similarity is so striking as to make the listener start involuntarily. Listen ! The same clear, low-pitched cruel voice ; the same keen, incisive phrases ; the same mordant bitterness ; the same caustic sneer'; the same sardonic humour ; the same personal enmity. It is the very reincarnation of the present Colonial Secretary in his younger clays—a spectre of his dead self arisen to</p>
        <p>haunt him.</p>
        <p>Will hen score of years hence be the tower of strength of the Imperial or the Parochial party ? None can now say, but that he will by then be one of the foremost men, in the nation's Parliament is bayond question. His faults are the faults of ineepc,rience, of strength untried and untrained ; but strength, force of character, indi- viduality are there without a doubt. Ms views may not now be acceptable to the people of this country, but here time is on his side. He is of the type that moulds public opinion to his own."</p>
        <p>• Dispute About Hurdles. Before his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C. at the County Court on Thursday, Samuel Pearson, com- mercial traveller, Stockton Heath, brought an action against Mr. Peers Egerton Warburton, Arley Hall, for the return of sixteen hurdles,and damages for trespass and fie alleged wrongful detention of the hurdles.— Mr. Browne appeared for the plaintiff and the defend- ant was represented by Mr. George Egerton Warburton, his agent. Plaintiff's else, as stated by Mr. Browne, was that in order to build a house, MN Pearson purchased land from Mr. C. W. Davenport. The hurdles and fence which stood on the plot were, it was contended, included in thei' purchase. Plaintiff was prepared to swear that the, hurdles were given to him by Mr. Davenport. They, I'did not belong to Mr. E7,erton Warburton but, if to, . anyone, the Rural District Council. After some correspondence, workmen in Mr. Warburton's employ removed the sixteen hurdles together with five which.' had been given to the plaintiff and Litir which had been bought from a Warrington firm. During the removal the land was damaged and a young hedge- was spoiled. Mr. G. Egerton Warburton ultimately wrote apologising for the removal of the nine hurdles which he admitted were not his property and returned them. He had, however, retained possession of the other sixteen—Mr. Warburton contended that the hurdles did not belong to Mr. Davenport, but were Mr. Peers Ezerton Warburton's property.—His Honour held that plaintiff was entitled to damages in respect of the nino hurdles to which Mr. Warburton had no legitimate claitn.7-Mr. Browne agreed to abandon that part of the claim relating to the sixteen• hurdles, and his _Honour gave judgment fm. ;£1 damages and costs in respect of the others. •</p>
        <p>ONE OF IRE PERILS OF TUE MINE. The fifth fatality at Leigh within a week occurred on Thursday morning. Leonard Marshall, aged 17, of Twist-lane, Leigh, was pushing a full tub of coal in the Plank-lane Collieries seven-feet mine, when the roof suddenly broke up and the heavy mass fell upon him killing him instantaneously. Another drawer named Thomas Counsel had a narrow escaps. It took thirty minutes to extricate the deceased.</p>
        <p>Deaths. MlLLENG—Tanuary 23rd at his resid4ce, Summerville Latchford, Henry Milling in his 6Sth year. No tiowers.by special request. SunLow—On the 22nd inst. at The Limes, Fearnhead Warrington, Sarah wife of Thomas Sudlow, aged 62 years.</p>
