        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January, 28 1905• SCIENCE NOTES AND tALL RIGHTS RESERTED.3 GLEANINGS, FROM STRENUOUS YOUTH M. GUSTAVE LE Row, who is an investigator (A TO GRACEFUL AGE, radio-activity, expresses the opinion that what are called "electrons" are something intermediate EV between matter and ether. THE REV. E. J. HARDY, M.A. ACCORDING to a paper read at the St. Louis (Author of" How to-be Happy though Married," Exhibition, storm waves agitate the sea to greater 4.e.) depths than has been supposed. Apparently they ; are felt at from 100 ft. to 150 ft. below the surface. \ Some time ago it was the fashion to snub youth, and make it take a very back seat indeed. M. RORDIEB, of the Faculty of Medicine, Lyons. It was only admired in ladies ; men had to has confirmed Blondlot's photographic proofs of the apologise for it as if it were a crime. They existence of the N-rays by taking photographs of could not help being born young, and in.this his own. A steel file emitting the rays was used , way were like Boswell, who pleaded toleration to enhance the brightness of sulphide of calcium, from Dr. Johnson for his Scotch extraction on which was photographed. the ground that it was not his fault that he was born a Scotchman. Carlyle wrote that youths should be shut up !in barrels until they passed their twenty-fifth year, because that is the time when they attain . their " maximum of detestability." In the Sen- ate or council of the aged, young members were occasionally seen, but they were not heard un- less they could make a defence for the crime of being a young man like the one so well known to have been made by the younger Pitt. In the army promotion used to be by seniority/and old men who had lost all nerve and initiative com- manded regiments. Now, young men are com- ing to the front. I heard it said lately by an experiencend officer that a man had not nerve to properly lead a cavalry regiment after forty-five years of age. Upon the Episcopal bench at pre- sent there are those who would once have teen thought boy bishops. Most people of our day I and generation admire and envy youth. If to be young is a fault it is one which we would all like to commit. " What would you give for my .youth?" a young fop asked Talleyrand. The wrinkled old diplomatist smiled and replied, " My faith I would almost be willing to be as , foolish !" Those of us who are beginning tc I feel symptoms of that incurable disease—old age would make the same sacrifice. BOW TO THE INEVITABLE. But we need not elaborate a proof that the sunny days of youth are the best days, or that tile vigour and strenuousness of young men are most valuable, because no one denies these things. What some will think much harder.to prove is that there is a beauty and a happiness in old age if it be taken in the right way. When Boswell expressed to Dr. Johnson a wish to ex- perience old age. the Doctor was much irritated. and thundered out : " What, would you have decrepitude ?" Whether we wish it or not, if we do not die young, we shall have the experi- ence of old age, but it depends very much upon the way in which we spend the beginning and middle of our lives whether old age, is or is not, in our case accompanied by decrepitude. The poet Rogers, who was often complimented on being a hue old man, used acidly to reply : " There is no such thing, sir, as a flue old man.'' Well, but to say the least, there are some old people far less disagreeable than are others, and it is the duty of everyone to try and grow old gracefully. To grow oil is natural ; being inn-- nral it is beautiful in lif we grumble at it we miss the lesson it would teach. When people asked Leontinas, the teacher of Isocrates, who reached the age of one hundred and seven, why he remained so long in this life, he answered. " because I have nothing whereof I can accuse my old age." Sometimes old age, when it is free from great infirmities, trials and privations. is the most tranquil, and perhaps, son the whole the happiest 'period of life. The passions and ambitions of other days have Passed. Time has nllayed animosities, and subdued asperities of ellaractee". St. Martin's slimmer lights with a pale but beautiful gleam the brief November day. It is pleasant to feel in oneself and to no- :ice in others, the sourness of immature and I ',wipe youth disappearing with the advanee of Years. Just before he died, the American poet. Walt 'Whitman, said, "As I grew older I am niere and more ready to take the good there is in men and authors, without concerning myself about the bad." It is only poor wine that grows sour with age. White hairs, which have been called "the flowers of the cemetery," may not of themselves always inspire respect, but they cannot fail to do so when they point to a long life that has been well speut. THE CHINAMAN AND AGE. Here in China, where the writer is now liv- 7ag, old age is much venerated, and it is con- sidered a compliment to enquire after your " honourable teeth," the. conventional phrase employed in asking people their age. The Chinese admire a long beard, and estimate age by the length of it, because social usage only allows a man to grow old when he is a grand- father, or at least forty-five years old. ' What is your honourable age f'" a Chinaman asks when introduced to a new acquaintance. " My in- significant years do not number more than thirty," may be the • BrOdest reply. "Oh!" it is polite to exclaim in it tone of great surprise, "I should have taken you: to be forty at the very least." And the other, even if a woman, will blush; with pleasure at the insinuated flat- tery. TI4 respect which the Chinese pay to their parents, and to ord people generally is one of the things in which they differ with advant- age from ourselves. Chinese children have not to be reminded that a parent is, after all, one of God's creatures. The Chinese never speak of old fogies, or make parents feel that they have over-stayed their welcome in the world. In speaking to a parent a child is taught to avoid all reference to old age, and every boy has held up to him as an example the conduct of Leon Lai-tze. This worthy fearing that the recogni- tion by his parents of the fact that he was seventy years old would remind them of their own great age, used to dress himself in a child's frook and play about the room like an infant. At the end of a beautiful summer when the evenings begin to " close in " and there are signs of approaching winter we feel very sorry ; but when winter does actually come, we find that it is not without its good things, and we accept it cheeTfully. In the same way we should accept old age, and not kick against it. and then we shall find that it has blessings, and that it need not by any means be a winter of discontent.</p>
