        <p>balls in the positions given under diagram 56 the red ball, when the stroke is successful, will strike the top and side cushions more or less as shown by the intersected line, and thus it is easy_ to see that an in-off into one ct the top pockets (or either of them) or into the centre pocket—according to the strength of the stroke—may be left to continue with. Also the latitude that the D gives may easily mean a simple cannon being on as well. Of course, if the position of the cue ball before playing the run-through be slightly altered, the red ball will strike and come off the two cushions at somewhat different angles from the ones shown on the diagram, but generally speaking the stroke can always be played in such a way as to leave good position to continue with from the D. Of course this run-through in-off requires well handling, and is beyond the powers of the very moderate player, but it is little if at all more difficult than the run-through from the red on the spot, which is so often played when the cue ball happens to be at X on the diagram, or in some similar position. (To be Continued.) Vol. I. of "Billiards: The Strokes of the Game." 100 illustrations. By RISO LEVI. Is now ready, Is. net. Mr. Riso Levi will be pleased to answer in these columns all questions relative to the article appearing weekly, and as far as possible any questions on other points in connection with the game of Billiards. Envelopes should be marked "Billiards."</p>
