        <p>urveyor reported that in the Frodsham district the whole of the bridges were well calculated to stand the , heavy traffic. The committee recommended that the Surveyors be empowered, if they saw motor cars in the district which they considered were carrying above the registered Amount,to have the cars weighed at a machine if there was one within half a mile. The recommendations were agreed to. FINANCIAL. Mr. Wright reported that the expenses for the fort- night included £403 for carting, £4ll for stones,and £67 for chippings. The general statement sh owed a credit balance of £229 Gs. sd. Mr. Stokes suggested that by attaching a waggon to their steam roller the Council might save much of the expense of carting. The Clerk reported that he had received the bill of costs from Parliamentary agents incurred in the opposi- tion to the Manchester Ship Canal Bill last session. His own bill of costs was also ready and the two totalled .fil3o. UNSATISFACTORY ROAD AT MOORS. Mr. Linaker asked who was responsible for the road in Mooreleadingtiom Moss-lane along the Shipeanal to the canal bridge. Mr. Swinton : We are responsible for it. Mr. Linaker said he wished to call attention to its very unsatisfactory condition. The road was well made by the Ship Canal Company, but now all the channels were overgrown with weeds, and it was very rutty. If the road did not receive attention there would be serious ' consequences. Mr. Swinton said he would attend to the road. Hakim' or THE DISTRICT. Dr. Adams reported one case of scarlet fever at Stockton Heath and said he was of opinion that the outbreak in that village was practically at an end. Two cases of the disease had been notified from Grappenhall Measles had become epidemic at Budworth and the schools had been closed for three weeks. There had been a considerable outbreak of scarlet fever at Weston Point, fourteen cases being notified. The day and Sunday schools had been closed for three weeks. 'Three cases of scarlet fever had occurred at Helsby. An epidemic of influenza in that locality was seriously interfering with the school attendance, but at present he saw no reason to recommend the closing of schools. Only one patient remained in the small-pox hospital and he expected the man would be discharged before the next Council meeting. Some discussion took place on a letter received by Dr. Adams respecting the washing of the clothing of patients at the Liverpool sanatorium at Delamere. The writer thought that as the washing was done at Five Ca•osses it might possibly be the means of spreading infection. Mr. Stokes said the matter had come before the County Council Health Committee and Dr. Vascher had been authorised to make inquiries. It was sug- gested that the clothing be disinfected. The Chairman thought they might safely leave the matter with the County Council.</p>
